<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<usx version="3.1">
<book code="LUK" style="id">BLB-Draft</book>

<para style="h">Luke</para>

<para style="toc1">Luke</para>

<para style="rem">sourceUrl=https://literalbible.com/blb.docx fetchedAtUtc=2026-09-01T15:43:00Z status=draft id=BLB-Draft spec=USFM-USX 3.1.1 char:add=docx-italic note:f=docx-footnotes note:x=docx-heading-crossrefs</para>

<chapter number="1" style="c" sid="LUK 1"/>

<para style="s">Dedication to Theophilus</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 1:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Acts 1:1–3</char></note>Inasmuch as many took in hand to draw up a narration concerning the things having been fully carried out among us, <verse eid="LUK 1:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 1:2"/>just as they delivered <char style="add">them</char> to us—those from the beginning having been eyewitnesses and servants of the word— <verse eid="LUK 1:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 1:3"/>it seemed good also to me, having followed closely from the first all things carefully, to write in consecutive order to you, most excellent Theophilus, <verse eid="LUK 1:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 1:4"/>so that you may know the certainty of the things that you were instructed. <verse eid="LUK 1:4"/></para>

<para style="s">Gabriel Foretells John’s Birth</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 1:5"/>There was in the days of Herod king of Judea a certain priest, by name Zechariah, of the division of Abijah, and his wife of the daughters of Aaron, and her name Elizabeth. <verse eid="LUK 1:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 1:6"/>And they were both righteous before God, going on in all the commandments and righteous requirements of the Lord, blameless. <verse eid="LUK 1:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 1:7"/>And unto them was no child, as Elizabeth was barren and both were advanced in their days. <verse eid="LUK 1:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 1:8"/>Now it came to pass in his serving as priest in the order of his division before God, <verse eid="LUK 1:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 1:9"/>according to the custom of the priesthood, <char style="add">that</char> it fell to him by lot to burn incense, having entered into the temple of the Lord. <verse eid="LUK 1:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 1:10"/>And all the multitude of the people was praying outside at the hour of the incense. <verse eid="LUK 1:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 1:11"/>And an angel of the Lord appeared to him, standing at the right side of the altar of the incense. <verse eid="LUK 1:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 1:12"/>And Zechariah was troubled having seen, and fear fell upon him. <verse eid="LUK 1:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 1:13"/>But the angel said to him, “Do not be afraid, Zechariah, because your petition was heard, and your wife Elizabeth will bear to you a son, and you will call his name John. <verse eid="LUK 1:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 1:14"/>And he will be joy and exultation to you, and many will rejoice at his birth, <verse eid="LUK 1:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 1:15"/>for he will be great before the Lord. And he shall never drink wine and strong drink, and he will be full of the Holy Spirit even from the womb of his mother. <verse eid="LUK 1:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 1:16"/>And many of the sons of Israel he will turn to the Lord their God. <verse eid="LUK 1:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 1:17"/>And he will go on before Him in the spirit and power of Elijah, to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children, and the disobedient to the wisdom of the righteous—to make ready a people prepared for the Lord.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Malachi 4:5–6</char></note><verse eid="LUK 1:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 1:18"/>And Zechariah said to the angel, “By what will I know this? For I am an old man, and my wife is advanced in her days.” <verse eid="LUK 1:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 1:19"/>And answering, the angel said to him, “I am Gabriel, the one standing before God, and I was sent to speak to you and to bring these good tidings to you. <verse eid="LUK 1:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 1:20"/>And behold, you will be silent and not able to speak until that day these things shall take place, because you did not believe my words, which will be fulfilled in their season.” <verse eid="LUK 1:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 1:21"/>And the people were expecting Zechariah, and they were marveling at his delaying in the temple. <verse eid="LUK 1:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 1:22"/>But having come out, he was not able to speak to them, and they recognized that he had seen a vision in the temple. And he was making signs to them and remained mute. <verse eid="LUK 1:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 1:23"/>And it came to pass, when the days of his service were fulfilled, <char style="add">that</char> he went away to his house. <verse eid="LUK 1:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 1:24"/>And after these days, his wife Elizabeth conceived, and she hid herself five months, saying, <verse eid="LUK 1:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 1:25"/>“Thus the Lord has done to me, in the days in which He looked upon <char style="add">me</char>, to take away my reproach among men.” <verse eid="LUK 1:25"/></para>

<para style="s">Gabriel Foretells Jesus’ Birth</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 1:26"/>Now in the sixth month the angel Gabriel was sent by God to a city of Galilee whose name <char style="add">was</char> Nazareth, <verse eid="LUK 1:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 1:27"/>to a virgin betrothed to a man whose name <char style="add">was</char> Joseph, of the house of David. And the name of the virgin <char style="add">was</char> Mary. <verse eid="LUK 1:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 1:28"/>And having come in to her, he said, “Greetings,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Rejoice</char></note> O favored one! The Lord <char style="add">is</char> with you.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Blessed are you among women!</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 1:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 1:29"/>And<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">when she saw this</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> she was troubled at the word and was reasoning what kind this greeting might be. <verse eid="LUK 1:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 1:30"/>And the angel said to her, “Do not be afraid, Mary, for you have found favor with God. <verse eid="LUK 1:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 1:31"/>And behold, you will conceive in womb and will give birth to a son, and you will call His name Jesus. <verse eid="LUK 1:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 1:32"/>He will be great, and He will be called Son of the Most High. And the Lord God will give Him the throne of His father David, <verse eid="LUK 1:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 1:33"/>and He will reign over the house of Jacob to the ages. And of His kingdom there will be no end!” <verse eid="LUK 1:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 1:34"/>And Mary said to the angel, “How will this be, since I do not know a man?” <verse eid="LUK 1:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 1:35"/>And answering, the angel said to her, “The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you; therefore also the Holy One being born<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Scrivener TR includes </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of you</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> will be called Son of God. <verse eid="LUK 1:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 1:36"/>And behold, Elizabeth your relative, she also has conceived a son in her old age, and this is the sixth month to her who <char style="add">was</char> called barren. <verse eid="LUK 1:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 1:37"/>For not any word will be impossible with God.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.37 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For no word from God will ever fail.</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 1:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 1:38"/>And Mary said, “Behold, the Lord’s maidservant. May it happen to me according to your word.” And the angel went away from her. <verse eid="LUK 1:38"/></para>

<para style="s">Mary Visits Elizabeth</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 1:39"/>Now Mary, having risen up in these days, went into the hill country with haste, to a city of Judah, <verse eid="LUK 1:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 1:40"/>and she entered into the house of Zechariah and greeted Elizabeth. <verse eid="LUK 1:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 1:41"/>And it came to pass, as Elizabeth heard the greeting of Mary, <char style="add">that</char> the baby in her womb leaped, and Elizabeth was filled with the Holy Spirit. <verse eid="LUK 1:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 1:42"/>And she cried out in a loud voice and said, “Blessed are you among women, and blessed is the fruit of your womb! <verse eid="LUK 1:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 1:43"/>And from where <char style="add">is</char> this to me, that the mother of my Lord should come to me? <verse eid="LUK 1:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 1:44"/>For behold, as the voice of your greeting came into my ears, the baby in my womb leaped in exultation. <verse eid="LUK 1:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 1:45"/>And blessed <char style="add">is</char> the one having believed that there will be a completion to the things spoken to her from the Lord.” <verse eid="LUK 1:45"/></para>

<para style="s">Mary’s Song1 Samuel 2:1–11</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 1:46"/>And Mary said: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 1:46">“My soul magnifies the Lord, <verse eid="LUK 1:46"/></para>

<para style="q2"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 1:47"/>and my spirit exulted in God my Savior! <verse eid="LUK 1:47"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 1:48"/>For He looked upon the humiliation of His maidservant. </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:48">For behold, from now on all the generations will call me blessed. <verse eid="LUK 1:48"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 1:49"/>For the Mighty One did great things to me, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:49">and holy <char style="add">is</char> His name. <verse eid="LUK 1:49"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 1:50"/>And His mercy <char style="add">is</char> to generations and generations, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:50">to those fearing Him. <verse eid="LUK 1:50"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 1:51"/>He showed strength with His arm; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:51">He scattered the proud </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:51">in the thought of their heart. <verse eid="LUK 1:51"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 1:52"/>He brought down rulers from thrones </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:52">and exalted the humble. <verse eid="LUK 1:52"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 1:53"/><char style="add">Those</char> hungering He filled with good things, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:53">and <char style="add">those</char> being rich He sent away empty. <verse eid="LUK 1:53"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 1:54"/>He took hold of Israel His servant, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:54">remembering mercy, <verse eid="LUK 1:54"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 1:55"/>as He spoke to our fathers, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:55">to Abraham and to his seed to the age.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.55 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 </char><char style="fq" closed="false">until eternity</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 1:55"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 1:56"/>And Mary remained with her about three months, and she returned to her house. <verse eid="LUK 1:56"/></para>

<para style="s">The Birth of John the Baptist</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="57" style="v" sid="LUK 1:57"/>Now to Elizabeth the time was fulfilled for her to give birth, and she bore a son. <verse eid="LUK 1:57"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="58" style="v" sid="LUK 1:58"/>And her neighbors and relatives heard that the Lord magnified His mercy with her, and they were rejoicing with her. <verse eid="LUK 1:58"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="59" style="v" sid="LUK 1:59"/>And it came to pass on the eighth day <char style="add">that</char> they came to circumcise the child, and they were calling him after the name of his father Zechariah. <verse eid="LUK 1:59"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="60" style="v" sid="LUK 1:60"/>And answering, his mother said, “No, but he will be called John.” <verse eid="LUK 1:60"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="61" style="v" sid="LUK 1:61"/>And they said to her, “There is no one among your relatives who is called by this name.” <verse eid="LUK 1:61"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="62" style="v" sid="LUK 1:62"/>And they were making signs to his father—what he might want him to be called. <verse eid="LUK 1:62"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="63" style="v" sid="LUK 1:63"/>And having asked for a writing tablet, he wrote, saying, “John is<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.63 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 </char><char style="fq" closed="false">shall be</char></note> his name.” And they all marveled. <verse eid="LUK 1:63"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="64" style="v" sid="LUK 1:64"/>And his mouth was opened immediately, and his tongue; and he was speaking, blessing God. <verse eid="LUK 1:64"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="65" style="v" sid="LUK 1:65"/>And fear came upon all those dwelling around them. And in all the hill country of Judea, all these words were being talked about. <verse eid="LUK 1:65"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="66" style="v" sid="LUK 1:66"/>And all those having heard laid <char style="add">them</char> in their heart, saying, “What then will this child be?” For<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.66 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And</char></note> also the hand of the Lord was with him. <verse eid="LUK 1:66"/></para>

<para style="s">Zechariah’s Song</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="67" style="v" sid="LUK 1:67"/>And Zechariah his father was filled with the Holy Spirit and prophesied, saying: <verse eid="LUK 1:67"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="68" style="v" sid="LUK 1:68"/>“Blessed <char style="add">be</char> the Lord, the God of Israel, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:68">because He visited and performed redemption for His people, <verse eid="LUK 1:68"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="69" style="v" sid="LUK 1:69"/>and He raised up a horn of salvation for us </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:69">in the house of His servant David, <verse eid="LUK 1:69"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="70" style="v" sid="LUK 1:70"/>as He spoke by the mouth of His holy prophets </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:70">from the age, <verse eid="LUK 1:70"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="71" style="v" sid="LUK 1:71"/>salvation from our enemies </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:71">and from the hand of all those hating us, <verse eid="LUK 1:71"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="72" style="v" sid="LUK 1:72"/>to show mercy toward our fathers </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:72">and to remember His holy covenant, <verse eid="LUK 1:72"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="73" style="v" sid="LUK 1:73"/>the oath that He swore to Abraham our father, to give us, <verse eid="LUK 1:73"/></para>

<para style="q2"><verse number="74" style="v" sid="LUK 1:74"/>having been saved from the hand of <char style="add">our</char> enemies, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:74">to serve Him without fear <verse eid="LUK 1:74"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="75" style="v" sid="LUK 1:75"/>in holiness and righteousness before Him </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:75">all our days.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.75 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">all the days of our lives</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 1:75"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="76" style="v" sid="LUK 1:76"/>And you, child, will be called </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:76">prophet of the Most High; </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 1:76">for you will go on before the Lord<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.76 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">before the presence of the Lord</char></note> </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:76">to prepare His ways, <verse eid="LUK 1:76"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="77" style="v" sid="LUK 1:77"/>to give knowledge of salvation to His people </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:77">in forgiveness of their sins, <verse eid="LUK 1:77"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="78" style="v" sid="LUK 1:78"/>through the affections of mercy of our God, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:78">in which the Sunrise<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.78 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Dawn</char></note> will visit<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.78 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">has visited</char></note> us from on high, <verse eid="LUK 1:78"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="79" style="v" sid="LUK 1:79"/>to shine upon those sitting in darkness </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:79">and in the shadow of death, </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 1:79">to direct our feet </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 1:79">into the way of peace.” <verse eid="LUK 1:79"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="80" style="v" sid="LUK 1:80"/>And the child continued to grow and to become strong in spirit;<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.80 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in the Spirit</char></note> and he was in the wilderness<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">1.80 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">desert</char></note> until the day of his appearance to Israel. <verse eid="LUK 1:80"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 1"/>

<chapter number="2" style="c" sid="LUK 2"/>

<para style="s">The Birth of Jesus</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 2:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 1:18–25</char></note>And it came to pass in those days <char style="add">that</char> a decree went out from Caesar Augustus to register all the world.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the whole empire</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 2:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 2:2"/>This registration first came to pass <char style="add">in</char> the governing<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">This registration came to pass before the governing</char></note> of Syria of Quirinius. <verse eid="LUK 2:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 2:3"/>And all were going to be registered, each to his own city. <verse eid="LUK 2:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 2:4"/>And Joseph also went up from Galilee, out of the city of Nazareth, to Judea, to the City of David, which is called Bethlehem, because of his being of the house and family of David, <verse eid="LUK 2:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 2:5"/>to register with Mary, the one betrothed to him,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">his betrothed wife</char></note> she being pregnant. <verse eid="LUK 2:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 2:6"/>And it came to pass, in their being there, <char style="add">that</char> the days of her giving birth were fulfilled. <verse eid="LUK 2:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 2:7"/>And she gave birth to her Son, the firstborn, and she wrapped Him in swaddling cloths and laid Him in a manger,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in the manger</char></note> because there was no place for them in the inn.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">guest room</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Greek </char><char style="fq" closed="false">katalymati</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 2:7"/></para>

<para style="s">The Shepherds and the Angels</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 2:8"/>And there were shepherds in the same region, lodging in the fields and keeping watch by night over their flock. <verse eid="LUK 2:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 2:9"/>And<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">behold</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> an angel of the Lord stood by them, and the glory of the Lord shone around them, and they feared <char style="add">with</char> great fear. <verse eid="LUK 2:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 2:10"/>And the angel said to them, “Do not be afraid, for behold, I bring good news to you⁺ <char style="add">of</char> great joy that will be to all the people. <verse eid="LUK 2:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 2:11"/>For today in the City of David a Savior was born to you⁺, who is Christ the Lord. <verse eid="LUK 2:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 2:12"/>And this <char style="add">is</char> the sign<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">a sign</char></note> to you⁺: You⁺ will find a baby wrapped in swaddling cloths and lying in a manger.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the manger</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 2:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 2:13"/>And suddenly there was with the angel a multitude of the heavenly host, praising God and saying: <verse eid="LUK 2:13"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 2:14"/>“Glory to God in the highest, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 2:14">and on earth peace among men with whom He is well pleased!<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">peace, good will among men</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 2:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 2:15"/>And it came to pass, as the angels went away from them into heaven, <char style="add">that</char> the shepherds were saying to one another, “Let us go through indeed as far as Bethlehem, and let us see this saying that has come to pass, which the Lord made known to us.” <verse eid="LUK 2:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 2:16"/>And having hurried, they came and found both Mary and Joseph, and the baby lying in the manger. <verse eid="LUK 2:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 2:17"/>And having seen, they made known concerning the saying having been spoken to them concerning this Child. <verse eid="LUK 2:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 2:18"/>And all those having heard marveled concerning the things having been spoken to them by the shepherds. <verse eid="LUK 2:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 2:19"/>But Mary was preserving all these matters, pondering in her heart. <verse eid="LUK 2:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 2:20"/>And the shepherds returned, glorifying and praising God for all things that they heard and saw, just as it was said to them. <verse eid="LUK 2:20"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Presented at the Temple</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 2:21"/>And when eight days to circumcise Him<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Child</char></note> were fulfilled, then His name was called Jesus, which He had been called by the angel before His being conceived in the womb. <verse eid="LUK 2:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 2:22"/>And when the days of their purification<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Scrivener TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">her purification</char></note> were fulfilled according to the Law of Moses, they brought Him up to Jerusalem to present to the Lord <verse eid="LUK 2:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 2:23"/>(just as it has been written in the Law of the Lord: “Every male opening a womb will be called holy to the Lord”), <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Exodus 13:2</char></note><verse eid="LUK 2:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 2:24"/>and to give a sacrifice according to that said in the Law of the Lord: “A pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Leviticus 12:8</char></note><verse eid="LUK 2:24"/></para>

<para style="s">The Prophecy of Simeon</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 2:25"/>And behold, there was a man in Jerusalem whose name <char style="add">was</char> Simeon. And this man <char style="add">was</char> righteous and devout, waiting for the consolation of Israel, and the Holy Spirit was upon him. <verse eid="LUK 2:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 2:26"/>And it was divinely revealed to him by the Holy Spirit <char style="add">that</char> he would not see death before he should see the Christ of the Lord. <verse eid="LUK 2:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 2:27"/>And he went in the Spirit into the temple.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the temple courts</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; also in verse 46</char></note> And in the parents bringing in the child Jesus <char style="add">for</char> them to do according to that having become customary by the Law concerning Him, <verse eid="LUK 2:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 2:28"/>then he took Him into the arms and blessed God, and he said: <verse eid="LUK 2:28"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 2:29"/>“Now, Lord, You dismiss Your servant in peace, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 2:29">according to Your word. <verse eid="LUK 2:29"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 2:30"/>For my eyes saw Your salvation, <verse eid="LUK 2:30"/></para>

<para style="q2"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 2:31"/>which You prepared before the face of all the peoples, <verse eid="LUK 2:31"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 2:32"/>a light to the uncovering of the Gentiles, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 2:32">and the glory of Your people Israel.” <verse eid="LUK 2:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 2:33"/>And His father and mother<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Joseph and His mother</char></note> were marveling at the things having been spoken concerning Him. <verse eid="LUK 2:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 2:34"/>And Simeon blessed them and said to His mother Mary: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 2:34">“Behold, this <char style="add">Child</char> is appointed </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 2:34">for the falling and rising up of many in Israel, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 2:34">and for a sign spoken against— <verse eid="LUK 2:34"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 2:35"/>and a sword will also<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">also</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> pass through your <char style="add">own</char> soul—</para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 2:35">so that the thoughts of many hearts may be revealed.” <verse eid="LUK 2:35"/></para>

<para style="s">The Prophecy of Anna</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 2:36"/>And there was Anna, a prophetess, daughter of Phanuel, of the tribe of Asher; she <char style="add">was</char> advanced in many days, having lived with a husband seven years from her virginity, <verse eid="LUK 2:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 2:37"/>and she <char style="add">was</char> a widow until eighty-four years,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.37 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">was a widow to the age of eighty-four</char></note> who did not depart the temple, serving with fastings and petitions night and day. <verse eid="LUK 2:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 2:38"/>And having come up at that hour, she was giving praise to God<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to the Lord</char></note> and was speaking concerning Him to all those waiting for the redemption of Jerusalem.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">redemption in Jerusalem</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 2:38"/></para>

<para style="s">The Return to NazarethMatthew 2:19–23</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 2:39"/>And when they accomplished everything according to the Law of the Lord, they returned to Galilee, to their city, Nazareth. <verse eid="LUK 2:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 2:40"/>And the Child continued to grow and become strong,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.40 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">became strong in spirit</char></note> being filled with wisdom. And the grace of God was upon Him. <verse eid="LUK 2:40"/></para>

<para style="s">The Boy Jesus at the Temple</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 2:41"/>And His parents were going every year to Jerusalem for the Feast of the Passover. <verse eid="LUK 2:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 2:42"/>And when He was twelve years <char style="add">old</char>, <char style="add">on</char> their having gone up<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.42 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to Jerusalem</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> according to the custom of the Feast <verse eid="LUK 2:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 2:43"/>and having completed the days, in their returning, the boy Jesus remained behind in Jerusalem, and His parents<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.43 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and Joseph and His mother</char></note> did not know. <verse eid="LUK 2:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 2:44"/>And having supposed Him to be in their company, they went a day’s journey and began seeking Him among the relatives and the acquaintances. <verse eid="LUK 2:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 2:45"/>And not having found <char style="add">Him</char>, they returned to Jerusalem, seeking Him. <verse eid="LUK 2:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 2:46"/>And it came to pass after three days <char style="add">that</char> they found Him in the temple, sitting in the midst of the teachers, both hearing them and questioning them. <verse eid="LUK 2:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 2:47"/>And all those hearing Him were amazed at His understanding and answers. <verse eid="LUK 2:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 2:48"/>And having seen Him, they were astonished, and His mother said to Him, “Child, why did You do thus to us? Behold, Your father and I, being distressed, were seeking You.” <verse eid="LUK 2:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 2:49"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Why is it that you⁺ were seeking Me? Did you⁺ not know that it is necessary for Me to be in the house of My Father?”</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">2.49 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in the things of My Father</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 2:49"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 2:50"/>And they did not understand the word that He spoke to them. <verse eid="LUK 2:50"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 2:51"/>And He went down with them and came to Nazareth, and He was subject to them. And His mother was keeping all these matters in her heart. <verse eid="LUK 2:51"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 2:52"/>And Jesus continued to advance in wisdom and stature, and in favor with God and men. <verse eid="LUK 2:52"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 2"/>

<chapter number="3" style="c" sid="LUK 3"/>

<para style="s">The Mission of John the Baptist</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 3:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 1:19–28</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 1:1–8</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 3:1–12</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Isaiah 40:1–5</char></note>And in the fifteenth year of the reign of Tiberius Caesar, Pontius Pilate being governor of Judea, and Herod being tetrarch of Galilee, and his brother Philip being tetrarch of the region of Ituraea and of Trachonitis, and Lysanias being tetrarch of Abilene, <verse eid="LUK 3:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 3:2"/>during the high priesthood of Annas and Caiaphas,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Annas and Caiaphas being the high priests</char></note> the word of God came upon John son of Zechariah in the wilderness.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">desert</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; also in verse 4</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 3:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 3:3"/>And he went into all the region around the Jordan, preaching a baptism of repentance unto forgiveness of sins, <verse eid="LUK 3:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 3:4"/>as it has been written in the book of the words of Isaiah the prophet: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 3:4">“A voice of one crying in the wilderness, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:4">‘Prepare the way of the Lord; </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:4">make straight His paths. <verse eid="LUK 3:4"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 3:5"/>Every valley will be filled, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:5">and every mountain and hill will be made low; </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 3:5">and the crooked will become straight, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:5">and the rough ways into smooth. <verse eid="LUK 3:5"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 3:6"/>And all flesh will see the salvation of God.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 40:3–5 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="LUK 3:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 3:7"/>Then he was saying to the crowds coming out to be baptized by him, “Offspring of vipers, who warned you⁺ to flee from the coming wrath? <verse eid="LUK 3:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 3:8"/>Therefore produce fruits worthy of repentance. And do not begin to say in yourselves, ‘We have Abraham <char style="add">as</char> father.’ For I say to you⁺ that out of these stones God is able to raise up children to Abraham. <verse eid="LUK 3:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 3:9"/>And already also the ax is laid to the root of the trees; therefore every tree not producing good fruit is cut down and is thrown into fire.” <verse eid="LUK 3:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 3:10"/>And the crowds were questioning him, saying, “What then shall we do?” <verse eid="LUK 3:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 3:11"/>And answering, he was saying to them, “The one having two tunics, let him impart to the one having none; and the one having food, let him do likewise.” <verse eid="LUK 3:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 3:12"/>And tax collectors also came to be baptized,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 </char><char style="fq" closed="false">baptized by him</char></note> and they said to him, “Teacher, what shall we do?” <verse eid="LUK 3:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 3:13"/>And he said to them, “Exact nothing more than that having been appointed to you⁺.” <verse eid="LUK 3:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 3:14"/>And those being soldiers were also questioning him, saying, “And what shall we do?” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 3:14">And he said to them, “Extort no one, nor accuse falsely, and be content with your⁺ wages.” <verse eid="LUK 3:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 3:15"/>And the people are expecting, and all are reasoning in their hearts concerning John, whether he might be the Christ. <verse eid="LUK 3:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 3:16"/>John answered, saying to all, “I indeed baptize you⁺ in water, but the One mightier than I comes, of whom I am not sufficient to loose the strap of His sandals.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">with water</char></note> He will baptize you⁺ in the Holy Spirit and fire,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Cited in Acts 13:25</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">with the Holy Spirit and with fire</char></note><verse eid="LUK 3:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 3:17"/>whose winnowing fork <char style="add">is</char> in His hand to thoroughly cleanse His<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">hand, and He will thoroughly cleanse</char></note> threshing floor and to gather the wheat into His barn; but He will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire.” <verse eid="LUK 3:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 3:18"/>Therefore indeed, exhorting many other things, he was proclaiming good news to the people. <verse eid="LUK 3:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 3:19"/>But Herod the tetrarch, being reproved by him concerning Herodias the wife of his brother<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of his brother Philip</char></note> and concerning all the evils that Herod did, <verse eid="LUK 3:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 3:20"/>also added this to all: He also locked up John in prison. <verse eid="LUK 3:20"/></para>

<para style="s">The Baptism of JesusMatthew 3:13–17</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 3:21"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 1:29–34</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 1:9–11</char></note>Now it came to pass, in all the people having been baptized and Jesus having been baptized and praying, <char style="add">that</char> heaven was opened, <verse eid="LUK 3:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 3:22"/>and the Holy Spirit descended upon Him in a bodily form as a dove. And a voice came out of heaven:<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">saying</char></note> “You are My Son, the Beloved; in You I was well pleased.” <verse eid="LUK 3:22"/></para>

<para style="s">The Genealogy of JesusRuth 4:18–22</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 3:23"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 1:1–17</char></note>And Jesus Himself, beginning, was about thirty years <char style="add">old</char>, </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 3:23">being son, as was supposed, of Joseph, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:23">of Heli,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> NA, F35, SBL, Stephanus TR, TH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Eli</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 3:23"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 3:24"/>of Matthat,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 and GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Matthan</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; NA and SBL </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Maththat</char></note> of Levi, of Melchi, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:24">of Jannai,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Janna</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Joanna</char></note> of Joseph, <verse eid="LUK 3:24"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 3:25"/>of Mattathias, of Amos, of Nahum, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:25">of Esli,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Eslim</char></note> of Naggai, <verse eid="LUK 3:25"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 3:26"/>of Maath, of Mattathias, of Semein,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Semei</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; TH and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Semeein</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; ALT and HF </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Semeei</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Semeu</char></note> </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:26">of Josech, of Joda,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Judah</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 3:26"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 3:27"/>of Joannen,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Joannan</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; ALT and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Joanna</char></note> of Rhesa, of Zerubbabel, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:27">of Shealtiel, of Neri, <verse eid="LUK 3:27"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 3:28"/>of Melchi, of Addi, of Cosam, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:28">of Elmadam,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Elmodam</char></note> of Er, <verse eid="LUK 3:28"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 3:29"/>of Joshua,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jose</char></note> of Eliezer, of Jorim, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:29">of Matthat, of Levi, <verse eid="LUK 3:29"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 3:30"/>of Simeon, of Judah, of Joseph, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:30">of Jonam,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.30 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> RP and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jonan</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jona</char></note> of Eliakim, <verse eid="LUK 3:30"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 3:31"/>of Melea, of Menna,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Mainan</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Scrivener TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Menam</char></note> of Mattatha, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:31">of Nathan, of David, <verse eid="LUK 3:31"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 3:32"/>of Jesse, of Obed, of Boaz, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:32">of Sala,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.32 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Salmon</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Ruth 4:20–21 and Matthew 1:4–5.</char></note> of Nahshon, <verse eid="LUK 3:32"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 3:33"/>of Amminadab, of Admin,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Aram</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; others </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Ram</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Ruth 4:19 and Matthew 1:3–4.</char></note> of Arni,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH, BYZ, and TR do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of Arni</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:33">of Hezron, of Perez, of Judah, <verse eid="LUK 3:33"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 3:34"/>of Jacob, of Isaac, of Abraham, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:34">of Terah, of Nahor, <verse eid="LUK 3:34"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 3:35"/>of Serug, of Reu, of Peleg, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:35">of Eber, of Shelah, <verse eid="LUK 3:35"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 3:36"/>of Cainan, of Arphaxad, of Shem, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:36">of Noah, of Lamech, <verse eid="LUK 3:36"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 3:37"/>of Methuselah, of Enoch, of Jared, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:37">of Mahalalel, of Cainan, <verse eid="LUK 3:37"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 3:38"/>of Enosh, of Seth, of Adam, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 3:38">of God. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">3.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Greek </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Enōs</char><char style="ft" closed="false">, a variant spelling of Enosh; see Genesis 5:6.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 3:38"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 3"/>

<chapter number="4" style="c" sid="LUK 4"/>

<para style="s">The Temptation of Jesus</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 4:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 1:12–13</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 4:1–11</char></note>And Jesus, full of the Holy Spirit, returned from the Jordan and was led in the Spirit in the wilderness,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in the desert</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 4:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 4:2"/>being tempted forty days by the devil. And He ate nothing in those days, and <char style="add">on</char> their having ended, He was hungry.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">afterward He was hungry</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 4:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 4:3"/>And the devil said to Him, “If You are the Son of God, speak to this stone, that it might become bread.” <verse eid="LUK 4:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 4:4"/>And Jesus answered to him, <char style="wj">“It has been written: ‘Not on bread alone shall the man live.’”</char> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 8:3; BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">but on every word of God</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">but on every word that comes from the mouth of God</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 4:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 4:5"/>And having led Him up,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">having led Him up to a high place</char></note> he showed Him all the kingdoms of the world in a moment of time. <verse eid="LUK 4:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 4:6"/>And the devil said to Him, “I will give to You all this authority and their glory; for it has been delivered to me, and I give it to whom I wish. <verse eid="LUK 4:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 4:7"/>You, therefore, if You worship before me, all will be Yours.” <verse eid="LUK 4:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 4:8"/>And answering, Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“It has been written: ‘You shall worship the Lord your God, and Him only shall you serve.’</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Get behind Me, Satan! It has been written:</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR and GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Get behind Me, Satan! For it has been written:</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 6:13</char></note><verse eid="LUK 4:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 4:9"/>And he led Him to Jerusalem and set <char style="add">Him</char> on the pinnacle of the temple, and he said to Him, “If You are the Son of God, throw Yourself down from here. <verse eid="LUK 4:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 4:10"/>For it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 4:10">‘He will command His angels concerning You </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 4:10">to guard You carefully, <verse eid="LUK 4:10"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 4:11"/>and on hands they will lift You up, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 4:11">lest ever You may strike Your foot against a stone.’” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 91:11–12</char></note><verse eid="LUK 4:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 4:12"/>And answering, Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“It has been said, ‘You shall not test the Lord your God.’”</char> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 6:16</char></note><verse eid="LUK 4:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 4:13"/>And the devil, having ended every temptation, departed from Him until an opportune time. <verse eid="LUK 4:13"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Begins His MinistryIsaiah 9:1–7</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 4:14"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 1:14–15</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 4:12–17</char></note>And Jesus returned in the power of the Spirit to Galilee, and a report went out through all the surrounding region concerning Him. <verse eid="LUK 4:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 4:15"/>And He was teaching in their synagogues, being glorified by all. <verse eid="LUK 4:15"/></para>

<para style="s">The Rejection at NazarethIsaiah 61:1–11</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 4:16"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 6:1–6</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 13:53–58</char></note>And He came to Nazareth, where He had been brought up; and according to His custom, He entered into the synagogue on the day of the Sabbaths, and He rose up to read. <verse eid="LUK 4:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 4:17"/>And the scroll of the prophet Isaiah<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Isaiah the prophet</char></note> was given to Him, and having unrolled the scroll, He found the place where it was written: <verse eid="LUK 4:17"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 4:18"/>“The Spirit of the Lord <char style="add">is</char> upon Me, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 4:18">because of which He anointed Me </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 4:18">to preach good news to the poor. </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 4:18">He has sent Me to proclaim deliverance to the captives </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 4:18">and recovery of sight to the blind, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 4:18">to send forth the oppressed in deliverance, <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to heal the brokenhearted</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 4:18"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 4:19"/>to proclaim the acceptable year of the Lord.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to proclaim the year of the Lord's favor</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Isaiah 61:1–2 (see also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="LUK 4:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 4:20"/>And having rolled up the scroll, having given <char style="add">it</char> back to the attendant, He sat down. And the eyes of all in the synagogue were looking intently on Him. <verse eid="LUK 4:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 4:21"/>And He began to say to them, <char style="wj">“Today this Scripture has been fulfilled in your⁺ ears.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 4:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 4:22"/>And all were testifying to Him and were marveling at the words of grace that are proceeding out of His mouth. And they were saying, “Is this not the son of Joseph?” <verse eid="LUK 4:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 4:23"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Surely you⁺ will say to Me this parable: ‘Physician, heal yourself! Whatsoever we heard was done in Capernaum, do also here in Your hometown.’”</char> <verse eid="LUK 4:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 4:24"/>And He said, <char style="wj">“Truly I say to you⁺ that no prophet is acceptable in his hometown. </char><verse eid="LUK 4:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 4:25"/><char style="wj">And in truth I say to you⁺, many widows were in Israel in the days of Elijah, when the sky was shut up for three years and six months, when a great famine came upon all the land. </char><verse eid="LUK 4:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 4:26"/><char style="wj">And Elijah was sent to none of them, except to Zarephath of the Sidonians, to a woman, a widow. </char><verse eid="LUK 4:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 4:27"/><char style="wj">And many lepers</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> A </char><char style="fq" closed="false">leper</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> was one afflicted with a skin disease. See Leviticus 13.</char></note><char style="wj"> were in Israel in the time of Elisha the prophet, and none of them was cleansed, except Naaman the Syrian.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 4:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 4:28"/>And all in the synagogue were filled with anger, hearing these things. <verse eid="LUK 4:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 4:29"/>And having risen up, they cast Him out of the city and led Him unto the brow of the hill upon which their city had been built, so as to throw Him over. <verse eid="LUK 4:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 4:30"/>But He, having passed through their midst, went away. <verse eid="LUK 4:30"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Expels an Unclean SpiritMark 1:21–28</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 4:31"/>And He went down to Capernaum, a city of Galilee, and He was teaching them on the Sabbaths. <verse eid="LUK 4:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 4:32"/>And they were astonished at His teaching, because His word was with authority. <verse eid="LUK 4:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 4:33"/>And in the synagogue was a man having a spirit of an unclean demon. And he cried out in a loud voice, <verse eid="LUK 4:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 4:34"/>“Ha! What to us and to You, Jesus the Nazarene? Did You come to destroy us? I know who You are—the Holy One of God!” <verse eid="LUK 4:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 4:35"/>And Jesus rebuked him, saying, <char style="wj">“Be silenced, and come out from him!”</char> And having thrown him into the midst, the demon came out from him, in nothing having hurt him. <verse eid="LUK 4:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 4:36"/>And amazement came upon all, and they were speaking together with one another, saying, “What word <char style="add">is</char> this, that with authority and power He commands the unclean spirits, and they come out?” <verse eid="LUK 4:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 4:37"/>And the report concerning Him was going out into every place of the surrounding region. <verse eid="LUK 4:37"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Heals at Peter’s HouseMatthew 8:14–17</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 4:38"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 1:29–34</char></note>And having arisen out of the synagogue, He entered into the house of Simon. And Simon’s mother-in-law was oppressed with a great fever, and they asked Him concerning her. <verse eid="LUK 4:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 4:39"/>And having stood over her, He rebuked the fever, and it left her. And immediately, having arisen, she was serving them. <verse eid="LUK 4:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 4:40"/>And <char style="add">at</char> the setting of the sun, all, as many as had <char style="add">any</char> ailing with various diseases, brought them to Him. And He, having laid the hands on each one of them, was healing them. <verse eid="LUK 4:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 4:41"/>Now demons also were going out from many, crying out and saying, “You are<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.41 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Christ</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> the Son of God!” And rebuking <char style="add">them</char>, He was not allowing them to speak, because they knew Him to be the Christ. <verse eid="LUK 4:41"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Preaches in JudeaMark 1:35–39</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 4:42"/>And day having come, having gone out, He went into a solitary place. And the crowds were seeking Him, and they came up to Him and were detaining Him not to go from them. <verse eid="LUK 4:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 4:43"/>But He said to them, <char style="wj">“It is necessary for Me to preach the good news of the kingdom of God to the other cities also, because for this I was sent.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 4:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 4:44"/>And He was preaching in the synagogues of Judea.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">4.44 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Galilee</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Mark 1:39.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 4:44"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 4"/>

<chapter number="5" style="c" sid="LUK 5"/>

<para style="s">The First Disciples</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 5:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 1:35–42</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 1:16–20</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 4:18–22</char></note>Now it came to pass, in the crowd also pressing on Him to hear the word of God, that He was standing by the lake of Gennesaret.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> That is, the Sea of Galilee</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 5:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 5:2"/>And He saw two boats standing by the lake; but the fishermen, having gone away from them, were washing the nets. <verse eid="LUK 5:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 5:3"/>And having entered into one of the boats, which was Simon’s, He asked him to put out a little from the land. And having sat down, out of the boat He was teaching the crowds. <verse eid="LUK 5:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 5:4"/>And when He ceased speaking, He said to Simon, <char style="wj">“Put out into the deep and let down your⁺ nets for a catch.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 5:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 5:5"/>And answering, Simon said, “Master, having toiled through the whole night, we took nothing. But upon Your word, I will let down the nets.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the net</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 5:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 5:6"/>And having done this, they enclosed a great multitude of fish, and their nets were tearing.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">their net was tearing</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 5:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 5:7"/>And they beckoned to the partners in the other boat, having come, to help them, and they came and filled both boats so that they were sinking. <verse eid="LUK 5:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 5:8"/>And having seen, Simon Peter fell at the knees of Jesus, saying, “Go out from me, Lord, because I am a sinful man.” <verse eid="LUK 5:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 5:9"/>For amazement laid hold on him and on all those with him at the catch of the fish that they took, <verse eid="LUK 5:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 5:10"/>and likewise also James and John, the sons of Zebedee, who were partners with Simon. And Jesus said to Simon, <char style="wj">“Do not be afraid; from now on you will be catching men.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 5:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 5:11"/>And having brought down the boats upon the land, having left all, they followed Him. <verse eid="LUK 5:11"/></para>

<para style="s">The Leper’s PrayerLeviticus 14:1–32</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 5:12"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 1:40–45</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 8:1–4</char></note>And it came to pass, in His being in one of the cities, that behold, a man full of leprosy,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Leprosy was a term used for various skin diseases. See Leviticus 13.</char></note> and having seen Jesus, having fallen upon <char style="add">his</char> face, begged Him, saying, “Lord, if You are willing, You are able to cleanse me.” <verse eid="LUK 5:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 5:13"/>And having stretched out the hand, He touched him, <char style="wj">“saying, I am willing; be cleansed!”</char> And immediately the leprosy went away from him. <verse eid="LUK 5:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 5:14"/>And He instructed him to tell no one: <char style="wj">“But, having gone, show yourself to the priest, and offer concerning your cleansing, just as Moses commanded, for a testimony to them.”</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Leviticus 14:1–32.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 5:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 5:15"/>But the word concerning Him was spreading still more, and many crowds were coming to hear and to be healed<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">by Him</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> from their infirmities. <verse eid="LUK 5:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 5:16"/>And He Himself was withdrawing into the wilderness<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">desert</char></note> and praying. <verse eid="LUK 5:16"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Heals a ParalyticMatthew 9:1–8</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 5:17"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 2:1–12</char></note>And it came to pass, on one of the days, that He was teaching, and Pharisees and teachers of the law were sitting, who were come out of every village of Galilee, and of Judea, and of Jerusalem. And the power of the Lord was <char style="add">there</char> for Him to heal. <verse eid="LUK 5:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 5:18"/>And behold, men bringing upon a bed a man who was paralyzed, and they were seeking to bring him in and to place him before Him. <verse eid="LUK 5:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 5:19"/>And not having found what way they might bring him in through the crowd, having gone up on the housetop, they let him down with the little bed through the tiles into the midst before Jesus. <verse eid="LUK 5:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 5:20"/>And having seen their faith, He said,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">He said to him</char></note> <char style="wj">“Man, your sins have been forgiven you.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 5:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 5:21"/>And the scribes and the Pharisees began to reason, saying, “Who is this who speaks blasphemies? Who is able to forgive sins except God alone?” <verse eid="LUK 5:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 5:22"/>And Jesus, having known their reasonings, answering, said to them, <char style="wj">“Why do you⁺ reason in your⁺ hearts? </char><verse eid="LUK 5:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 5:23"/><char style="wj">Which is easier: to say, ‘Your sins have been forgiven you,’ or to say, ‘Arise and walk’? </char><verse eid="LUK 5:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 5:24"/><char style="wj">But so that you⁺ may know that the Son of Man has authority on the earth to forgive sins...”</char> He said to the paralyzed one, <char style="wj">“I say to you, arise, and having taken up your mat, go to your house.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 5:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 5:25"/>And immediately, having risen up before them, having taken up <char style="add">that</char> on which he was lying, he went away to his house, glorifying God. <verse eid="LUK 5:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 5:26"/>And amazement took all, and they were glorifying God. And they were filled with fear, saying, “We saw remarkable things today.” <verse eid="LUK 5:26"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Calls LeviMatthew 9:9–13</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 5:27"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 2:13–17</char></note>And after these things, He went out and saw a tax collector named Levi sitting at the tax booth. And He said to him, <char style="wj">“Follow Me.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 5:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 5:28"/>And having left all, having arisen, he began to follow Him. <verse eid="LUK 5:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 5:29"/>And Levi made a great banquet for Him in his house, and there was a great crowd of tax collectors and others who were reclining with them. <verse eid="LUK 5:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 5:30"/>And the Pharisees and their scribes were grumbling at His disciples, saying, “Why do you⁺ eat and drink with the tax collectors and sinners?” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.30 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">their scribes and the Pharisees</char></note><verse eid="LUK 5:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 5:31"/>And answering, Jesus said to them, <char style="wj">“Those being in good health have no need of a physician, but those being ill. </char><verse eid="LUK 5:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 5:32"/><char style="wj">I have not come to call righteous ones, but sinners, to repentance.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 5:32"/></para>

<para style="s">Questions about FastingMatthew 9:14–15</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 5:33"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 2:18–20</char></note>And they said to Him, “The disciples<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Why do the disciples</char></note> of John fast often and make petitions; likewise also those of the Pharisees; but Yours⁺ eat and drink.” <verse eid="LUK 5:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 5:34"/>And Jesus said to them, <char style="wj">“Are you⁺ able to make the sons of the bridechamber fast while the bridegroom is with them? </char><verse eid="LUK 5:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 5:35"/><char style="wj">But days will come also when the bridegroom shall have been taken away from them; then they will fast in those days.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 5:35"/></para>

<para style="s">The Patches and the WineskinsMatthew 9:16–17</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 5:36"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 2:21–22</char></note>And He was speaking also a parable to them: <char style="wj">“No one having torn a patch from a new garment puts it on</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.36 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">No one sews a piece of cloth from a new garment onto</char></note><char style="wj"> an old garment. and if otherwise, he will tear the new also, and the old will not match the patch from the new. </char><verse eid="LUK 5:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 5:37"/>And no one puts new wine into old wineskins. and if otherwise, the new wine will burst the wineskins, and it will be poured out, and the wineskins will be destroyed. <verse eid="LUK 5:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 5:38"/>But new wine must be put into fresh wineskins.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Matthew 9:17; BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and both are preserved</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 5:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 5:39"/>And no one having drunk old <char style="add">wine</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">5.39 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">immediately</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> wants new; for he says, ‘The old is good.’” <verse eid="LUK 5:39"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 5"/>

<chapter number="6" style="c" sid="LUK 6"/>

<para style="s">The Lord of the Sabbath</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 6:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 2:23–28</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 12:1–8</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">1 Samuel 21:1–7</char></note>And it came to pass on a Sabbath<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the second chief Sabbath</char></note> <char style="add">that</char> He is passing through grainfields, and His disciples were plucking and were eating the heads of grain, rubbing <char style="add">them</char> in the hands. <verse eid="LUK 6:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 6:2"/>But some of the Pharisees said, “Why do you⁺ do what is not lawful<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to do</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <char style="add">on</char> the Sabbaths?” <verse eid="LUK 6:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 6:3"/>And answering, Jesus said to them, <char style="wj">“Did you⁺ not even read this, what David did when he was hungry—he and those being with him: </char><verse eid="LUK 6:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 6:4"/><char style="wj">how he entered into the house of God and, having taken the loaves of the presentation,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Bread of the Presence</char></note><char style="wj"> he ate and</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">also</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> gave to those with him—which it is not lawful to eat, except only for the priests?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 6:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 6:5"/>And He was saying to them, <char style="wj">“The Son of Man is Lord</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">even</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> of the Sabbath.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 6:5"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Heals on the SabbathMatthew 12:9–14</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 6:6"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 3:1–6</char></note>And it came to pass on another Sabbath <char style="add">that</char> He entered into the synagogue and taught, and there was a man there, and his right hand was withered. <verse eid="LUK 6:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 6:7"/>And the scribes and the Pharisees were closely watching Him, whether He heals on the Sabbath, so that they might find <char style="add">reason</char> to accuse Him.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">find an accusation Against Him</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 6:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 6:8"/>And He had known their reasonings, and He said to the man having the withered hand, <char style="wj">“Arise and stand in the midst.”</char> And having risen up, he stood. <verse eid="LUK 6:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 6:9"/>Then Jesus said to them, <char style="wj">“I ask you⁺, is it lawful</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">I ask you one thing: Is it lawful</char></note><char style="wj"> on the Sabbath to do good or to do evil, to save life or to destroy?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 6:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 6:10"/>And having looked around at them all, He said to him, <char style="wj">“Stretch out your hand.”</char> And he did, and his hand was restored.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">as sound as the other</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 6:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 6:11"/>And they were filled with madness, and they were discussing with one another what they might do to Jesus. <verse eid="LUK 6:11"/></para>

<para style="s">The Twelve ApostlesMatthew 10:1–4</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 6:12"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 3:13–19</char></note>And it came to pass in those days <char style="add">that</char> He went out to the mountain to pray, and He was spending the night in prayer to God. <verse eid="LUK 6:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 6:13"/>And when it became day, He summoned His disciples, also having chosen out from them twelve, whom He also named apostles: <verse eid="LUK 6:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 6:14"/>Simon, whom He also named Peter, and his brother Andrew, and James and John, and Philip and Bartholomew, <verse eid="LUK 6:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 6:15"/>and Matthew and Thomas, and James <char style="add">son of</char> Alphaeus, and Simon called Zealot, <verse eid="LUK 6:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 6:16"/>and Judas <char style="add">son</char> of James, and Judas Iscariot, who became a betrayer. <verse eid="LUK 6:16"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Heals the MultitudesMatthew 4:23–25</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 6:17"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 3:7–12</char></note>And having come down with them, He stood on a level place, and a great crowd<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">a crowd</char></note> of His disciples, and a great multitude of the people from all Judea and Jerusalem and the sea coast of Tyre and Sidon, <verse eid="LUK 6:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 6:18"/>who came to hear Him and to be healed from their diseases. And those troubled from unclean spirits were healed.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of their diseases, along with those troubled by unclean spirits. And they were healed.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 6:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 6:19"/>And all the crowd was seeking to touch Him, because power was going out from Him and was healing all. <verse eid="LUK 6:19"/></para>

<para style="s">The BeatitudesPsalm 1:1–6</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 6:20"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 5:3–12</char></note>And He, having lifted up His eyes to His disciples, was saying: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 6:20">“Blessed <char style="add">are</char> the poor, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 6:20">because yours⁺ is the kingdom of God. <verse eid="LUK 6:20"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 6:21"/>Blessed <char style="add">are</char> those hungering now, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 6:21">because you⁺ will be filled. </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 6:21">Blessed <char style="add">are</char> those weeping now, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 6:21">because you⁺ will laugh. <verse eid="LUK 6:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 6:22"/>Blessed are you⁺ when men may hate you⁺, and when they may exclude you⁺ and may reproach <char style="add">you</char> and may cast out your⁺ name as evil, on account of the Son of Man. <verse eid="LUK 6:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 6:23"/>Rejoice in that day and leap, for behold, your⁺ reward <char style="add">is</char> great in heaven. For according to the same their fathers were doing to the prophets. <verse eid="LUK 6:23"/></para>

<para style="s">Woes to the SatisfiedAmos 6:1–7</para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 6:24"/>But woe to you⁺ who <char style="add">are</char> rich, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 6:24">because you⁺ have your⁺ comfort in full. <verse eid="LUK 6:24"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 6:25"/>Woe to you⁺ who have been filled now,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">now</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 6:25">because you⁺ will hunger. </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 6:25">Woe <char style="add">to</char> those laughing now, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 6:25">because you⁺ will mourn and weep. <verse eid="LUK 6:25"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 6:26"/>Woe<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Most translations include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to you</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> as clarification, though it is not included in the vast majority of Greek manuscripts.</char></note> when all men<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> RP </char><char style="fq" closed="false">when men</char></note> may speak well of you⁺, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 6:26">for according to the same their fathers were doing to the false prophets. <verse eid="LUK 6:26"/></para>

<para style="s">Love Your EnemiesMatthew 5:38–48</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 6:27"/>But to you⁺ who <char style="add">are</char> hearing I say: Love your⁺ enemies, do good to those hating you⁺, <verse eid="LUK 6:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 6:28"/>bless those cursing you⁺, pray for those mistreating you⁺. <verse eid="LUK 6:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 6:29"/>To the one striking you on the cheek, offer the other also; and from the one taking away your cloak, do not withhold also <char style="add">your</char> tunic. <verse eid="LUK 6:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 6:30"/>To everyone asking you, give, and from the one taking away what <char style="add">is</char> yours⁺, do not demand <char style="add">it</char> back. <verse eid="LUK 6:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 6:31"/>And just as you⁺ want that men may do to you⁺, do to them likewise. <verse eid="LUK 6:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 6:32"/>And if you⁺ love those loving you⁺, what grace is it to you⁺? For even the sinners love those loving them. <verse eid="LUK 6:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 6:33"/>For even if<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For if</char></note> you⁺ do good to those doing good to you⁺, what grace is it to you⁺? Even<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For even</char></note> the sinners do the same. <verse eid="LUK 6:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 6:34"/>And if you⁺ lend <char style="add">to</char> <char style="add">those</char> from whom you⁺ expect to receive back, what grace is it to you⁺? Even<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For even</char></note> sinners lend to sinners, that they might receive back the equivalent. <verse eid="LUK 6:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 6:35"/>But love your⁺ enemies, and do good, and lend expecting nothing back. And your⁺ reward will be great, and you⁺ will be sons of the Most High, because He is kind to the ungrateful and evil. <verse eid="LUK 6:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 6:36"/>Be<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.36 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Therefore be</char></note> merciful, as also your⁺ Father is merciful. <verse eid="LUK 6:36"/></para>

<para style="s">Judging OthersMatthew 7:1–6</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 6:37"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Romans 14:1–12</char></note>And do not judge, and you⁺ shall not be judged; and do not condemn, and you⁺ shall not be condemned; release, and you⁺ will be released. <verse eid="LUK 6:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 6:38"/>Give, and it will be given to you⁺. A good measure, pressed down, shaken, running over, they will give into your⁺ bosom. For with what<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For with the same</char></note> measure you⁺ measure, it will be measured back to you⁺.” <verse eid="LUK 6:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 6:39"/>And He also spoke a parable to them: <char style="wj">“Is a blind man able to guide a blind man? Will they not both fall into a pit? </char><verse eid="LUK 6:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 6:40"/><char style="wj">A disciple is not above his teacher, but everyone fully trained will be like his teacher. </char><verse eid="LUK 6:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 6:41"/>And why do you look at the splinter that <char style="add">is</char> in the eye of your brother, but not notice the beam that <char style="add">is</char> in <char style="add">your</char> own eye? <verse eid="LUK 6:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 6:42"/>How<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.42 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Or how</char></note> are you able to say to your brother, ‘Brother, permit <char style="add">that</char> I might cast out the splinter that <char style="add">is</char> in your eye,’ yourself not seeing the beam in your eye? Hypocrite! First cast out the beam from your eye, and then you will see clearly to cast out the splinter that <char style="add">is</char> in the eye of your brother. <verse eid="LUK 6:42"/></para>

<para style="s">A Tree and Its FruitMatthew 7:15–23</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 6:43"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 12:33–37</char></note>For there is no good tree producing rotten fruit, nor again a rotten tree producing good fruit. <verse eid="LUK 6:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 6:44"/>For each tree is known by <char style="add">its</char> own fruit. For they do not gather figs from thorns, nor do they pick grapes from a bramble bush. <verse eid="LUK 6:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 6:45"/>The good man out of the good treasure of his heart brings forth what <char style="add">is</char> good, and the evil out of the evil brings forth what <char style="add">is</char> evil. For out of the abundance of the heart, his mouth speaks. <verse eid="LUK 6:45"/></para>

<para style="s">The House on the RockMatthew 7:24–27</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 6:46"/>And why do you⁺ call Me ‘Lord, Lord,’ and not do what I say? <verse eid="LUK 6:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 6:47"/>Everyone who is coming to Me and hearing My words and doing them, I will show you⁺ whom he is like. <verse eid="LUK 6:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 6:48"/>He is like a man building a house, who dug and deepened and laid a foundation on the rock. And a flood having come, the river burst upon that house, and it was not able to shake it, because it <char style="add">was</char> well built.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.48 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">because it had been founded upon the rock</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 7:25.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 6:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 6:49"/>But the one having heard and not having done is like a man having built a house<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">6.49 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 </char><char style="fq" closed="false">his house</char></note> on the ground without a foundation, on which the river burst, and immediately it fell—and the ruin of that house was great!” <verse eid="LUK 6:49"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 6"/>

<chapter number="7" style="c" sid="LUK 7"/>

<para style="s">The Faith of the Centurion</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 7:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 4:43–54</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 8:5–13</char></note>And when He completed all His words in the hearing of the people, He entered into Capernaum. <verse eid="LUK 7:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 7:2"/>And a certain servant of a centurion, who was valued highly to him, being ill, was about to die. <verse eid="LUK 7:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 7:3"/>And having heard about Jesus, he sent elders of the Jews to Him, asking Him that, having come, He might save his servant. <verse eid="LUK 7:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 7:4"/>And having come to Jesus, they were imploring Him earnestly, saying, “He is worthy to whom You will grant this, <verse eid="LUK 7:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 7:5"/>for he loves our nation, and he built the synagogue for us.” <verse eid="LUK 7:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 7:6"/>And Jesus was going with them, and now <char style="add">in</char> His being not far away from the house, the centurion sent friends, saying to Him, “Lord, do not trouble Yourself, for I am not fit that You should enter under my roof. <verse eid="LUK 7:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 7:7"/>Therefore neither did I count myself worthy to come to You. But say a word, and let my servant be healed. <verse eid="LUK 7:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 7:8"/>For I also am a man appointed under authority, having soldiers under me. And I say to this one, ‘Go,’ and he goes; and to another, ‘Come,’ and he comes; and to my servant, ‘Do this,’ and he does.” <verse eid="LUK 7:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 7:9"/>And having heard these things, Jesus marveled at him. And having turned to the crowd following Him, He said, <char style="wj">“I say to you⁺, not even in Israel did I find such great faith.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 7:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 7:10"/>And having returned to the house, those having been sent found the servant<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the ailing servant</char></note> in good health. <verse eid="LUK 7:10"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Raises a Widow’s Son</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 7:11"/>And it came to pass on the next day <char style="add">that</char> He went into a city called Nain, and His<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and many of His</char></note> disciples were going with Him, and a great crowd. <verse eid="LUK 7:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 7:12"/>And as He drew near to the gate of the city, then, behold, a dead <char style="add">man</char> was being carried out, the only begotten son of his mother; and she was a widow. And a large crowd of the city was with her. <verse eid="LUK 7:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 7:13"/>And having seen her, the Lord was moved with compassion toward her and said to her, <char style="wj">“Do not weep.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 7:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 7:14"/>And having come up, He touched the bier;<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">coffin</char><char style="ft" closed="false">, probably a wooden plank or open coffin</char></note> and those bearing <char style="add">it</char> stood still. And He said, <char style="wj">“Young man, I say to you, arise!”</char> <verse eid="LUK 7:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 7:15"/>And the dead <char style="add">man</char> sat up and began to speak, and He gave him to his mother. <verse eid="LUK 7:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 7:16"/>And fear took all, and they were glorifying God, saying, “A great prophet arose among us!” and, “God visited His people!” <verse eid="LUK 7:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 7:17"/>And the word of this went forth in all Judea about Him—and in all the surrounding region. <verse eid="LUK 7:17"/></para>

<para style="s">John’s InquiryMatthew 11:1–6</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 7:18"/>And his disciples reported to John about all these things. <verse eid="LUK 7:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 7:19"/>And having summoned a certain two of his disciples, John sent to the Lord,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to Jesus</char></note> saying, “Are You the coming One, or do we look for another?” <verse eid="LUK 7:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 7:20"/>And having come to Him, the men said, “John the Baptist sent us to You, saying, ‘Are You the coming One, or do we look for another?’” <verse eid="LUK 7:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 7:21"/>In<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Now in</char></note> that very hour, He healed many of diseases and afflictions and evil spirits, and to many blind He granted to see. <verse eid="LUK 7:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 7:22"/>And answering, He said<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jesus said</char></note> to them, <char style="wj">“Having gone, report to John what you⁺ saw and heard: The blind receive sight, the lame walk, the lepers</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> A </char><char style="fq" closed="false">leper</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> was one afflicted with a skin disease. See Leviticus 13.</char></note><char style="wj"> are cleansed, and the deaf hear, the dead are raised, the poor have good news proclaimed. </char><verse eid="LUK 7:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 7:23"/><char style="wj">And blessed is the one who shall not be caused to stumble in Me.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">who does not fall away on account of Me</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <verse eid="LUK 7:23"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Testifies about JohnMalachi 3:1–5</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 7:24"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 11:7–19</char></note>And the messengers<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">disciples</char></note> of John having gone away, He began to speak to the crowds about John: <char style="wj">“What did you⁺ go out into the wilderness</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">desert</char></note><char style="wj"> to see? A reed shaken by the wind? </char><verse eid="LUK 7:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 7:25"/><char style="wj">But what did you⁺ go out to see? A man arrayed in soft garments? Behold, those in splendid clothing and living in luxury are in the palaces. </char><verse eid="LUK 7:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 7:26"/>But what did you⁺ go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I say to you⁺, and more than a prophet. <verse eid="LUK 7:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 7:27"/>This is he about whom it has been written: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 7:27">‘Behold, I send My messenger before Your face, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 7:27">who will prepare Your way before You.’ <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Malachi 3:1</char></note><verse eid="LUK 7:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 7:28"/>I say to you⁺, no one among <char style="add">those</char> born of women is greater than John;<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For I say to you, among those born of women there is no one greater prophet than John the Baptist</char></note> yet the least in the kingdom of God is greater than he.” <verse eid="LUK 7:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 7:29"/>And all the people having heard, even the tax collectors, declared God righteous, having been baptized <char style="add">with</char> the baptism of John. <verse eid="LUK 7:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 7:30"/>But the Pharisees and the lawyers set aside the counsel of God for themselves, not having been baptized by him. <verse eid="LUK 7:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 7:31"/>“To what,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And the Lord said, “To what</char></note> therefore, will I liken the men of this generation? And to what are they like? <verse eid="LUK 7:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 7:32"/>They are like children sitting in the marketplace and calling to one another, saying: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 7:32">‘We played the flute for you⁺, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 7:32">and you⁺ did not dance; </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 7:32">we sang a dirge,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.32 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">for you</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 7:32">and you⁺ did not weep.’ <verse eid="LUK 7:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 7:33"/>For John the Baptist has come neither eating bread nor drinking wine, and you⁺ say, ‘He has a demon!’ <verse eid="LUK 7:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 7:34"/>The Son of Man has come eating and drinking, and you⁺ say, ‘Behold, a man, a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and of sinners!’ <verse eid="LUK 7:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 7:35"/>And wisdom was justified from all her children.” <verse eid="LUK 7:35"/></para>

<para style="s">A Sinful Woman Anoints JesusMatthew 26:6–13</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 7:36"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 12:1–8</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:3–9</char></note>And one of the Pharisees was requesting of Him that He should eat with him, and having entered into the house of the Pharisee, He reclined. <verse eid="LUK 7:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 7:37"/>And behold, a woman in the city who was a sinner. And having known that He is reclining in the house of the Pharisee, having taken an alabaster flask of fragrant oil, <verse eid="LUK 7:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 7:38"/>and having stood behind <char style="add">Him</char> at His feet weeping, she began to wet His feet with <char style="add">her</char> tears, and she was wiping <char style="add">them</char> with the hairs of her head, and she was kissing His feet and was anointing <char style="add">them</char> with the fragrant oil. <verse eid="LUK 7:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 7:39"/>And the Pharisee, the one having called Him, having seen, spoke within himself, saying, “If this <char style="add">man</char> were a prophet,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.39 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the prophet</char></note> He would have known who and what kind the woman <char style="add">is</char> who touches Him—because she is a sinner.” <verse eid="LUK 7:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 7:40"/>And answering, Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“Simon, I have something to say to you.”</char> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 7:40">And he says, “Teacher, say <char style="add">it</char>.” <verse eid="LUK 7:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 7:41"/>“There were two debtors to a certain lender. The one owed five hundred denarii,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.41 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; see Matthew 20:2.</char></note> and the other fifty. <verse eid="LUK 7:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 7:42"/>Of them having nothing to repay, he forgave both. Therefore<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.42 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Tell Me, therefore</char></note> which of them will love him more?” <verse eid="LUK 7:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 7:43"/>Answering, Simon said, “I suppose <char style="add">he</char> to whom he forgave the more.” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 7:43">And He said to him, <char style="wj">“You judged correctly.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 7:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 7:44"/>And having turned to the woman, He was saying to Simon, <char style="wj">“Do you see this woman? I entered into your house; you did not give water for My feet, but she herself wet My feet with her tears and wiped them with her hair.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.44 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">with the hair of her head</char></note><char style="wj"> </char><verse eid="LUK 7:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 7:45"/><char style="wj">You did not give to Me a kiss, but from which time I entered,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">7.45 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">from the time she arrived</char></note><char style="wj"> she herself did not cease kissing My feet. </char><verse eid="LUK 7:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 7:46"/><char style="wj">You did not anoint My head with oil, but she herself anointed My feet with fragrant oil. </char><verse eid="LUK 7:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 7:47"/><char style="wj">Therefore I say to you this: Her many sins have been forgiven, because she loved much; but to whom little is forgiven, he loves little.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 7:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 7:48"/>And He said to her, <char style="wj">“Your sins have been forgiven.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 7:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 7:49"/>And those reclining with <char style="add">Him</char> began to say within themselves, “Who is this who even forgives sins?” <verse eid="LUK 7:49"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 7:50"/>And He said to the woman, <char style="wj">“Your faith has saved you; go in peace.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 7:50"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 7"/>

<chapter number="8" style="c" sid="LUK 8"/>

<para style="s">Women Minister to Jesus</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 8:1"/>And it came to pass in the thereafter that He was traveling through each city and village preaching and proclaiming the good news of the kingdom of God, and the Twelve <char style="add">were</char> with Him, <verse eid="LUK 8:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 8:2"/>and certain women who had been healed from evil spirits<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">healed of diseases, afflictions, evil spirits,</char></note> and infirmities: Mary who is called Magdalene, from whom seven demons had gone out, <verse eid="LUK 8:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 8:3"/>and Joanna wife of Chuza, steward of Herod, and Susanna, and many others, who were ministering to them<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35, TR, and GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to Him</char></note> out of their means. <verse eid="LUK 8:3"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the SowerMatthew 13:1–23</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 8:4"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 4:1–20</char></note>And a great crowd assembling and those <char style="add">from</char> each town coming to Him, He spoke by a parable: <verse eid="LUK 8:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 8:5"/><char style="wj">“The one sowing went out to sow his seed. And in his sowing, some indeed fell along the path, and it was trampled upon, and the birds of the sky devoured it. </char><verse eid="LUK 8:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 8:6"/>And other fell upon the rock, and having sprung up, it withered because of <char style="add">it</char> not having moisture. <verse eid="LUK 8:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 8:7"/>And other fell in the midst of the thorns, and having sprung up with <char style="add">it</char>, the thorns choked it. <verse eid="LUK 8:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 8:8"/>And other fell upon the good soil, and having sprung up, it produced fruit—a hundredfold.” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 8:8">Saying these things, He was calling out, <char style="wj">“The one having ears to hear, let him hear.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 8:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 8:9"/>And His disciples were asking Him, “What may this parable be?” <verse eid="LUK 8:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 8:10"/>And He said, <char style="wj">“To you⁺ it has been given to know the mysteries of the kingdom of God, but to the rest, it is in parables, so that, </char></para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 8:10">‘Seeing they may not see, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 8:10">and hearing they may not understand.’ <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 6:9 (See also LXX)</char></note><verse eid="LUK 8:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 8:11"/>And this is the parable: The seed is the word of God. <verse eid="LUK 8:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 8:12"/>And the ones along the path are those having heard; then the devil comes and takes away the word from their heart, lest having believed, they should be saved. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the seeds</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; also in verses 13, 14, and 15</char></note><verse eid="LUK 8:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 8:13"/>And the ones upon the rock <char style="add">are</char> those who, when they may hear, receive the word with joy; and these have no root, who believe for a time, and in time of testing they fall away. <verse eid="LUK 8:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 8:14"/>And the <char style="add">seed</char> having fallen into the thorns, these are those having heard, and, going along, are choked under the anxieties and riches and pleasures of life, and they do not mature. <verse eid="LUK 8:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 8:15"/>And the <char style="add">seed</char> in the good soil, these are those <char style="add">who</char>, in a beautiful and good heart, having heard the word, hold <char style="add">it</char> fast and bear fruit in perseverance. <verse eid="LUK 8:15"/></para>

<para style="s">The Lesson of the LampMark 4:21–25</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 8:16"/>And no one,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 </char><char style="fq" closed="false">As He said this He called out, “He who has ears to hear, let him hear. No one</char></note> having lit a lamp, covers it with a vessel or puts <char style="add">it</char> under a bed. But he puts <char style="add">it</char> on a lampstand, so that those entering may see the light. <verse eid="LUK 8:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 8:17"/>For nothing is hidden that will not become manifest, nor hidden away that shall not be known and come to visibility. <verse eid="LUK 8:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 8:18"/>Therefore look how you⁺ hear; for whoever may have, to him <char style="add">it</char> will be given; and whoever may not have, even what he thinks to have will be taken away from him.” <verse eid="LUK 8:18"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus’ Mother and BrothersMatthew 12:46–50</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 8:19"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 3:31–35</char></note>Now His mother and brothers came to Him, and they were not able to get to Him because of the crowd. <verse eid="LUK 8:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 8:20"/>And it was reported to Him, “Your mother and Your brothers are standing outside, wanting to see You.” <verse eid="LUK 8:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 8:21"/>But answering, He said to them, <char style="wj">“My mother and My brothers are those who are hearing and doing the word of God.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 8:21"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Calms the StormPsalm 107:1–43</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 8:22"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 4:35–41</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 8:23–27</char></note>And it came to pass on one of the days also <char style="add">that</char> He and His disciples entered into a boat, and He said to them, <char style="wj">“Let us pass over to the other side of the lake.”</char> And they set out. <verse eid="LUK 8:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 8:23"/>And of their sailing, He fell asleep. And a storm of wind came down to the lake, and they were being swamped and were in danger. <verse eid="LUK 8:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 8:24"/>And having come, they awoke Him, saying, “Master, Master, we are perishing!” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 8:24">And having arisen, He rebuked the wind and the raging of the water, and they ceased, and it became calm. <verse eid="LUK 8:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 8:25"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Where is your⁺ faith?”</char> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 8:25">And having feared, they marveled, saying to one another, “Who then is this, that He commands even the winds and the water, and they obey Him?” <verse eid="LUK 8:25"/></para>

<para style="s">The Demons and the PigsMatthew 8:28–34</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 8:26"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 5:1–20</char></note>And they sailed down<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And He sailed</char></note> to the region of the Gerasenes,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Gadarenes</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Tischendorf </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Gergesenes</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; also in verse 37</char></note> which is opposite Galilee. <verse eid="LUK 8:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 8:27"/>And <char style="add">on</char> His having gone out upon the land, a certain man out of the city met <char style="add">Him</char>, having demons. And for a long time he was not wearing<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">He was met by a man from the city who had been demon-possessed for a long time. He was not wearing</char></note> clothing and was not abiding in a house, but in the tombs. <verse eid="LUK 8:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 8:28"/>And having seen Jesus, having cried out, he fell down before Him and said in a loud voice, “What <char style="add">is it</char> to me and to You, Jesus, Son of the Most High God? I beg You, do not torment me!” <verse eid="LUK 8:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 8:29"/>For He commanded the unclean spirit to come out from the man. For many times it had seized him, and he was bound, being kept with chains and shackles. And breaking the bonds, he was driven by the demon into the wilderness. <verse eid="LUK 8:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 8:30"/>And Jesus asked him, <char style="wj">“What is your name?”</char> And he said, “Legion,” because many demons were entered into him. <verse eid="LUK 8:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 8:31"/>And they were imploring<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and Stephanus TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">he was imploring</char></note> Him that He would not command them to go away into the Abyss. <verse eid="LUK 8:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 8:32"/>Now there was a herd of many pigs feeding on the mountain, and they implored Him that He would allow them to enter into them, and He allowed them. <verse eid="LUK 8:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 8:33"/>And the demons, having gone out from the man, entered into the pigs, and the herd rushed down the steep bank into the lake and was drowned. <verse eid="LUK 8:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 8:34"/>And those feeding, having seen what had happened, fled<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">fled, went away,</char></note> and reported <char style="add">it</char> to the city and to the fields. <verse eid="LUK 8:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 8:35"/>And they went out to see what had happened, and they came to Jesus and found the man from whom the demons went out sitting, clothed and sound-minded, at the feet of Jesus. And they were afraid. <verse eid="LUK 8:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 8:36"/>And those having seen related to them how the one having been possessed by demons was saved. <verse eid="LUK 8:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 8:37"/>And all the multitude of the surrounding region of the Gerasenes asked Him to go away from them, because they were seized with great fear. And He, having entered into the boat, turned back. <verse eid="LUK 8:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 8:38"/>But the man from whom the demons had gone out was begging Him to be with Him. But He sent him away,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">But Jesus sent him away</char></note> saying, <verse eid="LUK 8:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 8:39"/><char style="wj">“Return to your house and relate how much God did for you.”</char> And he went away, proclaiming through the whole city how much Jesus did for him. <verse eid="LUK 8:39"/></para>

<para style="s">The Healing Touch of JesusMatthew 9:18–26</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 8:40"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 5:21–43</char></note>And on the return <char style="add">of</char> Jesus, the crowd welcomed Him, for they were all looking for Him. <verse eid="LUK 8:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 8:41"/>And behold, there was a man whose name <char style="add">was</char> Jairus, and this <char style="add">man</char> was a ruler of the synagogue. And having fallen at the feet of Jesus, he was imploring Him to enter into his house <verse eid="LUK 8:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 8:42"/>because he had an only daughter, about twelve years <char style="add">old</char>, and she was dying. And in His going, the crowds were pressing around Him. <verse eid="LUK 8:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 8:43"/>And a woman being with a flow of blood for twelve years, who, having spent all her living on physicians,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.43 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> NE and WH do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">having spent all her living on physicians</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> was not able to be healed by <char style="add">any</char>, <verse eid="LUK 8:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 8:44"/>having come behind, touched the fringe of His garment, and immediately her flow of blood stopped. <verse eid="LUK 8:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 8:45"/>And Jesus said, <char style="wj">“Who is the one having touched Me?”</char> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 8:45">But all denying, Peter<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.45 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and those who were with him</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> said, “Master, the crowds surround You and press in.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.45 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and You say, ‘Who touched Me?’</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 8:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 8:46"/>But Jesus said, <char style="wj">“Someone touched Me, for I knew power has gone out from Me.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 8:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 8:47"/>And the woman, having seen that she was not hidden, came trembling. And having fallen down before Him, she declared for what cause she touched Him before all the people, and how she was healed immediately. <verse eid="LUK 8:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 8:48"/>And He said to her, <char style="wj">“Daughter,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.48 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Take courage, daughter</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> your faith has saved you; go in peace.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 8:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 8:49"/>of Him still speaking, someone comes from the synagogue ruler,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.49 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the house of the synagogue ruler</char></note> saying, “Your daughter has died; trouble the Teacher no longer.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.49 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">do not trouble the Teacher</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 8:49"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 8:50"/>But Jesus, having heard, answered him, <char style="wj">“Do not be afraid; only believe, and she will be saved.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 8:50"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 8:51"/>And having come into the house, He did not allow anyone to enter with Him<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.51 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">with Him</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> except Peter and John and James,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.51 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Peter and James and John</char></note> and the father and the mother of the child. <verse eid="LUK 8:51"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 8:52"/>And all were weeping and mourning for her. But He said, <char style="wj">“Do not weep; for she did not die, but sleeps.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 8:52"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 8:53"/>And they were laughing at Him, knowing that she died. <verse eid="LUK 8:53"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 8:54"/>And He,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">8.54 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">sent them all outside, and</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> having taken hold of her hand, called out, saying, <char style="wj">“Child, arise!”</char> <verse eid="LUK 8:54"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 8:55"/>And her spirit returned, and she arose immediately; and He directed there be given to her to eat. <verse eid="LUK 8:55"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 8:56"/>And her parents were amazed, but He instructed them to tell no one what had happened. <verse eid="LUK 8:56"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 8"/>

<chapter number="9" style="c" sid="LUK 9"/>

<para style="s">The Ministry of the Twelve</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 9:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 6:7–13</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 10:5–15</char></note>And having called together the Twelve,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the twelve disciples</char></note> He gave to them power and authority over all the demons, and to heal diseases, <verse eid="LUK 9:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 9:2"/>and He sent them forth to proclaim the kingdom of God and to heal the sick. <verse eid="LUK 9:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 9:3"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Take nothing for the way, neither staff,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">staffs</char></note><char style="wj"> nor bag, nor bread, nor silver,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">money</char></note><char style="wj"> nor two</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TH and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">extra</char></note><char style="wj"> tunics apiece to have. </char><verse eid="LUK 9:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 9:4"/><char style="wj">And into whatever house you⁺ may enter, remain there and go out from there. </char><verse eid="LUK 9:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 9:5"/><char style="wj">And as many as may not receive you⁺, going out from that city, shake off the dust from your⁺ feet for a testimony against them.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 9:6"/>And going out, they were passing through the villages, proclaiming the gospel and healing everywhere. <verse eid="LUK 9:6"/></para>

<para style="s">Herod Tries to See JesusMatthew 14:1–12</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 9:7"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 6:14–29</char></note>Now Herod the tetrarch heard of all the things happening,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">by Him</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> and he was perplexed because it was said by some that John was risen out from the dead, <verse eid="LUK 9:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 9:8"/>and by some that Elijah appeared, and by others that a prophet of the ancients was risen. <verse eid="LUK 9:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 9:9"/>And Herod said, “John I beheaded, but who is this about whom I hear such things?” And he was seeking to see Him. <verse eid="LUK 9:9"/></para>

<para style="s">The Feeding of the Five ThousandMatthew 14:13–21</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 9:10"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 6:1–15</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 6:30–44</char></note>And having returned, the apostles related to Him how much they had done. And having taken them, He withdrew by Himself<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to a deserted place</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> to a city called Bethsaida. <verse eid="LUK 9:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 9:11"/>But the crowds, having known <char style="add">it</char>, followed Him; and having received them, He was speaking to them concerning the kingdom of God, and He was healing those having need of healing. <verse eid="LUK 9:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 9:12"/>And the day began to decline, and having come, the Twelve said to Him, “Dismiss the crowd so that, having gone to the surrounding villages and fields, they might lodge and might find provisions; for we are in a wilderness<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">desert</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">deserted</char></note> place here.” <verse eid="LUK 9:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 9:13"/>But He said to them, <char style="wj">“You⁺ give to them to eat.”</char> But they said, “There are unto us no more than five loaves and two fish, unless, having gone, we should buy food for all this people.” <verse eid="LUK 9:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 9:14"/>For they were about five thousand men. And He said to His disciples, <char style="wj">“Make them sit down in groups of about fifty.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">groups of fifty</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 9:15"/>And they did so and made all sit down. <verse eid="LUK 9:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 9:16"/>And having taken the five loaves and the two fish, having looked up to the heaven, He blessed and broke them, and He was giving <char style="add">them</char> to the disciples to set before the crowd. <verse eid="LUK 9:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 9:17"/>And they all ate and were satisfied; and that having superabounded to them was taken up, twelve hand-baskets of fragments. <verse eid="LUK 9:17"/></para>

<para style="s">Peter’s Confession of ChristMatthew 16:13–20</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 9:18"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 6:67–71</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 8:27–30</char></note>And it came to pass as He is praying alone <char style="add">that</char> the disciples were with Him, and He questioned them, saying, <char style="wj">“Who do the crowds say I am?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 9:19"/>And answering, they said, “John the Baptist; and others, Elijah; and others, that a prophet, one of the ancients, was risen.” <verse eid="LUK 9:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 9:20"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“But you⁺, who do you⁺ say I am?”</char> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 9:20">And answering, Peter said, “The Christ of God.” <verse eid="LUK 9:20"/></para>

<para style="s">Christ’s Passion ForetoldMatthew 16:21–23</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 9:21"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 8:31–33</char></note>And having warned them, He instructed <char style="add">them</char> to say this to no one, <verse eid="LUK 9:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 9:22"/>having said, <char style="wj">“It is necessary for the Son of Man to suffer many things, and to be rejected by the elders and chief priests and scribes, and to be killed, and on the third day to be raised.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> RP </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to rise again</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:22"/></para>

<para style="s">Take Up Your CrossMatthew 16:24–28</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 9:23"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 8:34–38</char></note>And He was saying to all, <char style="wj">“If anyone wants to come after Me, let him deny himself and take up his cross every day</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">every day</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> and follow Me. </char><verse eid="LUK 9:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 9:24"/><char style="wj">For whoever may want to save his life will lose it, but whoever may lose his life on account of Me, he will save it. </char><verse eid="LUK 9:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 9:25"/>For what is a man profited, having gained the whole world, and having destroyed or having suffered the loss of himself? <verse eid="LUK 9:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 9:26"/>For whoever may be ashamed of Me and My words, the Son of Man will be ashamed of him when He shall come in His glory and <char style="add">that</char> of the Father and of the holy angels. <verse eid="LUK 9:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 9:27"/>And I say to you⁺, truly, there are some of those standing here who shall not taste of death until they shall see the kingdom of God.” <verse eid="LUK 9:27"/></para>

<para style="s">The TransfigurationMatthew 17:1–13</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 9:28"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">2 Peter 1:16–21</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 9:1–13</char></note>And it came to pass about eight days after these words, and having taken with <char style="add">Him</char> Peter and John and James, <char style="add">that</char> He went up onto the mountain to pray. <verse eid="LUK 9:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 9:29"/>And it came to pass in His praying <char style="add">that</char> the appearance of His face <char style="add">was</char> different and His clothing white, flashing forth. <verse eid="LUK 9:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 9:30"/>And behold, two men were talking with Him, who were Moses and Elijah, <verse eid="LUK 9:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 9:31"/>who, having appeared in glory, were speaking of His departure, which He was about to fulfill in Jerusalem. <verse eid="LUK 9:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 9:32"/>And Peter and those with him were heavy with sleep; and having awoken fully, they saw His glory and the two men standing with Him. <verse eid="LUK 9:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 9:33"/>And it came to pass in their departing from Him <char style="add">that</char> Peter said to Jesus, “Master, it is good for us to be here; and let us make three tabernacles<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">three shelters</char></note>—one for You, and one for Moses, and one for Elijah,” not knowing what he is saying. <verse eid="LUK 9:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 9:34"/>And <char style="add">in</char> his saying these things, there was a cloud, and it was overshadowing them; and they feared in their entering into the cloud. <verse eid="LUK 9:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 9:35"/>And a voice came out of the cloud, saying, “This is My Son, whom I have chosen; listen to Him!” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">This is My beloved Son</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 17:5.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 9:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 9:36"/>And on the voice having come, Jesus was found alone. And they were silent, and they reported to no one in those days anything of what they have seen. <verse eid="LUK 9:36"/></para>

<para style="s">The Boy with an Evil SpiritMatthew 17:14–18</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 9:37"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 9:14–29</char></note>And it came to pass the next day, <char style="add">on</char> their having come down from the mountain, <char style="add">that</char> a great crowd met Him. <verse eid="LUK 9:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 9:38"/>And behold, a man from the crowd cried out, saying, “Teacher, I implore You to look upon my son, because he is the only begotten unto me. <verse eid="LUK 9:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 9:39"/>And behold, a spirit takes him, and suddenly he cries out, and it throws him into convulsions with foaming, and with difficulty it departs from him, crushing him. <verse eid="LUK 9:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 9:40"/>And I begged Your disciples that they might cast it out, and they were not able.” <verse eid="LUK 9:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 9:41"/>And answering, Jesus said, <char style="wj">“O unbelieving and perverse generation, until when will I be with you⁺ and bear with you⁺? Bring your son here.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 9:42"/>And <char style="add">in</char> his still coming near, the demon threw him down and threw <char style="add">him</char> into convulsions. And Jesus rebuked the unclean spirit, and He healed the boy and gave him back to his father. <verse eid="LUK 9:42"/></para>

<para style="s">The Second Prediction of the PassionMatthew 17:22–23</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 9:43"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 9:30–32</char></note>And all were astonished at the majesty of God. But <char style="add">of</char> all wondering at all that He was doing,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.43 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jesus was doing</char></note> He said to His disciples, <verse eid="LUK 9:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 9:44"/><char style="wj">“You⁺ let these words sink into your⁺ ears; for the Son of Man is about to be betrayed into the hands of men.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 9:45"/>But they were not knowing this saying, and it was veiled from them, so that they should not understand it. And they were afraid to ask Him about this saying. <verse eid="LUK 9:45"/></para>

<para style="s">The Greatest in the KingdomMatthew 18:1–5</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 9:46"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 9:33–41</char></note>And a reasoning entered among them, who might be the greater of them. <verse eid="LUK 9:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 9:47"/>But Jesus, having known<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.47 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jesus, seeing </char></note> the reasoning of their heart, having taken hold of a little child, set him beside Himself, <verse eid="LUK 9:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 9:48"/>and He said to them, <char style="wj">“Whoever shall receive this little child in My name receives Me; and whoever shall receive Me receives the One having sent Me. For the one being least among you⁺ all, he is</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.48 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">he will be</char></note><char style="wj"> great.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 9:49"/>And answering, John said, “Master, we saw someone casting out demons in Your name, and we were forbidding him because he does not follow with us.” <verse eid="LUK 9:49"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 9:50"/>And Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“Do not forbid it; for whoever is not against you⁺ is for you⁺.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.50 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">not against us is for us</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:50"/></para>

<para style="s">The Samaritans Reject Jesus</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 9:51"/>And it came to pass in the completing of the days of His being taken up that He steadfastly set the face to go to Jerusalem. <verse eid="LUK 9:51"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 9:52"/>And He sent messengers before His face, and having gone, they entered into a village of the Samaritans, so as to prepare for Him. <verse eid="LUK 9:52"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 9:53"/>And they did not receive Him, because His face was going to Jerusalem. <verse eid="LUK 9:53"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 9:54"/>And having seen <char style="add">it</char>, the disciples<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.54 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">His disciples</char></note> James and John said, “Lord, do You want <char style="add">that</char> we should call fire to come down from heaven<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.54 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">from heaven, as also Elijah did</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see 2 Kings 1:10–12.</char></note> and to consume them?” <verse eid="LUK 9:54"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 9:55"/>But having turned, He rebuked them,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.55 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and He said, “You do not know of what kind of spirit you are. </char><char style="fq" closed="false">56</char><char style="fq" closed="false">For the Son of Man did not come to destroy the lives of men, but to save.”</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 9:55"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 9:56"/>and they went to another village. <verse eid="LUK 9:56"/></para>

<para style="s">The Cost of DiscipleshipMatthew 8:18–22</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="57" style="v" sid="LUK 9:57"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 6:59–66</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Luke 14:25–33</char></note>And <char style="add">in</char> their going along the road, someone said to Him, “I will follow<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.57 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Lord, I will follow</char></note> You wherever You may go.” <verse eid="LUK 9:57"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="58" style="v" sid="LUK 9:58"/>And Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“The foxes have holes, and the birds of the sky nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere He might lay the head.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:58"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="59" style="v" sid="LUK 9:59"/>And He said to another, <char style="wj">“Follow Me.”</char> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 9:59">But he said, “Lord,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">9.59 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Lord</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> allow me first, having gone away, to bury my father.” <verse eid="LUK 9:59"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="60" style="v" sid="LUK 9:60"/>But He said to him, <char style="wj">“Leave the dead to bury their own dead; but you, having gone away, declare the kingdom of God.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:60"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="61" style="v" sid="LUK 9:61"/>And yet another said, “I will follow You, Lord; but first allow me to bid farewell to those at my home.” <verse eid="LUK 9:61"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="62" style="v" sid="LUK 9:62"/>But Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“No one having laid the hand upon the plow and looking to the things behind is fit for the kingdom of God.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 9:62"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 9"/>

<chapter number="10" style="c" sid="LUK 10"/>

<para style="s">Jesus Sends the Seventy-Two</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 10:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 9:35–38</char></note>Now after these things, the Lord also appointed seventy-two<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> NE, most BYZ, and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">seventy</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; also in verse 17</char></note> others and sent them in two <char style="add">by</char> two before His face into every city and place where He Himself was about to go. <verse eid="LUK 10:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 10:2"/>And<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Therefore</char></note> He was saying to them, <char style="wj">“The harvest indeed is much, but the workers few. Therefore implore the Lord of the harvest that He might put forth workers into His harvest. </char><verse eid="LUK 10:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 10:3"/>Go! Behold, I send you⁺ forth as lambs in the midst of wolves. <verse eid="LUK 10:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 10:4"/>Carry no purse, no bag, no sandals; and greet no one on the road. <verse eid="LUK 10:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 10:5"/>And into whatever house you⁺ might enter, first say, ‘Peace to this house.’ <verse eid="LUK 10:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 10:6"/>And if a son of peace is there, your⁺ peace will rest on him; but if not, it will return to you⁺. <verse eid="LUK 10:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 10:7"/>And remain in the same house, eating and drinking the things unto them; for the worker <char style="add">is</char> worthy of his wages. Do not move from house to house. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Leviticus 19:13 and Deuteronomy 24:14–15; cited in 1 Timothy 5:18.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 10:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 10:8"/>And into whatever city you⁺ may enter and they may receive you⁺, eat the things set before you⁺. <verse eid="LUK 10:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 10:9"/>And heal the ailing in it, and say to them, ‘The kingdom of God has drawn near to you⁺.’ <verse eid="LUK 10:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 10:10"/>But into whatever city you⁺ might enter and they may not receive you⁺, having gone out into its streets, say, <verse eid="LUK 10:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 10:11"/>‘Even the dust of your⁺ city having clung to our feet<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">having clung to us</char></note> we wipe off against you⁺; yet know this, that the kingdom of God has drawn near.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to you</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note>’ <verse eid="LUK 10:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 10:12"/>I say to you⁺ that for Sodom in that day it will be more bearable than for that city. <verse eid="LUK 10:12"/></para>

<para style="s">Woe to the UnrepentantMatthew 11:20–24</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 10:13"/>Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the miracles having happened in you⁺ had happened in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago, sitting in sackcloth and ashes. <verse eid="LUK 10:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 10:14"/>But it will be more bearable for Tyre and Sidon in the judgment than for you⁺. <verse eid="LUK 10:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 10:15"/>And you, Capernaum, to heaven will you be lifted up? To Hades you will be brought down!<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And you, Capernaum, who are lifted to heaven, will be brought down to Hades</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 10:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 10:16"/>The one hearing you⁺ hears Me, and the one setting you⁺ aside sets Me aside, and the one setting Me aside sets aside the One having sent Me.” <verse eid="LUK 10:16"/></para>

<para style="s">The Joyful Return</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 10:17"/>And the seventy-two returned with joy, saying, “Lord, even the demons are subject to us in Your name.” <verse eid="LUK 10:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 10:18"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“I was beholding Satan having fallen like lightning out of heaven. </char><verse eid="LUK 10:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 10:19"/><char style="wj">Behold, I have given</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">I am giving</char></note><char style="wj"> you⁺ the authority to tread upon serpents and scorpions, and on all the power of the enemy, and nothing will injure you⁺. </char><verse eid="LUK 10:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 10:20"/><char style="wj">But do not rejoice in this, that the spirits are subject to you⁺; but</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">but rather</char></note><char style="wj"> rejoice that your⁺ names have been written in the heavens.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 10:20"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus’ Prayer of ThanksgivingMatthew 11:25–30</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 10:21"/>In the same hour, He exulted in the Holy Spirit<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in the Spirit</char></note> and said, <char style="wj">“I acknowledge You, Father, Lord of heaven and of the earth, because You hid away these things from the wise and intelligent, and You revealed them to little children. Yes, Father, because of thus it was good pleasure before You. </char><verse eid="LUK 10:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 10:22"/>All things<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And turning to the disciples, He said, “All things</char></note> were delivered to Me by My Father. And no one knows who the Son is except the Father, and who the Father is except the Son and the one to whom the Son may resolve to reveal <char style="add">Him</char>.” <verse eid="LUK 10:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 10:23"/>And having turned to the disciples, He said in private, <char style="wj">“Blessed are the eyes seeing what you⁺ see. </char><verse eid="LUK 10:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 10:24"/><char style="wj">For I say to you⁺ that many prophets and kings desired to see what you⁺ see and did not see, and to hear what you⁺ hear and did not hear.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 10:24"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Good Samaritan</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 10:25"/>And behold, a certain lawyer rose up, testing Him, saying, “Teacher, having done what, will I inherit eternal life?” <verse eid="LUK 10:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 10:26"/>And He said to him, <char style="wj">“What has been written in the Law? How do you read it?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 10:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 10:27"/>And answering, he said, “‘You will love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your strength and with all your mind,’ and ‘your neighbor as yourself.’<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 6:5</char></note>” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Leviticus 19:18</char></note><verse eid="LUK 10:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 10:28"/>And He said to him, <char style="wj">“You answered correctly. Do this and you will live.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 10:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 10:29"/>But wanting to justify himself, he said to Jesus, “And who is my neighbor?” <verse eid="LUK 10:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 10:30"/>And Jesus, having taken it up, said, <char style="wj">“A certain man was going down from Jerusalem to Jericho and fell among robbers who, having both stripped him and having inflicted wounds, went away, having left him half dead. </char><verse eid="LUK 10:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 10:31"/>Now by chance a certain priest was going down on that road, and having seen him, he passed by on the opposite side. <verse eid="LUK 10:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 10:32"/>And likewise also a Levite, having come to the place and having seen, passed by on the opposite side. <verse eid="LUK 10:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 10:33"/>But a certain Samaritan, journeying, came to him, and having seen, he was moved with compassion; <verse eid="LUK 10:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 10:34"/>and having come, he bound up his wounds, pouring on oil and wine; and having put him on his own beast, he brought him to an inn and took care of him. <verse eid="LUK 10:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 10:35"/>And on the next day,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">when he departed</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> having taken out two denarii,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; see Matthew 20:2.</char></note> he gave <char style="add">them</char> to the innkeeper and said, ‘Take care of him, and whatever more you might expend, I, in my returning, will repay you.’ <verse eid="LUK 10:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 10:36"/>Which<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.36 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">So which</char></note> of these three seems to you to have been a neighbor of the one having fallen among the robbers?” <verse eid="LUK 10:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 10:37"/>And he said, “The one having done the mercy toward him.” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 10:37">Then Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“Go, and you do likewise.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 10:37"/></para>

<para style="s">Martha and Mary</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 10:38"/>Now in their going, He entered into a certain village; and a certain woman named Martha received Him into <char style="add">her</char> house. <verse eid="LUK 10:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 10:39"/>And she had a sister called Mary, who also, having sat down at the feet of the Lord,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.39 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of Jesus</char></note> was hearing His word. <verse eid="LUK 10:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 10:40"/>But Martha was distracted about much service; and having stood, she said, “Lord, is it not concerning to You that my sister left me alone to serve? Therefore speak to her so that she might help me!” <verse eid="LUK 10:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 10:41"/>And answering, the Lord said<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">10.41 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jesus said</char></note> to her, <char style="wj">“Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things; </char><verse eid="LUK 10:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 10:42"/><char style="wj">but little is necessary, or one thing; for Mary chose the good portion, which will not be taken away from her.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 10:42"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 10"/>

<chapter number="11" style="c" sid="LUK 11"/>

<para style="s">The Lord’s Prayer</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 11:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 6:5–15</char></note>And it came to pass in His being in a certain place praying, <char style="add">that</char> when He ceased, one of His disciples said to Him, “Lord, teach us to pray, as John also taught his disciples.” <verse eid="LUK 11:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 11:2"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“When you⁺ pray, say: </char></para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 11:2">‘Father,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Our Father in the heavens</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 6:9.</char></note> hallowed be Your name. </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 11:2">Your kingdom come.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Your will be done, as in heaven, so also on earth</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 6:10.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 11:2"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 11:3"/>Give us each day our necessary bread. <verse eid="LUK 11:3"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 11:4"/>And forgive us our sins, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 11:4">for we ourselves also forgive everyone indebted to us. </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 11:4">And lead us not into temptation.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">but deliver us from evil</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 6:13.</char></note>’” <verse eid="LUK 11:4"/></para>

<para style="s">Ask, Seek, KnockMatthew 7:7–12</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 11:5"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Who of you⁺ will have a friend and will go to him at midnight and would say to him, ‘Friend, lend me three loaves, </char><verse eid="LUK 11:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 11:6"/><char style="wj">since a friend of mine</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">a friend</char></note><char style="wj"> came to me from a journey, and I have nothing that I will set before him.’? </char><verse eid="LUK 11:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 11:7"/>And he, answering from within, may say, ‘Do not cause me trouble; already the door has been shut, and my children are with me in the bed. I am not able, having risen up, to give to you.’ <verse eid="LUK 11:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 11:8"/>I say to you⁺, even if he will not give to him, having risen up because of being his friend, yet because of his persistence, having risen, he will give him as much as he needs. <verse eid="LUK 11:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 11:9"/>And I say to you⁺: Ask, and it will be given to you⁺; seek, and you⁺ will find; knock, and it will be opened to you⁺. <verse eid="LUK 11:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 11:10"/>For everyone asking receives; and the one seeking finds; and to the one knocking, it will be opened. <verse eid="LUK 11:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 11:11"/>And which father of you⁺, <char style="add">if</char> the son will ask for a fish,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">if his son will ask for bread, will give him a stone? Or also, if he will ask for a fish</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 7:9–10.</char></note> instead of even a fish, will give to him a serpent? <verse eid="LUK 11:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 11:12"/>Or also, <char style="add">if</char> he will ask for an egg, will give to him a scorpion? <verse eid="LUK 11:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 11:13"/>Therefore if you⁺, being evil, know to give good gifts to your⁺ children, how much more will the Father who <char style="add">is</char> from heaven give the Holy Spirit<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">a good spirit</char></note> to those asking Him!” <verse eid="LUK 11:13"/></para>

<para style="s">A House DividedMatthew 12:22–30</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 11:14"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 3:20–27</char></note>And He was casting out a demon, and it was mute; and it came to pass, <char style="add">on</char> the demon having gone out, <char style="add">that</char> the mute <char style="add">man</char> spoke. And the crowds marveled. <verse eid="LUK 11:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 11:15"/>And some of them said, “By Beelzebul,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Beezeboul</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Vulgate </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Beelzebub</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; also in verses 18 and 19</char></note> the ruler of the demons, He casts out the demons.” <verse eid="LUK 11:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 11:16"/>And others, testing, were seeking from Him a sign out of heaven. <verse eid="LUK 11:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 11:17"/>But He, knowing their thoughts, said to them, <char style="wj">“Every kingdom having been divided against itself is brought to desolation; and a house against a house falls. </char><verse eid="LUK 11:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 11:18"/><char style="wj">And if Satan also was divided against himself, how will his kingdom stand? For you⁺ say I cast out the demons by Beelzebul. </char><verse eid="LUK 11:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 11:19"/><char style="wj">And if I cast out the demons by Beelzebul, by whom do your⁺</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">will your</char></note><char style="wj"> sons cast them out? Because of this, they will be your⁺ judges. </char><verse eid="LUK 11:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 11:20"/><char style="wj">But if I cast out the demons by the finger of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you⁺. </char><verse eid="LUK 11:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 11:21"/>When the strong <char style="add">man</char>, being armed, might guard his house, his possessions are in peace. <verse eid="LUK 11:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 11:22"/>But when one stronger than he, having come upon <char style="add">him</char>, might overcome him, he takes away his full armor in which he had trusted, and he divides his plunder. <verse eid="LUK 11:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 11:23"/>The one not being with Me is against Me, and the one not gathering with Me scatters. <verse eid="LUK 11:23"/></para>

<para style="s">An Unclean Spirit ReturnsMatthew 12:43–45</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 11:24"/>When the unclean spirit may go out from the man, it passes through waterless places, seeking rest. And not finding <char style="add">it</char>, then it says, ‘I will return to my house from which I came out.’ <verse eid="LUK 11:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 11:25"/>And having come, it finds <char style="add">it</char> swept<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">unoccupied, swept,</char></note> and put in order. <verse eid="LUK 11:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 11:26"/>Then it goes and takes along seven other spirits more evil than itself, and having entered, they dwell there; and the last of that man becomes worse than the first.” <verse eid="LUK 11:26"/></para>

<para style="s">True Blessedness</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 11:27"/>And it came to pass, in His saying these things, <char style="add">that</char> a certain woman out of the crowd, having lifted up <char style="add">her</char> voice, said to Him, “Blessed <char style="add">is</char> the womb having borne You and the breasts at which You nursed!” <verse eid="LUK 11:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 11:28"/>But He said, <char style="wj">“Rather, blessed are those hearing the word of God and keeping it.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 11:28"/></para>

<para style="s">The Sign of JonahJonah 3:1–10</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 11:29"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 12:38–42</char></note>Now the crowds being pressed around together, He began to say, <char style="wj">“This generation is an evil generation; it seeks a sign, and no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the prophet</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> </char><verse eid="LUK 11:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 11:30"/><char style="wj">For just as Jonah was a sign to the Ninevites, so also the Son of Man will be</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.30 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">will be a sign</char></note><char style="wj"> to this generation. </char><verse eid="LUK 11:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 11:31"/>The Queen of the South will rise up in the judgment with the men of this generation and will condemn them; for she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and behold, One greater<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">something greater</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; also in verse 32</char></note> than Solomon <char style="add">is</char> here. <verse eid="LUK 11:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 11:32"/>The men of Nineveh will stand up in the judgment with this generation and will condemn it; for they repented at the preaching of Jonah, and behold, One greater than Jonah <char style="add">is</char> here. <verse eid="LUK 11:32"/></para>

<para style="s">The Lamp of the BodyMatthew 6:22–24</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 11:33"/>No one having lit a lamp puts <char style="add">it</char> in secret, nor under the basket,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TH does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">nor under the basket</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> but on the lampstand, so that those entering may see the light. <verse eid="LUK 11:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 11:34"/>The lamp of the body is your eye.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the eye</char></note> When your eye may be sound,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">When your eyes are good</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Proverbs 22:9.</char></note> also your whole body is full of light; but when it may be evil,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">when they are bad</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Proverbs 23:6 and Proverbs 28:22.</char></note> your body <char style="add">is</char> also full of darkness. <verse eid="LUK 11:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 11:35"/>Take heed, therefore, <char style="add">that</char> the light that <char style="add">is</char> in you is not darkness. <verse eid="LUK 11:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 11:36"/>If, therefore, your whole body <char style="add">is</char> full of light, not having any part full of darkness, the whole will be full of light, as when the lamp shining may shine on you.” <verse eid="LUK 11:36"/></para>

<para style="s">Woes to Pharisees and Experts in the LawMatthew 23:1–36</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 11:37"/>And in <char style="add">His</char> speaking, a Pharisee asked Him that He might dine with him. And having entered, He reclined. <verse eid="LUK 11:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 11:38"/>But the Pharisee, having seen, marveled that He did not wash first before the dinner. <verse eid="LUK 11:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 11:39"/>But the Lord said to him, <char style="wj">“Now you⁺, the Pharisees, you⁺ cleanse the outside of the cup and of the dish, but your⁺ inside is full of plundering and of wickedness. </char><verse eid="LUK 11:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 11:40"/><char style="wj">Fools! Did not the One having made the outside also make the inside? </char><verse eid="LUK 11:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 11:41"/><char style="wj">But give as alms the things being within, and behold, all things are clean for you⁺. </char><verse eid="LUK 11:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 11:42"/>But woe to you⁺, the Pharisees, because you⁺ pay tithes of mint and rue and every herb, and you⁺ pass by the judgment and the love of God. But it was necessary to do these things, and those not to neglect. <verse eid="LUK 11:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 11:43"/>Woe to you⁺, the Pharisees, because you⁺ love the first seat in the synagogues and the greetings in the marketplaces. <verse eid="LUK 11:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 11:44"/>Woe to you⁺,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.44 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites,</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> because you⁺ are like unmarked tombs, and the men walking above do not know.” <verse eid="LUK 11:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 11:45"/>But answering, one of the lawyers says to Him, “Teacher, saying these things, You insult us also.” <verse eid="LUK 11:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 11:46"/>But He said, <char style="wj">“Woe also to you⁺, the lawyers, because you⁺ burden men with burdens hard to bear, and you⁺ yourselves do not touch the burdens with one of your⁺ fingers. </char><verse eid="LUK 11:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 11:47"/>Woe to you⁺, because you⁺ build the tombs of the prophets, but your⁺ fathers killed them. <verse eid="LUK 11:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 11:48"/>So you⁺ are witnesses and consent<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.48 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">So you bear witnesses and consent</char></note> to the works of your⁺ fathers, because they indeed killed them, and you⁺ build the tombs. <verse eid="LUK 11:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 11:49"/>Because of this also the wisdom of God said, ‘I will send to them prophets and apostles, and some of them they will kill and persecute,’ <verse eid="LUK 11:49"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 11:50"/>so that the blood of all the prophets poured out from the foundation of the world might be required from this generation, <verse eid="LUK 11:50"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 11:51"/>from the blood of Abel to the blood of Zechariah, the one having perished between the altar and the house.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.51 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the sanctuary</char></note> Yes, I say to you⁺, it will be required from this generation. <verse eid="LUK 11:51"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 11:52"/>Woe to you⁺, the lawyers, because you⁺ took away the key of knowledge. You yourselves did not enter, and you⁺ hindered those entering.” <verse eid="LUK 11:52"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 11:53"/><char style="add">On</char> His having gone out from there,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.53 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">In His speaking these things to them,</char></note> the scribes and the Pharisees began terribly to press upon <char style="add">Him</char> and to make Him speak about many things, <verse eid="LUK 11:53"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 11:54"/>lying in wait to catch Him in something<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.54 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">lying in wait for Him and seeking to catch Him in something</char></note> out of His mouth.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">11.54 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">so that they might accuse Him</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 11:54"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 11"/>

<chapter number="12" style="c" sid="LUK 12"/>

<para style="s">The Leaven of the Pharisees</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 12:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 8:14–21</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 16:5–12</char></note>In these <char style="add">times</char>, the myriads of the crowd having been gathered together so as to trample upon one another, He began to say to His disciples first, <char style="wj">“Take heed to yourselves of the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 12:2"/><char style="wj">And nothing is concealed that will not be revealed, and hidden that will not be known. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 12:3"/><char style="wj">Instead, whatever you⁺ said in the darkness will be heard in the light, and what you⁺ spoke to the ear in the inner rooms will be proclaimed upon the housetops. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:3"/></para>

<para style="s">Fear God AloneMatthew 10:26–31</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 12:4"/>And I say to you⁺, My friends, you⁺ should not be afraid of those killing the body and after these things not having anything more to do. <verse eid="LUK 12:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 12:5"/>But I will show you⁺ whom you⁺ should fear: Fear the One having authority, after the killing, to cast into Gehenna.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">hell</char></note> Yes, I say to you⁺, fear Him! <verse eid="LUK 12:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 12:6"/>Are not five sparrows sold for two assaria?<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">two pennies</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; an assarion was a Roman copper coin worth about 1/16 of a denarius.</char></note> And not one of them is forgotten before God. <verse eid="LUK 12:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 12:7"/>But even the hairs of your⁺ head have all been numbered. Do not be afraid; you⁺ are more valuable than many sparrows. <verse eid="LUK 12:7"/></para>

<para style="s">Confessing ChristMatthew 10:32–33</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 12:8"/>And I say to you⁺, everyone who will confess in Me before men, the Son of Man also will confess in him before the angels of God. <verse eid="LUK 12:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 12:9"/>But the one having denied Me before men will be denied before the angels of God. <verse eid="LUK 12:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 12:10"/>And everyone who will speak a word to the Son of Man, it will be forgiven him; but the one having blasphemed to the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. <verse eid="LUK 12:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 12:11"/>And when they bring you⁺ in before the synagogues and the rulers and the authorities, do not be anxious about how or what you⁺ may reply in defense, or what you⁺ may say. <verse eid="LUK 12:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 12:12"/>For the Holy Spirit will teach you⁺ in the same hour what it is necessary to say.” <verse eid="LUK 12:12"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Rich Fool</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 12:13"/>And one from the crowd said to Him, “Teacher, tell my brother to divide with me the inheritance.” <verse eid="LUK 12:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 12:14"/>And He said to him, <char style="wj">“Man, who appointed Me a judge or divider over you⁺?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 12:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 12:15"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Beware, and keep yourselves from all covetousness,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">from covetousness</char></note><char style="wj"> because not to anyone is his life in the abundance of that which he possesses.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 12:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 12:16"/>And He spoke a parable to them, saying, <char style="wj">“The ground of a certain rich man brought forth abundantly. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 12:17"/><char style="wj">And he was reasoning within himself, saying, ‘What shall I do, because I have nowhere I will store up my fruits?’ </char><verse eid="LUK 12:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 12:18"/><char style="wj">And he said, ‘This I will do: I will tear down my barns and will build larger ones, and I will gather there all my grain</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">fruits</char></note><char style="wj"> and goods. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 12:19"/><char style="wj">And I will say to my soul, “Soul, you have many good things laid up for many years; take your rest, eat, drink, be merry.”’ </char><verse eid="LUK 12:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 12:20"/>And God said to him, ‘Fool! This night, your soul is required of you; but what you prepared—to whom will <char style="add">it</char> be?’ <verse eid="LUK 12:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 12:21"/>So <char style="add">is</char> the one treasuring up for himself and not being rich toward God.” <verse eid="LUK 12:21"/></para>

<para style="s">Do Not WorryMatthew 6:25–34</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 12:22"/>And He said to His disciples,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the disciples</char></note> <char style="wj">“Because of this I say to you⁺, do not be anxious for life, what you⁺ may eat, nor for your⁺ body, what you⁺ might put on. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 12:23"/><char style="wj">For life is more than</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Life is more than</char></note><char style="wj"> food, and the body than clothing. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 12:24"/><char style="wj">Consider the ravens, that they do not sow nor reap; unto them there is not a storehouse nor a barn—and God feeds them. How much more valuable are you⁺ than the birds!</char> <verse eid="LUK 12:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 12:25"/>And who of you⁺, being anxious, is able to add to his lifespan a cubit?<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to his height a cubit</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; a cubit in reference to time is a small unit of time; in reference to length it is approximately 18 inches or 45 centimeters.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 12:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 12:26"/>If, then, you⁺ are not even able <char style="add">to do</char> the least, why are you⁺ anxious about the rest? <verse eid="LUK 12:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 12:27"/>Consider the lilies, how they grow: They do not labor, nor do they spin.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> NE and Tischendorf </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Consider the lilies: they do not spin, nor do they weave.</char></note> Yet<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Yet</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> I say to you⁺, not even Solomon in all his glory was clothed like one of these. <verse eid="LUK 12:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 12:28"/>But if God thus clothes the grass in the field, being <char style="add">here</char> today and tomorrow being thrown into the furnace, how much more you⁺, O little of faith! <verse eid="LUK 12:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 12:29"/>And you⁺, do not seek what you⁺ might eat and what you⁺ might drink, and do not be in suspense; <verse eid="LUK 12:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 12:30"/>for all the nations of the world seek after these things, and your⁺ Father knows that you⁺ have need of these. <verse eid="LUK 12:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 12:31"/>But seek His kingdom, and these things will be added to you⁺. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">But seek the kingdom of God, and all these things</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 6:33.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 12:31"/></para>

<para style="s">Treasures in HeavenMatthew 6:19–21</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 12:32"/>Do not fear, little flock, because your⁺ Father was well pleased to give you⁺ the kingdom. <verse eid="LUK 12:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 12:33"/>Sell your⁺ possessions and give alms; make for yourselves purses not growing old, an unfailing treasure in the heavens, where thief does not draw near, nor does moth destroy. <verse eid="LUK 12:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 12:34"/>For where your⁺ treasure is, there also your⁺ heart will be. <verse eid="LUK 12:34"/></para>

<para style="s">Readiness at Any HourMatthew 24:36–51</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 12:35"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 13:32–37</char></note>Let your⁺ loins be girded around and the lamps burning, <verse eid="LUK 12:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 12:36"/>and you⁺ <char style="add">be</char> like men waiting for their master when he shall return from the wedding feasts, that, having come and having knocked, immediately they might open to him. <verse eid="LUK 12:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 12:37"/>Blessed <char style="add">are</char> those servants whom, having come, the master will find watching. Truly I say to you⁺ that he will gird himself and will make them recline, and having come up, will serve them. <verse eid="LUK 12:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 12:38"/>And if he should come in the second, and if in the third watch,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> That is, between nine at night and three in the morning</char></note> and he finds thus, blessed are those. <verse eid="LUK 12:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 12:39"/>But know this, that if the master of the house had known in what hour the thief comes,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.39 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">he would have kept watch, and</char></note> he would not have permitted his house to be broken into. <verse eid="LUK 12:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 12:40"/>And you⁺,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.40 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Therefore you also</char></note> be ready, because the Son of Man comes in that hour you⁺ do not think.” <verse eid="LUK 12:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 12:41"/>And Peter said, “Lord, do You say this parable to us, or also to all?” <verse eid="LUK 12:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 12:42"/>And the Lord said, <char style="wj">“Who then is the faithful, wise manager, whom the master will set over the care of his, to give in season the grain measure? </char><verse eid="LUK 12:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 12:43"/><char style="wj">Blessed is that servant whom his master, having come, will find doing so. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 12:44"/><char style="wj">Truly I say to you⁺ that he will set him over all his possessions. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 12:45"/>But if that servant should say in his heart, ‘My master delays to come,’ and should begin to beat the menservants and the maidservants, and to eat and to drink and to get drunk, <verse eid="LUK 12:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 12:46"/>the master of that servant will come in a day that he does not expect and in an hour that he does not know, and he will cut him in two and will appoint his portion with the unbelievers. <verse eid="LUK 12:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 12:47"/>Now that servant, the one having known the will of his master and not having prepared nor having done according to his will, he will be beaten with many. <verse eid="LUK 12:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 12:48"/>But the one not having known but having done <char style="add">things</char> worthy of stripes will be beaten with few. And of everyone to whom much was given, much will be required; and from him to whom much was committed, more will they ask of him. <verse eid="LUK 12:48"/></para>

<para style="s">Not Peace but DivisionMicah 7:1–6</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 12:49"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 10:34–39</char></note>I came to cast fire upon<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.49 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to bring fire to</char></note> the earth, and how I wish it were already kindled! <verse eid="LUK 12:49"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 12:50"/>But I have a baptism with which to be baptized, and how distressed I am until it should be accomplished! <verse eid="LUK 12:50"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 12:51"/>Do you⁺ think that I came to grant peace on the earth? No, I say to you⁺, but rather division. <verse eid="LUK 12:51"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 12:52"/>For from now there will be five in one house divided, three against two and two against three. <verse eid="LUK 12:52"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 12:53"/>They will be divided, father against son and son against father, mother against the daughter and daughter against the mother, mother-in-law against her daughter-in-law and daughter-in-law against the mother-in-law.” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.53 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Micah 7:6.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 12:53"/></para>

<para style="s">Interpreting the Present TimeMatthew 16:1–4</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 12:54"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 8:11–13</char></note>And He was also saying to the crowds, <char style="wj">“When you⁺ see the cloud rising up in</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.54 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">from</char></note><char style="wj"> the west, immediately you⁺ say, ‘A shower is coming,’ and so it happens. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:54"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 12:55"/><char style="wj">And when a south wind is blowing, you⁺ say, ‘There will be heat,’ and it happens. </char><verse eid="LUK 12:55"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 12:56"/><char style="wj">Hypocrites! The face of the earth and of the sky</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.56 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC, F35, and Scrivener TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of the sky and of the earth</char></note><char style="wj"> you⁺ know to discern, but how do you⁺ not know to discern</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.56 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">why can you not interpret</char></note><char style="wj"> this time? </char><verse eid="LUK 12:56"/></para>

<para style="s">Reconciling with an AdversaryMatthew 5:21–26</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="57" style="v" sid="LUK 12:57"/>And why do you⁺ not even judge for<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.57 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">from</char></note> yourselves what <char style="add">is</char> right? <verse eid="LUK 12:57"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="58" style="v" sid="LUK 12:58"/>For as you are going with your adversary before a ruler, give effort to be set free from him in the way, lest ever he should drag you away to the judge, and the judge will deliver you to the officer, and the officer will cast you into prison. <verse eid="LUK 12:58"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="59" style="v" sid="LUK 12:59"/>I say to you, you shall never come out from there until you should pay back even the last lepton.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">12.59 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">penny</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; a lepton was a Jewish coin of bronze or copper worth about 1/128 of a denarius.</char></note>” <verse eid="LUK 12:59"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 12"/>

<chapter number="13" style="c" sid="LUK 13"/>

<para style="s">A Call to Repentance</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 13:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Zephaniah 2:1–3</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Amos 5:4–15</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Joel 1:13–20</char></note>Now at the same time, some were present, reporting to Him about the Galileans whose blood Pilate mixed with their sacrifices. <verse eid="LUK 13:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 13:2"/>And answering, He said to them,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Jesus said to them</char></note> <char style="wj">“Do you⁺ think that these Galileans were sinners beyond all the Galileans, because they have suffered these things? </char><verse eid="LUK 13:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 13:3"/><char style="wj">No, I say to you⁺; but unless you⁺ repent, you⁺ will all likewise perish. </char><verse eid="LUK 13:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 13:4"/><char style="wj">Or those eighteen on whom the tower in Siloam fell, and it killed them: Do you⁺ think that these were debtors beyond all the men dwelling in Jerusalem? </char><verse eid="LUK 13:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 13:5"/><char style="wj">No, I say to you⁺; but unless you⁺ repent, you⁺ will all likewise perish.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 13:5"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Barren Fig TreeIsaiah 5:1–7</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 13:6"/>And He was speaking this parable: <char style="wj">“A certain man had a fig tree planted in his vineyard, and he came seeking fruit on it, and he did not find any. </char><verse eid="LUK 13:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 13:7"/><char style="wj">And he said to the vinedresser, ‘Behold, for these three years I come seeking fruit on this fig tree and do not find it. Therefore cut it down!</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL, NE, WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Cut it down!</char></note><char style="wj"> Why does it also render the soil useless?’ </char><verse eid="LUK 13:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 13:8"/>And answering, he says to him, ‘Sir, leave it this year also, until I may dig around it and put <char style="add">in</char> manure, <verse eid="LUK 13:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 13:9"/>and if indeed it should make fruit in the <char style="add">time</char>, so be it. But if not, you will cut it down.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and if indeed it may bear fruit—and if not so, you will cut it down</char></note>’” <verse eid="LUK 13:9"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Heals a Disabled Woman</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 13:10"/>And He was teaching in one of the synagogues on the Sabbaths. <verse eid="LUK 13:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 13:11"/>And behold, a woman having a spirit of infirmity eighteen years, and she was bent over and not able to lift herself up to the full. <verse eid="LUK 13:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 13:12"/>And having seen her, Jesus called <char style="add">her</char> near and said to her, <char style="wj">“Woman, you have been freed from your infirmity.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 13:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 13:13"/>And He laid the hands on her, and immediately she was set upright and was glorifying God. <verse eid="LUK 13:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 13:14"/>And answering, the synagogue ruler, indignant because Jesus healed on the Sabbath, was saying to the crowd, “There are six days in which it is necessary to work. Therefore, coming, be healed in these, and not on the day of the Sabbath.” <verse eid="LUK 13:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 13:15"/>But the Lord answered him and said, <char style="wj">“Hypocrites!</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR and GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">“You hypocrite!”</char></note><char style="wj"> Does not each one of you⁺ loose his ox or donkey from the stall on the Sabbath, and having led it away, give to it to drink? </char><verse eid="LUK 13:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 13:16"/><char style="wj">And this one, being a daughter of Abraham whom Satan bound, behold, eighteen years, was it not necessary for her to be loosed from this bond on the day of the Sabbath?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 13:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 13:17"/>And <char style="add">on</char> His saying these things, all those opposed to Him were ashamed; and all the crowd was rejoicing at all the glorious things that were being done by Him. <verse eid="LUK 13:17"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Mustard SeedMatthew 13:31–32</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 13:18"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 4:30–34</char></note>And He was saying, <char style="wj">“What is the kingdom of God like? And to what will I liken it? </char><verse eid="LUK 13:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 13:19"/><char style="wj">It is like a grain of mustard that a man, having taken, cast into his garden; and it grew and became a tree,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">a great tree</char></note><char style="wj"> and the birds of the sky lodged in its branches.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 13:19"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the LeavenMatthew 13:33</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 13:20"/>And again, He said, <char style="wj">“To what shall I liken the kingdom of God? </char><verse eid="LUK 13:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 13:21"/><char style="wj">It is like leaven that a woman, having taken it, hid in three measures of flour, until it all was leavened.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 13:21"/></para>

<para style="s">The Narrow DoorMatthew 7:13–14</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 13:22"/>And He was going through by towns and villages, teaching and making progress toward Jerusalem. <verse eid="LUK 13:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 13:23"/>And someone said to Him, “Lord, <char style="add">are</char> those being saved few?” And He said to them, <verse eid="LUK 13:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 13:24"/><char style="wj">“Strive to enter through the narrow door,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">narrow gate</char></note><char style="wj"> because many, I say to you⁺, will seek to enter and will not be able. </char><verse eid="LUK 13:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 13:25"/><char style="wj">From that time the master of the house shall have risen up and shall have shut the door, then you⁺ shall begin to stand outside and to knock at the door, saying, ‘Lord,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Lord, Lord</char></note><char style="wj"> open to us.’ </char></para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 13:25">And answering, he will say to you⁺, ‘I do not know from where you⁺ are.’ <verse eid="LUK 13:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 13:26"/>Then you⁺ will begin to say, ‘We ate in your presence, and we drank, and you taught in our streets.’ <verse eid="LUK 13:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 13:27"/>And he will say, ‘<char style="add">I am</char> saying to you⁺, I do not know from where you⁺ are; depart from me, all <char style="add">you⁺</char> workers of unrighteousness.’ <verse eid="LUK 13:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 13:28"/>There will be weeping and gnashing of the teeth when you⁺ shall see Abraham and Isaac and Jacob and all the prophets in the kingdom of God, but you⁺ are being cast outside. <verse eid="LUK 13:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 13:29"/>And they will come from east and west and from north and south and will recline in the kingdom of God. <verse eid="LUK 13:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 13:30"/>And behold, there are last who will be first, and there are first who will be last.” <verse eid="LUK 13:30"/></para>

<para style="s">Lament over JerusalemMatthew 23:37–39</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 13:31"/>In the same hour,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">On that very day</char></note> some Pharisees came near, saying to Him, “Go out and proceed from here, because Herod wants to kill You.” <verse eid="LUK 13:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 13:32"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Having gone, say to this fox, ‘Behold, I cast out demons and I complete cures today and tomorrow, and the third day, I am accomplished.’ </char><verse eid="LUK 13:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 13:33"/><char style="wj">But it is necessary for Me to proceed today and tomorrow and the following day, because it is not permissible for a prophet to perish outside of Jerusalem. </char><verse eid="LUK 13:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 13:34"/>O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, killing the prophets and stoning those sent to her, how often I willed to gather your children, the way that a hen <char style="add">does</char> her brood under the wings, and you⁺ were not willing. <verse eid="LUK 13:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 13:35"/>Behold, your⁺ house is left to you⁺. And I say to you⁺, you⁺ shall not see Me until <char style="add">it</char> will come when you⁺ say, ‘Blessed is the One coming in the name of the Lord.’” <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">13.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 118:26</char></note><verse eid="LUK 13:35"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 13"/>

<chapter number="14" style="c" sid="LUK 14"/>

<para style="s">Jesus Heals a Man with Dropsy</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 14:1"/>And it came to pass, in His going into the house of one of the rulers of the Pharisees on a Sabbath to eat bread, that they were watching Him. <verse eid="LUK 14:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 14:2"/>And behold, there was a certain man with dropsy before Him. <verse eid="LUK 14:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 14:3"/>And answering, Jesus spoke to the lawyers and to the Pharisees, saying, <char style="wj">“Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath or not?</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">or not</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <verse eid="LUK 14:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 14:4"/>But they were silent. And having taken hold <char style="add">of him</char>, He healed him and let <char style="add">him</char> go. <verse eid="LUK 14:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 14:5"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Which of you⁺ whose son</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">donkey</char></note><char style="wj"> or ox will fall into a well will not also immediately pull him up on the day of the Sabbath?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 14:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 14:6"/>And they were not able to reply to these things. <verse eid="LUK 14:6"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Guests</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 14:7"/>And He was speaking a parable to those invited, remarking how they were choosing out the first places, saying to them, <verse eid="LUK 14:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 14:8"/><char style="wj">“When you are invited by anyone to the wedding feasts, do not recline in the first place, lest ever one more honorable than you may have been invited by him. </char><verse eid="LUK 14:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 14:9"/><char style="wj">And the one having invited you and him, having come, will say to you, ‘Give to this one the place,’ and then you should begin with shame to take the last place. </char><verse eid="LUK 14:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 14:10"/>But when you are invited, having gone, recline in the last place, so that when the one having invited you might come, he will say to you, ‘Friend, come up higher.’<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Proverbs 25:7.</char></note> Then glory will be to you before all those reclining<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">before those reclining</char></note> <char style="add">with</char> you. <verse eid="LUK 14:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 14:11"/>For everyone exalting himself will be humbled, and the one humbling himself will be exalted.” <verse eid="LUK 14:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 14:12"/>And also He was saying to the one having invited Him, <char style="wj">“When you may make a dinner or a supper, do not call your friends, nor your brothers, nor your relatives nor rich neighbors, lest ever they should also invite you in return, and repayment be made to you. </char><verse eid="LUK 14:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 14:13"/><char style="wj">But when you may make a feast, call the poor, the crippled, the lame, the blind, </char><verse eid="LUK 14:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 14:14"/><char style="wj">and you will be blessed, because they have nothing to repay you. For it will be repaid to you in the resurrection of the righteous.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 14:14"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the BanquetMatthew 22:1–14</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 14:15"/>And one of those reclining with <char style="add">Him</char>, having heard these things, said to Him, “Blessed <char style="add">is</char> anyone who will eat bread<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">who will eat at the feast</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> </char></note> in the kingdom of God.” <verse eid="LUK 14:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 14:16"/>But He said to him, <char style="wj">“A certain man was preparing a great supper, and he called many. </char><verse eid="LUK 14:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 14:17"/><char style="wj">And he sent his servant at the hour of the supper to say to those having been called, ‘Come, because now it is ready.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">everything is now ready</char></note><char style="wj">’ </char><verse eid="LUK 14:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 14:18"/>And all with one <char style="add">voice</char> began to excuse themselves. The first said to him, ‘I bought a field, and I have need <char style="add">of</char> going out to see<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">I need to go out and see</char></note> it. I ask you, have me excused.’ <verse eid="LUK 14:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 14:19"/>And another said, ‘I bought five yoke of oxen, and I am going to examine them. I request of you, have me excused.’ <verse eid="LUK 14:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 14:20"/>And another said, ‘I married a wife, and because of this I am not able to come.’ <verse eid="LUK 14:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 14:21"/>And having come, the servant reported<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">The servant returned and reported</char></note> these things to his master. Then, having become angry, the master of the house said to his servant, ‘Go out quickly into the streets and lanes of the city, and bring in here the poor and crippled and blind and lame.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">lame and blind</char></note>’ <verse eid="LUK 14:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 14:22"/>And the servant said, ‘Lord, what you commanded has been done,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">it has been done as you commanded</char></note> and there is still room.’ <verse eid="LUK 14:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 14:23"/>And the master said to the servant, ‘Go out into the roads and hedges and compel <char style="add">them</char> to enter, so that my house may be filled. <verse eid="LUK 14:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 14:24"/>For I say to you⁺ that not one of those men having been called will taste my supper.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> RP includes </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For many are called, but few are chosen</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note>’” <verse eid="LUK 14:24"/></para>

<para style="s">The Cost of DiscipleshipMatthew 8:18–22</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 14:25"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 6:59–66</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Luke 9:57–62</char></note>And great crowds were going with Him, and having turned, He said to them, <verse eid="LUK 14:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 14:26"/><char style="wj">“If anyone comes to Me and does not hate his father and mother and wife and children and brothers and sisters, and yes, even his life, he is not able to be My disciple. </char><verse eid="LUK 14:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 14:27"/><char style="wj">Whoever does not carry his cross and come after Me is not able to be My disciple. </char><verse eid="LUK 14:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 14:28"/>For which of you⁺, wanting to build a tower, does not first, having sat down, count the cost, whether he has the <char style="add">things</char> for <char style="add">its</char> completion, <verse eid="LUK 14:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 14:29"/>lest ever, <char style="add">of him</char> having laid its foundation and not being able to finish, all seeing <char style="add">it</char> may begin to mock him, <verse eid="LUK 14:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 14:30"/>saying, ‘This man began to build and was not able to finish’? <verse eid="LUK 14:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 14:31"/>Or what king proceeding to engage with another king in war will not,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">14.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">does not</char></note> having sat down, first take counsel, whether he is able with ten thousand to meet the one coming against him with twenty thousand? <verse eid="LUK 14:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 14:32"/>And if not, <char style="add">in</char> his being still far off, having sent a delegation, he asks for peace. <verse eid="LUK 14:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 14:33"/>So therefore, every one of you⁺ who does not take leave of all that he himself possesses is not able to be My disciple. <verse eid="LUK 14:33"/></para>

<para style="s">Good SaltMatthew 5:13–16</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 14:34"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 9:49–50</char></note>Therefore the salt <char style="add">is</char> good, but if even the salt should become tasteless, with what will it be seasoned? <verse eid="LUK 14:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 14:35"/>It is fit neither for soil nor for manure; they cast it out. </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 14:35">The one having ears to hear, let him hear.” <verse eid="LUK 14:35"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 14"/>

<chapter number="15" style="c" sid="LUK 15"/>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Lost Sheep</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 15:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 18:10–14</char></note>Now all the tax collectors and the sinners were drawing near to Him to hear Him. <verse eid="LUK 15:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 15:2"/>And both the Pharisees and the scribes were grumbling, saying, “This <char style="add">man</char> receives sinners and eats with them.” <verse eid="LUK 15:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 15:3"/>And He spoke to them this parable, saying, <verse eid="LUK 15:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 15:4"/><char style="wj">“What man of you⁺, having a hundred sheep and having lost one of them, does not leave the ninety-nine in the wilderness</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">desert</char></note><char style="wj"> and go after the lost one until he should find it? </char><verse eid="LUK 15:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 15:5"/><char style="wj">And having found it, he lays it on his shoulders, rejoicing. </char><verse eid="LUK 15:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 15:6"/><char style="wj">And having come to the house, he calls together the friends and the neighbors, saying to them, ‘Rejoice with me, because I found my sheep, the lost one!’ </char><verse eid="LUK 15:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 15:7"/><char style="wj">I say to you⁺ that thus there will be joy in heaven over one sinner repenting, rather than over ninety-nine righteous ones who have no need of repentance. </char><verse eid="LUK 15:7"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Lost Coin</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 15:8"/>Or what woman having ten drachmas,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">ten silver coins</char><char style="ft" closed="false">, each worth about a day’s wages</char></note> if she should lose one drachma, does not light a lamp and sweep the house and seek carefully until she should find it? <verse eid="LUK 15:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 15:9"/>And having found <char style="add">it</char>, she calls together the friends and neighbors, saying, ‘Rejoice with me, for I found the drachma that I lost.’ <verse eid="LUK 15:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 15:10"/>Thus, I say to you⁺, there is joy before the angels of God over one sinner repenting.” <verse eid="LUK 15:10"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Prodigal SonDeuteronomy 21:18–21</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 15:11"/>And He said, <char style="wj">“A certain man had two sons. </char><verse eid="LUK 15:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 15:12"/><char style="wj">And the younger of them said to the father, ‘Father, give to me the portion of the substance falling to me.’ And he divided the livelihood between them. </char><verse eid="LUK 15:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 15:13"/>And after not many days, the younger son, having gathered together all, went away into a far country, and there he scattered his substance living prodigally. <verse eid="LUK 15:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 15:14"/>But <char style="add">on</char> his having spent all, there was a strong famine throughout that country, and he began to lack. <verse eid="LUK 15:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 15:15"/>And having gone, he joined himself to one of the citizens of that country, and he sent him into his fields to feed pigs. <verse eid="LUK 15:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 15:16"/>And he was desiring to fill his belly<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> NA, TH, and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">desiring to be filled</char></note> from the pods that the pigs were eating, and no one was giving to him. <verse eid="LUK 15:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 15:17"/>But having come to himself, he was saying, ‘How many hired men of my father have an abundance of loaves, but here I am<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">but I am</char></note> perishing with hunger?<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">starving to death</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 15:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 15:18"/>Having risen up, I will go to my father and I will say to him, “Father, I sinned against heaven and before you; <verse eid="LUK 15:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 15:19"/>no longer am I worthy to be called your son. Make me like one of your hired men.”’ <verse eid="LUK 15:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 15:20"/>And having risen up, he went to his father. But <char style="add">on</char> his still being distant, his father saw him and was moved with compassion, and having run, he fell upon his neck and kissed him. <verse eid="LUK 15:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 15:21"/>And the son said to him, ‘Father, I sinned against heaven and before you; no longer am I worthy to be called your son.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH includes </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Make me like one of your hired men</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see verse 19.</char></note>’ <verse eid="LUK 15:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 15:22"/>And the father said to his servants, ‘Quickly<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Quickly</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> bring out the best robe and clothe him, and give a ring for his hand and sandals for his feet; <verse eid="LUK 15:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 15:23"/>and having brought the fattened calf, kill <char style="add">it</char>, and having eaten, let us be merry, <verse eid="LUK 15:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 15:24"/>because this son of mine was dead and lived again! He was lost and was found!’ And they began to be merry. <verse eid="LUK 15:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 15:25"/>And his elder son was in the field; and as, coming, he drew near to the house, he heard music and dancing. <verse eid="LUK 15:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 15:26"/>And having summoned one of the<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">15.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Stephanus TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">his</char></note> servants, he was inquiring what these things might be. <verse eid="LUK 15:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 15:27"/>And he said to him, ‘Your brother has come, and your father killed the fattened calf, because he received him back in good health.’ <verse eid="LUK 15:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 15:28"/>But he was angry and was not willing to go in. And his father, having gone, was imploring him. <verse eid="LUK 15:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 15:29"/>But answering, he said to his father, ‘Behold, so many years I serve you, and never did I transgress a commandment of yours, and never did you give to me a young goat so that I might be merry with my friends. <verse eid="LUK 15:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 15:30"/>But when this son of yours came, the one having devoured your living with prostitutes, you killed the fattened calf for him!’ <verse eid="LUK 15:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 15:31"/>And he said to him, ‘Child, you are always with me, and all that <char style="add">is</char> mine is yours⁺. <verse eid="LUK 15:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 15:32"/>But it was fitting to be merry and to rejoice, because this brother of yours was dead and lived again; and he was lost and was found.’” <verse eid="LUK 15:32"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 15"/>

<chapter number="16" style="c" sid="LUK 16"/>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Shrewd Manager</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 16:1"/>Now He was also saying to the disciples,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">His disciples</char></note> <char style="wj">“There was a certain rich man who had a manager, and this one was accused to him as wasting his possessions. </char><verse eid="LUK 16:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 16:2"/><char style="wj">And having called him, he said to him, ‘What is this I hear about you? Give the account of your management, for you are not able to manage any longer.’ </char><verse eid="LUK 16:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 16:3"/>And the manager said in himself, ‘What shall I do, because my master is taking away the management from me? I have no strength to dig; I am ashamed to beg. <verse eid="LUK 16:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 16:4"/>I have known what I shall do, so that when I shall have been removed from the management, they might receive me into their houses.’ <verse eid="LUK 16:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 16:5"/>And having summoned each one of the debtors of his master, he was saying to the first, ‘How much do you owe to my master?’ <verse eid="LUK 16:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 16:6"/>And he said, ‘A hundred baths of oil.’<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">‘A hundred measures of olive oil’</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; that is, approximately 870 gallons or 3,300 liters</char></note> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 16:6">And he said to him, ‘Take your bill, and having sat down quickly, write fifty.’ <verse eid="LUK 16:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 16:7"/>Then he said to another, ‘And how much do you owe?’ </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 16:7">And he said, ‘A hundred cors of wheat.’<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">‘A hundred measures of wheat’</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; that is, approximately 1,000 bushels or 35,000 liters (probably about 30 tons or 27 metric tons of wheat)</char></note> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 16:7">He says to him, ‘Take your bill and write eighty.’ <verse eid="LUK 16:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 16:8"/>And the master praised the unrighteous manager because he acted shrewdly, because the sons of this age are more shrewd than the sons of the light in their own generation. <verse eid="LUK 16:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 16:9"/>And I say to you⁺, make friends for yourselves by the mammon of unrighteousness, so that when it fails,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">when you fail</char></note> they might receive you⁺ into the eternal dwellings. <verse eid="LUK 16:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 16:10"/>The one faithful in very little is also faithful in much, and the one unrighteous in very little is also unrighteous in much. <verse eid="LUK 16:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 16:11"/>If, therefore, you⁺ were not faithful in the unrighteous mammon, who will entrust to you⁺ the true? <verse eid="LUK 16:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 16:12"/>And if you⁺ were not faithful in that which <char style="add">is</char> of another, who will give to you⁺ that which <char style="add">is</char> your own? <verse eid="LUK 16:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 16:13"/>No servant is able to serve two masters. For either he will hate the one and he will love the other, or he will hold to the one and he will despise the other. You⁺ are not able to serve God and mammon.” <verse eid="LUK 16:13"/></para>

<para style="s">The Law and the Prophets</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 16:14"/>Now the Pharisees, being lovers of money, were hearing all these things, and they were ridiculing Him. <verse eid="LUK 16:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 16:15"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“You⁺ are the ones justifying themselves before men, but God knows your⁺ hearts, because that which is high among men is an abomination before God. </char><verse eid="LUK 16:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 16:16"/>The Law and the Prophets <char style="add">were</char> until John. From then, the kingdom of God is proclaimed <char style="add">as</char> good news, and everyone presses into it.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">everyone is urged to enter into it</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 16:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 16:17"/>But it is easier for the heaven and the earth to pass away than one stroke of a letter of the Law to fall. <verse eid="LUK 16:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 16:18"/>Everyone sending away his wife and marrying another commits adultery. And the one marrying her sent away from a husband, he commits adultery. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">anyone marrying</char></note><verse eid="LUK 16:18"/></para>

<para style="s">The Rich Man and LazarusJohn 5:39–47</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 16:19"/>And there was a certain rich man, and he was clothed in purple and fine linen, being merry in splendor every day. <verse eid="LUK 16:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 16:20"/>And a certain poor man named Lazarus was laid at his gate, being full of sores <verse eid="LUK 16:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 16:21"/>and desiring to be fed from that falling<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">from the crumbs that fell</char></note> from the table of the rich man; but even the dogs, coming, were licking his sores. <verse eid="LUK 16:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 16:22"/>And it came to pass <char style="add">that</char> the poor man died, and he was carried away by the angels into the bosom of Abraham.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to Abraham's side</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; similarly in verse 23</char></note> And the rich man also died and was buried. <verse eid="LUK 16:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 16:23"/>And in Hades, having lifted up his eyes, being in torment, he sees Abraham from afar and Lazarus in his bosom. <verse eid="LUK 16:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 16:24"/>And having cried out, he said, ‘Father Abraham, have mercy on me and send Lazarus so that he might dip the tip of his finger in water and cool my tongue, because I am suffering in this flame.’ <verse eid="LUK 16:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 16:25"/>But Abraham said, ‘Child, remember that you fully received your good <char style="add">things</char> in your life, and Lazarus likewise the evil <char style="add">things</char>. But now he is comforted here,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">16.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">here</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> and you are suffering. <verse eid="LUK 16:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 16:26"/>And besides all these things, a great chasm has been fixed between us and you⁺, so that those desiring to go over from here to you⁺ are not able, nor may they cross over from there to us.’ <verse eid="LUK 16:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 16:27"/>And he said, ‘Then I ask you, father, that you might send him to the house of my father— <verse eid="LUK 16:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 16:28"/>for I have five brothers—that he might warn them so that they might not also come to this place of torment.’ <verse eid="LUK 16:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 16:29"/>But Abraham says, ‘They have Moses and the prophets; let them hear them.’ <verse eid="LUK 16:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 16:30"/>But he said, ‘No, father Abraham, but if one from the dead should go to them, they will repent.’ <verse eid="LUK 16:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 16:31"/>But he said to him, ‘If they do not hear Moses and the prophets, not even will they be persuaded if one should rise out from the dead.’” <verse eid="LUK 16:31"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 16"/>

<chapter number="17" style="c" sid="LUK 17"/>

<para style="s">Temptations and Trespasses</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 17:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 9:42–48</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 18:6–9</char></note>And He said to His disciples,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the disciples</char></note> <char style="wj">“It is impossible for the stumbling blocks not to come, but woe to him through whom they come! </char><verse eid="LUK 17:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 17:2"/><char style="wj">It is better for him if a millstone</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">a large millstone</char></note><char style="wj"> is put around his neck and he is thrown into the sea than that he might cause to stumble one of these little ones. </char><verse eid="LUK 17:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 17:3"/>Take heed to yourselves: If your brother should sin,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">against you</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> rebuke him; and if he should repent, forgive him. <verse eid="LUK 17:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 17:4"/>And if he should sin against you seven times in the day, and seven times<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in that day</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> he should return to you, saying, ‘I repent,’ you will forgive him.” <verse eid="LUK 17:4"/></para>

<para style="s">The Power of FaithMatthew 17:19–20</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 17:5"/>And the apostles said to the Lord, “Add to our faith!” <verse eid="LUK 17:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 17:6"/>And the Lord said, <char style="wj">“If you⁺ have faith as a grain of mustard, you⁺ would have said to this mulberry tree, ‘Be uprooted and be planted in the sea,’ and it would have obeyed you⁺. </char><verse eid="LUK 17:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 17:7"/>And which of you⁺ having a servant plowing or shepherding, the one having come in out of the field, will say to him,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.7 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">will say</char></note> ‘Having come, immediately recline’? <verse eid="LUK 17:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 17:8"/>But will he not say to him, ‘Prepare what I may eat, and having girded yourself about, serve me while I eat and drink; and after these things you will eat and drink’? <verse eid="LUK 17:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 17:9"/>Does he have favor to the servant because he did the <char style="add">things</char> having been directed?<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.9 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">I think not</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 17:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 17:10"/>Thus also you⁺, when you⁺ may have done all the <char style="add">things</char> having been directed you⁺, say, ‘We are unworthy servants; we have done that which we were obligated to do.’” <verse eid="LUK 17:10"/></para>

<para style="s">The Ten Lepers2 Kings 5:1–14</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 17:11"/>And it came to pass, in <char style="add">His</char> going to Jerusalem, that He was passing through the midst of Samaria and Galilee. <verse eid="LUK 17:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 17:12"/>And <char style="add">on</char> His entering into a certain village, ten leprous men<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">lepers</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; a </char><char style="fq" closed="false">leper</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> was one afflicted with a skin disease. See Leviticus 13.</char></note> met Him, who stood afar off. <verse eid="LUK 17:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 17:13"/>And they lifted up <char style="add">their</char> voice, saying, “Jesus, Master, have mercy on us!” <verse eid="LUK 17:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 17:14"/>And having seen, He said to them, <char style="wj">“Having gone, show yourselves to the priests.”</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Leviticus 14:1–32.</char></note> And it came to pass, in their going, <char style="add">that</char> they were cleansed. <verse eid="LUK 17:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 17:15"/>And one of them, having seen that he was healed, turned back, glorifying God with a loud voice, <verse eid="LUK 17:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 17:16"/>and he fell on <char style="add">his</char> face at His feet, giving thanks to Him. And he was a Samaritan. <verse eid="LUK 17:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 17:17"/>And having answered, Jesus said, <char style="wj">“Were not the ten cleansed? But where are the nine? </char><verse eid="LUK 17:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 17:18"/><char style="wj">Was there found none having returned to give glory to God except this foreigner?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 17:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 17:19"/>And He said to him, <char style="wj">“Having risen up, go; your faith has saved you!”</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">has made you well</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 17:19"/></para>

<para style="s">The Coming of the KingdomGenesis 19:24–29</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 17:20"/>And having been questioned by the Pharisees <char style="add">as to</char> when the kingdom of God is coming, He answered them and said, <char style="wj">“The kingdom of God does not come with observation, </char><verse eid="LUK 17:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 17:21"/><char style="wj">nor will they say, ‘Behold, here,’ or, ‘There.’ For behold, the kingdom of God is in your⁺ midst.”</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">within you</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">within your grasp</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 17:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 17:22"/>And He said to the disciples, <char style="wj">“The days will come when you⁺ will desire to see one of the days of the Son of Man, and you⁺ will not see it. </char><verse eid="LUK 17:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 17:23"/><char style="wj">And they will say to you⁺, ‘Behold, there,’ or, ‘Behold, here.’ you⁺ should not go away, nor should you⁺ pursue. </char><verse eid="LUK 17:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 17:24"/><char style="wj">For just as the lightning shines, flashing from the one end of the sky to the other end of the sky, so will be the Son of Man in His day.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in His day</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> </char><verse eid="LUK 17:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 17:25"/><char style="wj">But first it is necessary for Him to suffer many things and to be rejected by this generation. </char><verse eid="LUK 17:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 17:26"/>And just as it came to pass in the days of Noah, so also will it be in the days of the Son of Man: <verse eid="LUK 17:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 17:27"/>They were eating, they were drinking, they were marrying, they were being given in marriage, until that day Noah entered into the ark, and the flood came and destroyed them all. <verse eid="LUK 17:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 17:28"/>Likewise, just as it came to pass in the days of Lot: They were eating, they were drinking, they were buying, they were selling, they were planting, they were building, <verse eid="LUK 17:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 17:29"/>and in that day Lot went out from Sodom, it rained fire and sulfur from heaven and destroyed all. <verse eid="LUK 17:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 17:30"/>According to the same <char style="add">things</char> it will be in that day the Son of Man is revealed. <verse eid="LUK 17:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 17:31"/>In that day, the one <char style="add">who</char> will be on the housetop, and his goods in the house, let him not come down to take them away; and likewise the one in the field, let him not return to the <char style="add">things</char> behind. <verse eid="LUK 17:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 17:32"/>Remember the wife of Lot! <verse eid="LUK 17:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 17:33"/>Whoever may seek to keep<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to save</char></note> his life will lose it; but whoever may lose <char style="add">it</char> will preserve it. <verse eid="LUK 17:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 17:34"/>I say to you⁺, in that night there will be two upon one bed: The one will be taken, and the other will be left. <verse eid="LUK 17:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 17:35"/>There will be two <char style="add">women</char> grinding at the same <char style="add">place</char>: The one will be taken, and the other will be left.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">17.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR includes </char><char style="fq" closed="false">36</char><char style="fq" closed="false">There will be two in the field: The one will be taken and the other will be left</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 24:40.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 17:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 17:37"/>And answering, they say to Him, “Where, Lord?” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 17:37">And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Where the body is, there also the vultures will be gathered together.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 17:37"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 17"/>

<chapter number="18" style="c" sid="LUK 18"/>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Persistent Widow</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 18:1"/>And He was speaking a parable to them that it is necessary for them<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">18.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And He was also speaking to them a parable that it is necessary</char></note> always to pray and not to grow fainthearted, <verse eid="LUK 18:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 18:2"/>saying, <char style="wj">“There was a certain judge in a certain city, not fearing God and not respecting man. </char><verse eid="LUK 18:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 18:3"/><char style="wj">And there was a widow in that city, and she was coming to him, saying, ‘Avenge me from my adversary.’ </char><verse eid="LUK 18:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 18:4"/>And he was not willing for a time, but after these things he said in himself, ‘Even if I do not fear God nor respect man, <verse eid="LUK 18:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 18:5"/>yet because this widow causes me trouble, I will avenge her, so that coming until the end, she does not exhaust me.’” <verse eid="LUK 18:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 18:6"/>And the Lord said, <char style="wj">“Hear what the unrighteous judge says. </char><verse eid="LUK 18:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 18:7"/><char style="wj">And shall not God execute the avenging of His chosen—the ones crying out to Him day and night—and be deferring in regard to them? </char><verse eid="LUK 18:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 18:8"/><char style="wj">I say to you⁺ that He will execute their avenging in quickness. Nevertheless, the Son of Man having come, will He find the faith on the earth?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 18:8"/></para>

<para style="s">The Pharisee and the Tax Collector</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 18:9"/>And He also spoke this parable to some trusting in themselves that they are righteous, and despising others: <verse eid="LUK 18:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 18:10"/><char style="wj">“Two men went up into the temple to pray, the one a Pharisee and the other a tax collector. </char><verse eid="LUK 18:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 18:11"/><char style="wj">The Pharisee, having stood, was praying toward himself</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">18.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">stood by himself and prayed</char></note><char style="wj"> thus: ‘God, I thank You that I am not like the rest of the men—swindlers, unrighteous, adulterers—or even like this tax collector. </char><verse eid="LUK 18:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 18:12"/><char style="wj">I fast twice in the week; I tithe all things, as many as I gain.’ </char><verse eid="LUK 18:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 18:13"/>But the tax collector, standing afar off, was not even willing to lift up the eyes to heaven, but was striking his breast, saying, ‘God, be merciful to me, the sinner!’ <verse eid="LUK 18:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 18:14"/>I say to you⁺, this one went down to his house justified, rather than that one. For everyone exalting himself will be humbled, but the one humbling himself will be exalted.” <verse eid="LUK 18:14"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Blesses the ChildrenMatthew 19:13–15</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 18:15"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 10:13–16</char></note>And they were bringing the infants also to Him so that He might touch them; but having seen, the disciples were rebuking them. <verse eid="LUK 18:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 18:16"/>But Jesus summoned them, saying, <char style="wj">“Permit the little children to come to Me, and do not forbid them; for of the such is the kingdom of God. </char><verse eid="LUK 18:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 18:17"/><char style="wj">Truly I say to you⁺, whoever shall not receive the kingdom of God as a little child, he shall never enter into it.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 18:17"/></para>

<para style="s">The Rich Young RulerMatthew 19:16–30</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 18:18"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 10:17–31</char></note>And a certain ruler asked Him, saying, “Good Teacher, having done what will I inherit eternal life?” <verse eid="LUK 18:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 18:19"/>But Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“Why do you call Me good? No one is good except One—God. </char><verse eid="LUK 18:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 18:20"/><char style="wj">You know the commandments: ‘You shall not commit adultery, you shall not murder, you shall not steal, you shall not bear false witness, honor your father and mother.’”</char> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">18.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Exodus 20:12–16; Deuteronomy 5:16–20</char></note><verse eid="LUK 18:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 18:21"/>And he said, “All these I kept from my youth.” <verse eid="LUK 18:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 18:22"/>And having heard, Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“Still one thing to you is lacking: Sell all, as much as you have, and distribute to the poor, and you will have treasure in the heavens; and come, follow Me.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 18:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 18:23"/>But having heard these things, he became very sorrowful; for he was exceedingly rich. <verse eid="LUK 18:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 18:24"/>And Jesus, having seen him, having become sorrowful,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">18.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">having seen the man's sadness</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; SBL, NE, and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">having seen him</char></note> said, <char style="wj">“How difficultly those having riches enter into the kingdom of God! </char><verse eid="LUK 18:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 18:25"/><char style="wj">For it is easier for a camel to enter through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 18:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 18:26"/>And those having heard said, “And who is able to be saved?” <verse eid="LUK 18:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 18:27"/>And He said, <char style="wj">“The things impossible with men are possible with God.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 18:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 18:28"/>And Peter said, “Behold, we, having left <char style="add">our</char> own,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">18.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">all we had</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">left all</char></note> followed You.” <verse eid="LUK 18:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 18:29"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Truly I say to you⁺ that there is no one who left house or wife or brothers or parents</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">18.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">house or parents or brothers or wife</char></note><char style="wj"> or children for the sake of the kingdom of God </char><verse eid="LUK 18:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 18:30"/><char style="wj">who shall not receive back manifold more in this time, and in the age that is coming, eternal life.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 18:30"/></para>

<para style="s">The Third Prediction of the PassionMatthew 20:17–19</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 18:31"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 10:32–34</char></note>And having taken aside the Twelve, He said to them, <char style="wj">“Behold, we are going up to Jerusalem, and all things having been written by the prophets about the Son of Man will be accomplished. </char><verse eid="LUK 18:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 18:32"/><char style="wj">For He will be betrayed to the Gentiles and will be mocked and will be insulted and will be spit upon. </char><verse eid="LUK 18:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 18:33"/><char style="wj">And having flogged Him, they will kill Him; and on the third day He will rise again.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 18:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 18:34"/>And they understood none of these things, and this saying was hidden from them, and they did not know the things being spoken. <verse eid="LUK 18:34"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Heals a Blind BeggarMatthew 20:29–34</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 18:35"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 10:46–52</char></note>And it came to pass, in His drawing near to Jericho, <char style="add">that</char> a certain blind <char style="add">man</char> was sitting beside the road, begging. <verse eid="LUK 18:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 18:36"/>And having heard a crowd going by, he was asking what this might be. <verse eid="LUK 18:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 18:37"/>And they told him, “Jesus of Nazareth is passing by.” <verse eid="LUK 18:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 18:38"/>And he called out, saying, “Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!” <verse eid="LUK 18:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 18:39"/>And those going before were rebuking him, that he should be silent. But much more he was crying out, “Son of David, have mercy on me!” <verse eid="LUK 18:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 18:40"/>And having stood, Jesus commanded him to be brought to Him. And <char style="add">on</char> his having drawn near, He questioned him, <verse eid="LUK 18:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 18:41"/><char style="wj">“What do you desire that I may do for you?”</char> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 18:41">And he said, “Lord, that I may receive sight.” <verse eid="LUK 18:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 18:42"/>And Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“Receive your sight! Your faith has saved you.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 18:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 18:43"/>And immediately he received his sight, and he was following Him, glorifying God. And all the people, having seen <char style="add">it</char>, gave praise to God. <verse eid="LUK 18:43"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 18"/>

<chapter number="19" style="c" sid="LUK 19"/>

<para style="s">Jesus and Zacchaeus</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 19:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Numbers 5:5–10</char></note>And having entered, He was passing through Jericho. <verse eid="LUK 19:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 19:2"/>And behold, a man called by the name Zacchaeus, and he was a chief tax collector, and he <char style="add">was</char> rich. <verse eid="LUK 19:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 19:3"/>And he was seeking to see Jesus, who He is. And he was not able on account of the crowd, because he was small in stature. <verse eid="LUK 19:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 19:4"/>And having run ahead to the front, he went up on a sycamore-fig tree so that he might see Him, for He was about to pass through that <char style="add">way</char>. <verse eid="LUK 19:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 19:5"/>And as He came upon the place, having looked up, Jesus<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And as He came to that place, Jesus looked up and saw him, and</char></note> said to him, <char style="wj">“Zacchaeus, having hurried, come down, for it is necessary for Me to stay in your house today.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 19:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 19:6"/>And having hurried, he came down and received Him, rejoicing. <verse eid="LUK 19:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 19:7"/>And having seen <char style="add">it</char>, they were all grumbling, saying, “He entered to lodge with a sinful man.” <verse eid="LUK 19:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 19:8"/>But having stood, Zacchaeus said to the Lord, “Behold, half of my possessions, Lord, I give to the poor; and if I defrauded anything of anyone, I repay <char style="add">it</char> fourfold.” <verse eid="LUK 19:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 19:9"/>And Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“Today salvation came to this house, because he also is a son of Abraham. </char><verse eid="LUK 19:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 19:10"/><char style="wj">For the Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 19:10"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Ten MinasMatthew 25:14–30</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 19:11"/>Now <char style="add">in</char> their hearing these things, having proceeded, He spoke a parable because of His being near Jerusalem and their thinking that the kingdom of God is about to appear immediately. <verse eid="LUK 19:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 19:12"/>Therefore He said, <char style="wj">“A certain man of noble birth went to a far country to receive for himself a kingdom and to return. </char><verse eid="LUK 19:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 19:13"/><char style="wj">And having called ten of his servants, he gave to them ten minas</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> That is, he gave each servant one mina. A mina was most likely a silver coin worth a hundred drachmas, that is, about a hundred days’ wages.</char></note><char style="wj"> and said to them, ‘Do business until I come.’ </char><verse eid="LUK 19:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 19:14"/>But his citizens hated him and sent a delegation after him, saying, ‘We are not willing <char style="add">for</char> this <char style="add">man</char> to reign over us.’ <verse eid="LUK 19:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 19:15"/>And it came to pass on his returning, having received the kingdom, that he directed these servants to whom he had given the money to be called to him, so that he might know what each had gained by doing business. <verse eid="LUK 19:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 19:16"/>And the first came up, saying, ‘Lord, your mina produced ten minas.’ <verse eid="LUK 19:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 19:17"/>And he said to him, ‘Well done, good servant! Because you were faithful in very little, be having authority over ten cities.’ <verse eid="LUK 19:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 19:18"/>And the second came, saying, ‘Your mina, master, made five minas.’ <verse eid="LUK 19:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 19:19"/>And he said also to this one, ‘And you, be over five cities.’ <verse eid="LUK 19:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 19:20"/>And the other came, saying, ‘Master, behold your mina, which I had lying away in a soudario.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> That is, a piece of cloth or a handkerchief</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 19:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 19:21"/>For I was afraid of you, because you are a harsh man. You take up what you did not lay down, and you reap what you did not sow.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC includes </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and you gather where you scattered no seed</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note>’ <verse eid="LUK 19:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 19:22"/>He says to him, ‘Out of your mouth I will judge you, evil servant. You knew that I am a harsh man, taking up what I did not lay down and reaping what I did not sow?<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC includes </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and gathering where I scattered no seed</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 19:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 19:23"/>And why did you not give my money to the bank, and I, having come, might have collected it with interest?’ <verse eid="LUK 19:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 19:24"/>And to those standing by he said, ‘Take from him the mina and give <char style="add">it</char> to the one having the ten minas.’ <verse eid="LUK 19:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 19:25"/>And they said to him, ‘Lord, he has ten minas!’ <verse eid="LUK 19:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 19:26"/>I say to you⁺ that to everyone having, it will be given; but from the one not having, even that which he has will be taken away.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.26 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">from him</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 19:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 19:27"/>However, these enemies of mine, those not having been willing <char style="add">for</char> me to reign over them, bring <char style="add">them</char> here and slay them before me.’” <verse eid="LUK 19:27"/></para>

<para style="s">The Triumphal EntryZechariah 9:9–13</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 19:28"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 12:12–19</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 11:1–11</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 21:1–11</char></note>And having said these things, He was going on ahead, going up to Jerusalem. <verse eid="LUK 19:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 19:29"/>And it came to pass as He drew near to Bethphage<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Most MT </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Bethsphage</char></note> and Bethany, toward the mount called Olivet,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.29 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> That is, </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Mount of Olives</char></note> <char style="add">that</char> He sent two of the disciples, <verse eid="LUK 19:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 19:30"/>saying, <char style="wj">“Go into the village ahead, in which, entering, you⁺ will find a colt tied, on which no one of men ever sat; and having loosed it, bring it. </char><verse eid="LUK 19:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 19:31"/><char style="wj">And if anyone may ask you⁺, ‘Why are you⁺ loosing it?’ thus you⁺ will say, ‘Because the Lord has need of it.’”</char> <verse eid="LUK 19:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 19:32"/>And having gone away, those sent found <char style="add">it</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.32 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">found the colt standing there</char></note> just as He said to them. <verse eid="LUK 19:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 19:33"/>And <char style="add">on</char> their loosing the colt, its masters said to them, “Why are you⁺ loosing the colt?” <verse eid="LUK 19:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 19:34"/>And they said, “The Lord has need of it.” <verse eid="LUK 19:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 19:35"/>And they led it to Jesus, and having cast their garments on the colt, they put Jesus on <char style="add">it</char>. <verse eid="LUK 19:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 19:36"/>And <char style="add">in</char> His going, they were spreading their garments in the road. <verse eid="LUK 19:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 19:37"/>And <char style="add">in</char> His drawing near, already at the descent of the Mount of Olives, the whole multitude of the disciples, rejoicing, began to praise God in a loud voice for all the mighty works that they had seen, <verse eid="LUK 19:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 19:38"/>saying: </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 19:38">“Blessed is the coming King in the name of the Lord!” </para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 19:38">“Peace in heaven and glory in the highest!”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 118:26</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Psalm 148:1.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 19:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 19:39"/>And some of the Pharisees from the crowd said to Him, “Teacher, rebuke Your disciples!” <verse eid="LUK 19:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 19:40"/>And answering, He said, <char style="wj">“I say to you⁺ that if these will be silent, the stones will cry out.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 19:40"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Weeps over JerusalemIsaiah 29:1–16</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 19:41"/>And as He drew near, having seen the city, He wept over it, <verse eid="LUK 19:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 19:42"/>saying, <char style="wj">“If you knew in this day, even you, the things for peace! But now they were hidden from your eyes. </char><verse eid="LUK 19:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 19:43"/><char style="wj">For the days will come upon you, and your enemies will cast around you a barricade, and they will surround you and will hem you in on every side. </char><verse eid="LUK 19:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 19:44"/><char style="wj">And they will level you to the ground, and your children in you, and they will not leave a stone upon a stone in you, because you did not know the season of your visitation.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.44 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">your visitation from God</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <verse eid="LUK 19:44"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Cleanses the TempleMatthew 21:12–17</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 19:45"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 2:12–25</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 11:15–19</char></note>And having entered into the temple,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.45 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the temple courts</char></note> He began to cast out those selling,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.45 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in it, and buying</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 19:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 19:46"/>saying to them, <char style="wj">“It has been written: ‘And My house will be a house of prayer.’ But you⁺ made it ‘a den of robbers.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.46 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 56:7</char></note><char style="wj">’”</char> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">19.46 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Jeremiah 7:11</char></note><verse eid="LUK 19:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 19:47"/>And He was teaching every day in the temple. But the chief priests and the scribes and the foremost of the people were seeking to destroy Him. <verse eid="LUK 19:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 19:48"/>And they were not finding what they might do; for all the people were hanging on Him, hearing. <verse eid="LUK 19:48"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 19"/>

<chapter number="20" style="c" sid="LUK 20"/>

<para style="s">Jesus’ Authority Challenged</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 20:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 11:27–33</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 21:23–27</char></note>And it came to pass on one of the days, <char style="add">in</char> His teaching the people in the temple<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the temple courts</char></note> and proclaiming the gospel, <char style="add">that</char> the chief priests<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the priests</char></note> and the scribes came up with the elders, <verse eid="LUK 20:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 20:2"/>and they spoke to Him, saying, “Tell us by what authority You do these things, or who is the one having given You this authority?” <verse eid="LUK 20:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 20:3"/>And answering, He said to them, <char style="wj">“I also will ask you⁺ a word,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">one word</char></note><char style="wj"> and you⁺ tell Me: </char><verse eid="LUK 20:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 20:4"/><char style="wj">The baptism of John—was it from heaven or from men?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 20:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 20:5"/>And they reasoned with themselves, saying, “If we should say, ‘From heaven,’ He will say, ‘Why<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.5 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR and GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Why then</char></note> did you⁺ not believe him?’ <verse eid="LUK 20:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 20:6"/>But if we should say, ‘From men,’ all the people will stone us, for they are persuaded <char style="add">that</char> John is a prophet.” <verse eid="LUK 20:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 20:7"/>And they answered <char style="add">that</char> they did not know from where. <verse eid="LUK 20:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 20:8"/>And Jesus said to them, <char style="wj">“Neither do I tell you⁺ by what authority I do these things.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 20:8"/></para>

<para style="s">The Parable of the Wicked TenantsMatthew 21:33–46</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 20:9"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 12:1–12</char></note>And He began to speak to the people this parable: <char style="wj">“A certain man planted a vineyard, and he rented it out to farmers and traveled abroad a long time. </char><verse eid="LUK 20:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 20:10"/><char style="wj">And in the season, he sent a servant to the farmers, so that they will give to him from the fruit of the vineyard. But the farmers sent him away empty-handed, having beaten him. </char><verse eid="LUK 20:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 20:11"/>And he proceeded to send another servant, but having beaten and having dishonored that one also, they sent <char style="add">him</char> away empty-handed. <verse eid="LUK 20:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 20:12"/>And he proceeded to send a third, and having wounded this one also, they cast <char style="add">him</char> out. <verse eid="LUK 20:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 20:13"/>Then the lord of the vineyard said, ‘What shall I do? I will send my beloved son; perhaps they will respect him.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">when they see him</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note>’ <verse eid="LUK 20:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 20:14"/>But having seen him, the farmers were reasoning with one another, saying, ‘This is the heir; let us kill<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">come, let us kill</char></note> him, so that the inheritance might become ours.’ <verse eid="LUK 20:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 20:15"/>And having cast him out of the vineyard, they killed <char style="add">him</char>. What, therefore, will the lord of the vineyard do to them? <verse eid="LUK 20:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 20:16"/>He will come and will destroy these farmers, and will give the vineyard to others.” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 20:16">And having heard, they said, “Never may it be!” <verse eid="LUK 20:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 20:17"/>But having looked at them, He said, <char style="wj">“What then is this that has been written: </char></para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 20:17">‘The stone that those building rejected, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 20:17">this became the head of the corner’? <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 118:22</char></note><verse eid="LUK 20:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 20:18"/>Everyone having fallen on that stone will be broken; but on whomever it might fall, it will grind him into powder.” <verse eid="LUK 20:18"/></para>

<para style="s">Paying Taxes to CaesarMatthew 22:15–22</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 20:19"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 12:13–17</char></note>And the scribes and the chief priests<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the chief priests and the scribes</char></note> sought to lay hands on Him in that hour, and they feared the people.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.19 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the people</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> For they knew that He spoke this parable against them. <verse eid="LUK 20:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 20:20"/>And having closely watched, they sent spies feigning themselves to be righteous, so that they might catch <char style="add">Him</char> in His word, so as to deliver Him to the rule and to the authority of the governor. <verse eid="LUK 20:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 20:21"/>And they questioned Him, saying, “Teacher, we know that You speak and teach rightly and do not receive <char style="add">any</char> person, but teach the way of God on the basis of truth. <verse eid="LUK 20:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 20:22"/>Is it lawful for us to give tax to Caesar or not?” <verse eid="LUK 20:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 20:23"/>But having perceived their craftiness, He said to them,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Why do you test Me?</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 20:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 20:24"/><char style="wj">“Show Me a denarius.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.24 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> A denarius was customarily a day’s wage for a laborer; see Matthew 20:2.</char></note><char style="wj"> Whose image and inscription does it have?”</char> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 20:24">And they said, “Caesar’s.” <verse eid="LUK 20:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 20:25"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Therefore give back the things of Caesar to Caesar, and the things of God to God.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 20:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 20:26"/>And they were not able to catch <char style="add">Him</char> in His saying before the people. And having marveled at His answer, they were silent. <verse eid="LUK 20:26"/></para>

<para style="s">The Sadducees and the ResurrectionMatthew 22:23–33</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 20:27"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 12:18–27</char></note>And some of the Sadducees, the ones denying there is a resurrection, having come, questioned Him, <verse eid="LUK 20:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 20:28"/>saying, “Teacher, Moses wrote for us <char style="add">that</char> if anyone’s brother should die having a wife, and he should be childless, that his brother should take the wife and should raise up seed for his brother. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.28 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Deuteronomy 25:5</char></note><verse eid="LUK 20:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 20:29"/>Therefore there were seven brothers. And the first, having taken a wife, died childless; <verse eid="LUK 20:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 20:30"/>and the second<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.30 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">took her as wife, and he died childless</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 20:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 20:31"/>and the third took her; and likewise also the seven did not leave behind children and died. <verse eid="LUK 20:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 20:32"/>Finally the woman also died. <verse eid="LUK 20:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 20:33"/>Therefore, in the resurrection, the woman, of which of them does she become wife? For the seven had her <char style="add">as</char> wife.” <verse eid="LUK 20:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 20:34"/>And Jesus said to them, <char style="wj">“The sons of this age marry and are given in marriage. </char><verse eid="LUK 20:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 20:35"/><char style="wj">But those having been considered worthy to obtain that which is to the age and the resurrection that is out from the dead, they neither marry nor are given in marriage. </char><verse eid="LUK 20:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 20:36"/><char style="wj">For neither are they able to die anymore, for they are like angels, and they are sons of God, being sons of the resurrection. </char><verse eid="LUK 20:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 20:37"/>But that the dead are raised even Moses showed at the bush, as he calls the Lord ‘the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob.’ <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.37 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Exodus 3:6</char></note><verse eid="LUK 20:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 20:38"/>Now He is not the God of the dead but of the living, for all live to Him.” <verse eid="LUK 20:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 20:39"/>And answering, some of the scribes said, “Teacher, You spoke well!” <verse eid="LUK 20:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 20:40"/>For<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.40 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And</char></note> no longer did they dare to question Him anything. <verse eid="LUK 20:40"/></para>

<para style="s">Whose Son Is the Christ?Matthew 22:41–46</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 20:41"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 12:35–37</char></note>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“How do they say the Christ is Son of David? </char><verse eid="LUK 20:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 20:42"/><char style="wj">For David himself says in the book of Psalms: </char></para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 20:42">‘The Lord said to my Lord, </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 20:42">“Sit at My right hand <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.42 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And</char></note><verse eid="LUK 20:42"/></para>

<para style="q1"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 20:43"/>until I place Your enemies </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 20:43"><char style="add">as</char> a footstool for Your feet.”’<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.43 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 110:1</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 20:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 20:44"/>David therefore calls Him ‘Lord,’ and how is He his son?” <verse eid="LUK 20:44"/></para>

<para style="s">Beware of the ScribesMark 12:38–40</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 20:45"/>And all the people hearing, He said to His disciples,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.45 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the disciples</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 20:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 20:46"/><char style="wj">“Beware of the scribes, who are wanting to walk in long robes, and loving greetings in the marketplaces and first seats in the synagogues and first places at the suppers, </char><verse eid="LUK 20:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 20:47"/><char style="wj">who devour the houses of widows and pray at great length as a pretext. These will receive more abundant judgment.”</char> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">20.47 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">They defraud widows of their houses</char></note><verse eid="LUK 20:47"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 20"/>

<chapter number="21" style="c" sid="LUK 21"/>

<para style="s">The Poor Widow’s Offering</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 21:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 12:41–44</char></note>And having looked up, He saw the ones casting their gifts into the treasury—the rich. <verse eid="LUK 21:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 21:2"/>And He saw a certain poor widow casting there two lepta.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">two small copper coins</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; a lepton was a Jewish coin of bronze or copper worth about 1/128 of a denarius.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 21:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 21:3"/>“And He said, <char style="wj">“Truly I say to you⁺ that this poor widow cast in more than all. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 21:4"/><char style="wj">For these all cast in the gifts</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to God</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> out of that which is abounding to them, but she out of her lack cast in all the livelihood that she had.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 21:4"/></para>

<para style="s">Temple Destruction and Other SignsMatthew 24:1–8</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 21:5"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 13:1–8</char></note>And some speaking about the temple, that it was adorned with goodly stones and consecrated gifts, He said, <verse eid="LUK 21:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 21:6"/><char style="wj">“These things that you⁺ are beholding, the days will come in which not a stone will be left upon a stone that will not be thrown down.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 21:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 21:7"/>And they questioned Him, saying, “Teacher, when, then, will these things be, and what <char style="add">is</char> the sign when these things are about to happen?” <verse eid="LUK 21:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 21:8"/>And He said, <char style="wj">“Take heed, lest you⁺ be led astray; for many will come in My name, saying, ‘I am He,’ and, ‘The time has drawn near.’ Do not</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Therefore do not</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> go after them. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 21:9"/><char style="wj">And when you⁺ may hear of wars and disturbances, do not be terrified; for it is necessary for these things to happen first, but the end is not immediately.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 21:9"/></para>

<para style="s">Witnessing to All NationsMatthew 24:9–14</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 21:10"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 13:9–13</char></note>Then He was saying to them, <char style="wj">“Nation will rise up against nation, and kingdom against kingdom. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 21:11"/><char style="wj">There will be also great earthquakes and famines and pestilences in various places.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">great earthquakes in various places, and famines, and pestilences</char></note><char style="wj"> There will also be fearful sights and great signs from heaven. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 21:12"/>But before all these things, they will lay their hands upon you⁺ and will persecute <char style="add">you</char>, delivering <char style="add">you</char> up to the synagogues and prisons, leading <char style="add">you</char> away before kings and governors on account of My name. <verse eid="LUK 21:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 21:13"/>It will result to you⁺ for a testimony. <verse eid="LUK 21:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 21:14"/>Settle, therefore, in your⁺ hearts not to premeditate to make a defense. <verse eid="LUK 21:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 21:15"/>For I will give you⁺ a mouth and wisdom, which all those opposing you⁺ will neither be able to resist nor contradict.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">contradict nor resist</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 21:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 21:16"/>And you⁺ will be delivered up even by parents and brothers and relatives and friends,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">parents and relatives and friends and brothers</char></note> and they will put to death some of you⁺. <verse eid="LUK 21:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 21:17"/>And you⁺ will be hated by all because of My name. <verse eid="LUK 21:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 21:18"/>And no, not a hair of your⁺ head shall perish. <verse eid="LUK 21:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 21:19"/>By your⁺ perseverance you⁺ will gain your⁺ souls. <verse eid="LUK 21:19"/></para>

<para style="s">The Destruction of JerusalemMatthew 24:15–25</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 21:20"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 13:14–23</char></note>But when you⁺ shall see Jerusalem being encircled by encampments, then know that her desolation has drawn near. <verse eid="LUK 21:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 21:21"/>Then those in Judea, let them flee to the mountains; and those in her midst, let them depart; and those in the country, let them not enter into her. <verse eid="LUK 21:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 21:22"/>For these are the days of avenging, to fulfill all things having been written. <verse eid="LUK 21:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 21:23"/>Woe to those having in womb and to those nursing in those days! For<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">For at that time</char></note> there will be great distress upon the land and wrath to this people. <verse eid="LUK 21:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 21:24"/>And they will fall by the mouth of the sword and will be led captive into all the nations, and Jerusalem will be trodden down by the nations until the times of the nations are fulfilled. <verse eid="LUK 21:24"/></para>

<para style="s">The Return of the Son of ManMatthew 24:26–31</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 21:25"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 13:24–27</char></note>And there will be signs in sun and moon and stars, and upon the earth distress of nations with perplexity <char style="add">at</char> the roaring of the sea and the rolling surge— <verse eid="LUK 21:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 21:26"/>men fainting from fear and expectation of the <char style="add">things</char> coming on the world. For the powers of the heavens will be shaken. <verse eid="LUK 21:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 21:27"/>And then they will see the Son of Man coming in a cloud with power and great glory. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.27 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Daniel 7:13–14.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 21:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 21:28"/>And these things beginning to come to pass, look up and lift up your⁺ heads, because your⁺ redemption draws near.” <verse eid="LUK 21:28"/></para>

<para style="s">The Lesson of the Fig TreeMatthew 24:32–35</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 21:29"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 13:28–31</char></note>And He spoke to them a parable: <char style="wj">“Behold the fig tree and all the trees. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 21:30"/><char style="wj">When they already may cast forth, looking for yourselves you⁺ know that already the summer is near. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 21:31"/><char style="wj">So also you⁺, when you⁺ see these things coming to pass, know that the kingdom of God is near. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 21:32"/><char style="wj">Truly I say to you⁺ that this generation shall not pass away until all shall come to pass. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 21:33"/><char style="wj">The heaven and the earth will pass away, but My words will never pass away. </char><verse eid="LUK 21:33"/></para>

<para style="s">Be Watchful for the Day</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 21:34"/>And take heed to yourselves, lest your⁺ hearts ever be burdened with dissipation and drunkenness and the cares of life—and that day would come upon you⁺ suddenly as a snare. <verse eid="LUK 21:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 21:35"/>For it will come upon all those sitting upon the face of all the earth. <verse eid="LUK 21:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 21:36"/>But stay awake in every season, praying that you⁺ may have strength<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.36 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">you may be deemed worthy</char></note> to escape all these things that are about to come to pass, and to stand before the Son of Man.” <verse eid="LUK 21:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 21:37"/>And during the day He was teaching in the temple, and <char style="add">in</char> the evening, going out, He was lodging on the mount called Olivet.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.37 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> That is, </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Mount of Olives</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 21:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 21:38"/>And all the people were coming early to Him in the temple<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">21.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">on the mount</char></note> to hear Him. <verse eid="LUK 21:38"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 21"/>

<chapter number="22" style="c" sid="LUK 22"/>

<para style="s">The Plot to Kill Jesus</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 22:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 11:45–57</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:1–2</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 26:1–5</char></note>And the Feast of the Unleavened,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Feast of Unleavened Bread</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Exodus 12:14–20.</char></note> called the Passover, was drawing near. <verse eid="LUK 22:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 22:2"/>And the chief priests and the scribes were seeking how they might put Him to death, for they were afraid of the people. <verse eid="LUK 22:2"/></para>

<para style="s">Judas Agrees to Betray JesusMatthew 26:14–16</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 22:3"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:10–11</char></note>And Satan entered into Judas, the one being called Iscariot, being of the number of the Twelve. <verse eid="LUK 22:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 22:4"/>And having gone away, he discussed with the chief priests<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">chief priests, scribes,</char></note> and captains how he might betray Him to them. <verse eid="LUK 22:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 22:5"/>And they rejoiced and agreed to give him money. <verse eid="LUK 22:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 22:6"/>And he promised, and began seeking opportunity to betray Him to them apart from a crowd. <verse eid="LUK 22:6"/></para>

<para style="s">Preparing the PassoverMatthew 26:17–19</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 22:7"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:12–16</char></note>And the day of the Unleavened came, on which it was necessary for the Passover to be sacrificed. <verse eid="LUK 22:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 22:8"/>And He sent Peter and John, having said, <char style="wj">“Having gone, prepare the Passover for us, that we may eat.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 22:9"/>And they said to Him, “Where do You want <char style="add">that</char> we should prepare?” <verse eid="LUK 22:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 22:10"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Behold, of you⁺ having entered into the city, a man carrying a pitcher of water will meet you⁺. Follow him into the house into which he enters, </char><verse eid="LUK 22:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 22:11"/><char style="wj">and you⁺ will say to the master of the house, ‘The Teacher says to you, “Where is the guest room, where I may eat the Passover with My disciples?”’ </char><verse eid="LUK 22:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 22:12"/><char style="wj">And he will show you⁺ a large furnished upper room. Make preparations there.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:12"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 22:13"/>And having gone, they found <char style="add">it</char> as He had said to them, and they prepared the Passover. <verse eid="LUK 22:13"/></para>

<para style="s">The Last SupperMatthew 26:20–30</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 22:14"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">1 Corinthians 11:17–34</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:17–26</char></note>And when the hour was come, He reclined, and the apostles<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.14 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the twelve apostles</char></note> with Him. <verse eid="LUK 22:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 22:15"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“With desire I desired to eat this Passover with you⁺ before I suffer. </char><verse eid="LUK 22:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 22:16"/><char style="wj">For I say to you⁺ that no longer will I eat thereof until it is fulfilled in the kingdom of God.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 22:17"/>And having received a cup,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the cup</char></note> having given thanks, He said, <char style="wj">“Take this and divide it among yourselves. </char><verse eid="LUK 22:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 22:18"/><char style="wj">For I say to you⁺ that I will not drink of the fruit of the vine from now until</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.18 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of the vine until</char></note><char style="wj"> the kingdom of God shall come.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 22:19"/>And having taken the bread, having given thanks, He broke <char style="add">it</char> and gave to them, saying, <char style="wj">“This is My body, which is given for you⁺; do this in remembrance of Me,”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 22:20"/>and the cup likewise, after the supping, saying, <char style="wj">“This cup is the new covenant in My blood, which is being poured out for you⁺.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.20 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Some manuscripts end verse 19 after </char><char style="fq" closed="false">This is My body</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> and do not include verse 20.</char></note><char style="wj"> </char><verse eid="LUK 22:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 22:21"/>But, behold, the hand of him betraying Me <char style="add">is</char> with Me on the table. <verse eid="LUK 22:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 22:22"/>For indeed, the Son of Man goes according to that having been determined. But woe to that man by whom He is betrayed.” <verse eid="LUK 22:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 22:23"/>And they began to question among themselves who then it might be of them who is about to do this. <verse eid="LUK 22:23"/></para>

<para style="s">Who Is the Greatest?</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 22:24"/>And there was also a dispute among them <char style="add">as to</char> which of them is thought to be the greatest. <verse eid="LUK 22:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 22:25"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“The kings of the Gentiles rule over them, and those exercising authority over them are called benefactors. </char><verse eid="LUK 22:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 22:26"/><char style="wj">But you⁺ shall not be thus. Rather, the greatest among you⁺, let him be as the younger, and the one leading as the one serving. </char><verse eid="LUK 22:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 22:27"/><char style="wj">For who is greater, the one reclining or the one serving? Is it not the one reclining? But I am in your⁺ midst as the One serving. </char><verse eid="LUK 22:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 22:28"/>Now you⁺ are those having remained with Me in My trials. <verse eid="LUK 22:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 22:29"/>And I appoint to you⁺ a kingdom, as My Father appointed to Me, <verse eid="LUK 22:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 22:30"/>so that you⁺ may eat and may drink at My table in My kingdom,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.30 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">in My kingdom</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> and may sit on thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel. <verse eid="LUK 22:30"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Predicts Peter’s DenialMatthew 26:31–35</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 22:31"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 13:36–38</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:27–31</char></note>Simon, Simon,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.31 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And the Lord said, “Simon, Simon,</char></note> behold, Satan asked for all of you⁺ to sift like wheat. <verse eid="LUK 22:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 22:32"/>But I begged for you, that your faith may not fail. And you, when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.” <verse eid="LUK 22:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 22:33"/>And he said to Him, “Lord, I am ready to go with You both to prison and to death.” <verse eid="LUK 22:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 22:34"/>But He said, <char style="wj">“I tell you, Peter, the rooster will not</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">will certainly not</char></note><char style="wj"> crow today until</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">before</char></note><char style="wj"> you shall deny three times knowing Me.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 22:35"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“When I sent you⁺ without purse and bag and sandals, did you⁺ lack anything?”</char> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 22:35">And they said, “Nothing.” <verse eid="LUK 22:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 22:36"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“But now, the one having a purse, let him take it, and likewise a bag; and the one having no sword, let him sell his cloak and buy one. </char><verse eid="LUK 22:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 22:37"/><char style="wj">For I say to you⁺ that this which has been written, it is necessary to be accomplished in Me: ‘And He was reckoned with the lawless.’</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.37 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">yet</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> For also the things concerning Me have an end.”</char> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.37 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Isaiah 53:12</char></note><verse eid="LUK 22:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 22:38"/>And they said, “Lord, behold, here <char style="add">are</char> two swords.” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 22:38">And He said to them, <char style="wj">“It is enough.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:38"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Prays on the Mount of OlivesMatthew 26:36–46</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 22:39"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:32–42</char></note>And having gone out, He went according to the custom to the Mount of Olives, and the disciples<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.39 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">His disciples</char></note> also followed Him. <verse eid="LUK 22:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 22:40"/>And having come to the place, He said to them, <char style="wj">“Pray not to enter into temptation.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 22:41"/>And He withdrew from them about a stone’s throw, and having fallen on the knees, He was praying, <verse eid="LUK 22:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 22:42"/>saying, <char style="wj">“Father, if You are willing, take away this cup from Me. Yet not My will, but Yours, be done.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 22:43"/>And an angel from heaven appeared to Him, strengthening Him. <verse eid="LUK 22:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 22:44"/>And having been in agony, He was praying more earnestly. And His sweat became like large drops of blood falling down upon the ground.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.44 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Some manuscripts do not include verses 43 and 44.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 22:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 22:45"/>And having risen up from the prayer, having come to the disciples,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.45 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Scrivener TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">His disciples</char></note> He found them sleeping from the grief. <verse eid="LUK 22:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 22:46"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Why are you⁺ sleeping? Having risen up, pray that you⁺ might not enter into temptation.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:46"/></para>

<para style="s">The Betrayal of JesusMatthew 26:47–56</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 22:47"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 18:1–14</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:43–52</char></note><char style="add">In</char> His still speaking, behold, a crowd, and he who is called Judas, one of the Twelve, was going before them, and he drew near to Jesus to kiss Him.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.47 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> F35 and GOC include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">for he had given them this sign: “Whomever I kiss, He is the One.”</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 22:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 22:48"/>And Jesus said to him, <char style="wj">“Judas, are you betraying the Son of Man with a kiss?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 22:49"/>And those around Him, having seen what would be, said,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.49 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">to Him</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> “Lord, will we strike with the sword?” <verse eid="LUK 22:49"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 22:50"/>And a certain one of them struck the servant of the high priest and cut off his right ear. <verse eid="LUK 22:50"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 22:51"/>But Jesus, answering, said, <char style="wj">“Permit only as far as”</char> this! And having touched the ear, He healed him. <verse eid="LUK 22:51"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 22:52"/>And Jesus said to those having come out against Him, the chief priests and captains of the temple and elders, <char style="wj">“Have you⁺ come out with swords and clubs as against a robber? </char><verse eid="LUK 22:52"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 22:53"/><char style="wj">Every day of My being with you⁺ in the temple,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.53 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the temple courts</char></note><char style="wj"> you⁺ did not stretch out the hands against Me; but this is your⁺ hour, and that of the power of the darkness.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:53"/></para>

<para style="s">Peter Denies JesusMatthew 26:69–75</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 22:54"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 18:15–18</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:66–72</char></note>And having seized Him, they led <char style="add">Him</char> away and led <char style="add">Him</char> into the house of the high priest. And Peter was following afar off. <verse eid="LUK 22:54"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 22:55"/>And <char style="add">on</char> their having kindled a fire in the midst of the courtyard and having sat down together, Peter was sitting among them. <verse eid="LUK 22:55"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 22:56"/>And a certain servant girl, having seen him sitting by the light and having looked intently on him, said, “This one also was with Him.” <verse eid="LUK 22:56"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="57" style="v" sid="LUK 22:57"/>But he denied <char style="add">it</char>,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.57 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">he denied Him</char></note> saying, “Woman, I do not know Him.” <verse eid="LUK 22:57"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="58" style="v" sid="LUK 22:58"/>And after a little, having seen him, another was saying, “You also are of them.” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 22:58">But Peter was saying, “Man, I am not.” <verse eid="LUK 22:58"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="59" style="v" sid="LUK 22:59"/>And about one hour having elapsed, a certain other strongly affirmed <char style="add">it</char>, saying, “Of a truth, this one also was with Him; for he is also a Galilean.” <verse eid="LUK 22:59"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="60" style="v" sid="LUK 22:60"/>But Peter said, “Man, I do not know what you say.” And immediately <char style="add">in</char> his still speaking, a rooster crowed. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.60 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the rooster</char></note><verse eid="LUK 22:60"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="61" style="v" sid="LUK 22:61"/>And having turned, the Lord looked at Peter. And Peter remembered the word of the Lord, how He said to him, <char style="wj">“Before the rooster crows today,</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.61 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">today</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> you will deny Me three times.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:61"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="62" style="v" sid="LUK 22:62"/>And having gone forth outside, he wept<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.62 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Peter wept</char></note> bitterly. <verse eid="LUK 22:62"/></para>

<para style="s">The Soldiers Mock JesusIsaiah 50:4–11</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="63" style="v" sid="LUK 22:63"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 19:1–15</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 15:16–20</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 27:27–31</char></note>And the men who are holding Him began mocking Him, beating <char style="add">Him</char>. <verse eid="LUK 22:63"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="64" style="v" sid="LUK 22:64"/>And having blindfolded Him,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.64 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">they were striking Him on the face, and</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> they were questioning <char style="add">Him</char>, saying, “Prophesy, who is the one having struck You?” <verse eid="LUK 22:64"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="65" style="v" sid="LUK 22:65"/>And they were saying many other things to Him, blaspheming. <verse eid="LUK 22:65"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus before the SanhedrinMatthew 26:57–68</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="66" style="v" sid="LUK 22:66"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 18:19–24</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 14:53–65</char></note>And when it was day, the elderhood of the people were gathered together, both chief priests and scribes, and they led Him into their Council,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.66 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">their Sanhedrin</char></note> saying, <verse eid="LUK 22:66"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="67" style="v" sid="LUK 22:67"/>“If You are the Christ, tell us.” But He said to them, <char style="wj">“If I should tell you⁺, you⁺ would not believe, </char><verse eid="LUK 22:67"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="68" style="v" sid="LUK 22:68"/><char style="wj">and if I should ask you, you⁺ would not answer.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.68 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Me nor release Me</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note><char style="wj"> </char><verse eid="LUK 22:68"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="69" style="v" sid="LUK 22:69"/><char style="wj">But from now on the Son of Man will be sitting at the right hand of the power of God.”</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">22.69 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Psalm 110:1.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 22:69"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="70" style="v" sid="LUK 22:70"/>And they all said, “Are You then the Son of God?” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 22:70">And He was saying to them, <char style="wj">“You⁺ say that I am.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 22:70"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="71" style="v" sid="LUK 22:71"/>And they said, “What need do we have of any more testimony? For we ourselves heard <char style="add">it</char> from His mouth.” <verse eid="LUK 22:71"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 22"/>

<chapter number="23" style="c" sid="LUK 23"/>

<para style="s">Jesus before Pilate</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 23:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 18:28–40</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 27:11–14</char></note>And having risen up, all the multitude of them led Him to Pilate. <verse eid="LUK 23:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 23:2"/>And they began to accuse Him, saying, “We found this <char style="add">man</char> misleading our nation<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.2 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the nation</char></note> and forbidding taxes to be given to Caesar, and declaring Himself to be Christ, a King.” <verse eid="LUK 23:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 23:3"/>And Pilate questioned Him, saying, “Are You the King of the Jews?” </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 23:3">And answering him, He was saying, <char style="wj">“You say.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 23:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 23:4"/>And Pilate said to the chief priests and the crowds, “I find no guilt in this man.” <verse eid="LUK 23:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 23:5"/>But they kept insisting, saying, “He stirs up the people, teaching throughout all of Judea, and He has begun from Galilee even to here.” <verse eid="LUK 23:5"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus before Herod</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 23:6"/>And Pilate, having heard,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.6 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of Galilee</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> asked whether the man is a Galilean. <verse eid="LUK 23:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 23:7"/>And having learned that He is from the jurisdiction of Herod, he sent Him up to Herod, he himself being also in Jerusalem in those days. <verse eid="LUK 23:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 23:8"/>And Herod, having seen Jesus, was exceedingly glad; for of a long time he is wanting to see Him, because of hearing<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.8 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">many things</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> concerning Him; and he was hoping to see some sign done by Him. <verse eid="LUK 23:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 23:9"/>And he kept questioning Him in many words, but He answered him nothing. <verse eid="LUK 23:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 23:10"/>And the chief priests and the scribes<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.10 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the scribes and the chief priests</char></note> had been standing by, vehemently accusing Him. <verse eid="LUK 23:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 23:11"/>And Herod, with his troops, having treated Him with contempt and also having mocked <char style="add">Him</char>, having put on <char style="add">Him</char> splendid apparel, sent Him back to Pilate. <verse eid="LUK 23:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 23:12"/>And both Herod and Pilate<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Pilate and Herod</char></note> became friends with one another on that day; for previously they were at enmity between themselves. <verse eid="LUK 23:12"/></para>

<para style="s">The Crowd Chooses BarabbasMatthew 27:15–23</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 23:13"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 15:6–11</char></note>And Pilate, having called together the chief priests and the rulers and the people, <verse eid="LUK 23:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 23:14"/>said to them, “You⁺ brought to me this man as one misleading the people; and behold, having examined <char style="add">Him</char> before you⁺, I found nothing in this man guilty of that accusation you⁺ are bringing against Him— <verse eid="LUK 23:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 23:15"/>no, nor even Herod, for he sent Him back to us.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.15 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">for I sent you back to him</char></note> And behold, nothing worthy of death is done by Him. <verse eid="LUK 23:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 23:16"/>Therefore, having chastised Him, I will release <char style="add">Him</char>.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.16 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">17</char><char style="fq" closed="false">Now of necessity Pilate had to release to them one at the feast</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Matthew 27:15 and Mark 15:6.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 23:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 23:18"/>But they cried out all together, saying, “Away with this <char style="add">man</char>, and release to us Barabbas,” <verse eid="LUK 23:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 23:19"/>who was one having been cast into the prison on account of a certain insurrection having been made in the city, and murder. <verse eid="LUK 23:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 23:20"/>Therefore Pilate, wanting to release Jesus, called to them again. <verse eid="LUK 23:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 23:21"/>But they were crying out, saying, “Crucify! Crucify Him!” <verse eid="LUK 23:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 23:22"/>And he said to them a third <char style="add">time</char>, “What evil indeed did this <char style="add">man</char> commit? I found no cause of death in Him. Therefore having chastised Him, I will release <char style="add">Him</char>.” <verse eid="LUK 23:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 23:23"/>But they were urgent, asking with loud voices for Him to be crucified. And their voices<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.23 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and those of the chief priests</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> were prevailing. <verse eid="LUK 23:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 23:24"/>And Pilate sentenced their demand to be done. <verse eid="LUK 23:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 23:25"/>And he released<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.25 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">released for them</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; GOC </char><char style="fq" closed="false">released for them Barabbas</char></note> the one having been cast into prison on account of insurrection and murder, whom they had asked for; and Jesus he delivered up to their will. <verse eid="LUK 23:25"/></para>

<para style="s">The CrucifixionPsalm 22:1–31</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 23:26"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 19:16–27</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 15:21–32</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 27:32–44</char></note>And as they led Him away, having laid hold on Simon, a certain <char style="add">man</char> of Cyrene coming from the country, they put upon him the cross, to carry <char style="add">it</char> behind Jesus. <verse eid="LUK 23:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 23:27"/>And a great multitude of the people were following Him, and of women who were mourning and lamenting for Him. <verse eid="LUK 23:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 23:28"/>But having turned to them, Jesus said, <char style="wj">“Daughters of Jerusalem, do not weep for Me, but weep for yourselves and for your⁺ children. </char><verse eid="LUK 23:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 23:29"/><char style="wj">For behold, the days are coming in which they will say, ‘Blessed are the barren, and the wombs that never did bear, and the breasts that never nursed!’ </char><verse eid="LUK 23:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 23:30"/><char style="wj">Then </char></para>

<para style="q1" vid="LUK 23:30">‘they will begin to say to the mountains, “Fall upon us,” </para>

<para style="q2" vid="LUK 23:30">and to the hills, “Cover us.”’ <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.30 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Hosea 10:8</char></note><verse eid="LUK 23:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 23:31"/>For if they do these things in the green tree, what might happen in the dry?” <verse eid="LUK 23:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 23:32"/>And two other criminals also were being led away to be put to death with Him. <verse eid="LUK 23:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 23:33"/>And when they came to the place called The Skull,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.33 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Greek </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Kranion</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; Vulgate </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Calvariae</char><char style="ft" closed="false">, rendered in some translations as </char><char style="fq" closed="false">Calvary</char></note> there they crucified Him, and the criminals, one on the right hand and one on the left hand. <verse eid="LUK 23:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 23:34"/>And Jesus was saying, <char style="wj">“Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do.”</char> And they cast lots, dividing His garments.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Some manuscripts do not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And Jesus was saying, “Father... what they do.”</char></note> <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.34 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See Psalm 22:18.</char></note><verse eid="LUK 23:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 23:35"/>And the people stood, looking on. And the rulers also were deriding <char style="add">Him</char>,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">with them</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see Psalm 22:7.</char></note> saying, “He saved others; let Him save Himself if this is the Christ of God,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.35 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">the Christ, the Chosen One</char></note> the Chosen One.” <verse eid="LUK 23:35"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 23:36"/>And the soldiers also mocked Him, coming near, offering Him sour wine<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.36 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">offering Him wine vinegar</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 23:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 23:37"/>and saying, “If You are the King of the Jews, save Yourself!” <verse eid="LUK 23:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 23:38"/>And there was also an inscription over Him:<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.38 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">written in Greek and Latin and Hebrew</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; see John 19:20.</char></note> </para>

<para style="p" vid="LUK 23:38">THIS <char style="add">IS</char> THE KING OF THE JEWS.<verse eid="LUK 23:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 23:39"/>And one of the criminals having been hanged was railing at Him, saying, “Are You not the Christ? Save Yourself<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.39 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">If You are the Christ, save Yourself</char></note> and us!” <verse eid="LUK 23:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 23:40"/>But the other, answering, was rebuking him, saying, “Do you not even fear God, since you are under the same judgment, <verse eid="LUK 23:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 23:41"/>and we indeed justly? For we are receiving back things worthy of what we did, but <char style="add">this</char> man did nothing wrong.” <verse eid="LUK 23:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 23:42"/>And he was saying, “Jesus, remember me<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.42 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">was saying to Jesus, “Remember me, Lord,</char></note> when You come into Your kingdom!” <verse eid="LUK 23:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 23:43"/>And He said to him, <char style="wj">“Truly I say to you, today you will be with Me in Paradise.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.43 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> See 2 Corinthians 12:4 and Revelation 2:7.</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <verse eid="LUK 23:43"/></para>

<para style="s">The Death of JesusPsalm 31:1–24</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 23:44"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 19:28–30</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 15:33–41</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 27:45–56</char></note>And now it was about the sixth hour,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.44 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> That is, noon</char></note> and darkness came over all the land until the ninth hour,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.44 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> That is, three in the afternoon</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 23:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 23:45"/>the sun having darkened,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.45 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">having failed</char><char style="ft" closed="false"> or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">having been obscured</char><char style="ft" closed="false">; BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">having become dark</char></note> and the veil of the temple was torn in the middle. <verse eid="LUK 23:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 23:46"/>And having called out in a loud voice, Jesus said, <char style="wj">“Father, into Your hands I commit My Spirit.”</char> And having said this, He breathed His last. <note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.46 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Psalm 31:5</char></note><verse eid="LUK 23:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 23:47"/>And having seen that having taken place, the centurion was glorifying God, saying, “Certainly this man was righteous.”<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.47 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">innocent</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 23:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 23:48"/>And all the crowds having come together to this spectacle, having seen the things having taken place, were turning back, beating <char style="add">their</char> breasts. <verse eid="LUK 23:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 23:49"/>But all from those who knew Him, and the women, those having followed Him from Galilee, stood afar off, beholding these things. <verse eid="LUK 23:49"/></para>

<para style="s">The Burial of JesusIsaiah 53:9–12</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 23:50"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 19:38–42</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 15:42–47</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 27:57–61</char></note>And behold, a man named Joseph, being also a Council member, a good and righteous man— <verse eid="LUK 23:50"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 23:51"/>he was not having consented to their counsel and deed—from Arimathea, a city of the Jews, who<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.51 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">also himself</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> was waiting for the kingdom of God, <verse eid="LUK 23:51"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 23:52"/>he, having gone to Pilate, asked <char style="add">for</char> the body of Jesus. <verse eid="LUK 23:52"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 23:53"/>And having taken it down, he wrapped it in a linen cloth and placed it in a tomb cut in a rock, in which no one yet was laid. <verse eid="LUK 23:53"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="54" style="v" sid="LUK 23:54"/>And it was the Day of Preparation, and Sabbath was beginning.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">23.54 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">was about to begin</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 23:54"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="55" style="v" sid="LUK 23:55"/>And the women who had come with Him out of Galilee, having followed, saw the tomb and how His body was laid. <verse eid="LUK 23:55"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="56" style="v" sid="LUK 23:56"/>And having returned, they prepared spices and anointing oils. And they rested indeed on the Sabbath according to the commandment. <verse eid="LUK 23:56"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 23"/>

<chapter number="24" style="c" sid="LUK 24"/>

<para style="s">The Resurrection</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="1" style="v" sid="LUK 24:1"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">John 20:1–9</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Mark 16:1–8</char></note><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Matthew 28:1–10</char></note>But <char style="add">on</char> the first of the Sabbaths<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">On the first day of the week,</char></note> at early dawn they came<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.1 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">they and certain other women with them came</char></note> to the tomb bringing the spices that they prepared. <verse eid="LUK 24:1"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="2" style="v" sid="LUK 24:2"/>And they found the stone having been rolled away from the tomb, <verse eid="LUK 24:2"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="3" style="v" sid="LUK 24:3"/>but having entered, they did not find the body of the Lord Jesus.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.3 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of the Lord Jesus</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 24:3"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="4" style="v" sid="LUK 24:4"/>And it came to pass that while they are perplexed<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.4 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">are puzzling</char></note> about this, behold, two men in dazzling apparel stood by them. <verse eid="LUK 24:4"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="5" style="v" sid="LUK 24:5"/>And <char style="add">on</char> their having become terrified and bowing the faces to the ground, they said to them, “Why do you⁺ seek the living with the dead? <verse eid="LUK 24:5"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="6" style="v" sid="LUK 24:6"/>He is not here, but He is risen! Remember how He spoke to you⁺, being yet in Galilee, <verse eid="LUK 24:6"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="7" style="v" sid="LUK 24:7"/>saying, <char style="wj">‘It is necessary for the Son of Man to be delivered into the hands of sinful men, and to be crucified, and the third day to rise again.’</char>” <verse eid="LUK 24:7"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="8" style="v" sid="LUK 24:8"/>And they remembered His words. <verse eid="LUK 24:8"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="9" style="v" sid="LUK 24:9"/>And having returned from the tomb, they related all these things to the Eleven and to all the rest. <verse eid="LUK 24:9"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="10" style="v" sid="LUK 24:10"/>Now it was Mary Magdalene, and Joanna, and Mary the <char style="add">mother</char> of James, and the other women with them, <char style="add">who</char> were saying these things to the apostles. <verse eid="LUK 24:10"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="11" style="v" sid="LUK 24:11"/>And these<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.11 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">their</char></note> words appeared before them like folly, and they did not believe them. <verse eid="LUK 24:11"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="12" style="v" sid="LUK 24:12"/>But Peter, having risen up, ran to the tomb, and having stooped down, he sees only the linen strips.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.12 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">lying there</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> And he went away, wondering in himself at that having come to pass. <verse eid="LUK 24:12"/></para>

<para style="s">The Road to EmmausMark 16:12–13</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="13" style="v" sid="LUK 24:13"/>And behold, on the same day, two of them were going to a village whose name <char style="add">is</char> Emmaus, sixty stadia distant from Jerusalem.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.13 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> Or </char><char style="fq" closed="false">about seven miles from Jerusalem</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 24:13"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="14" style="v" sid="LUK 24:14"/>And they were talking with one another about all these things having taken place. <verse eid="LUK 24:14"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="15" style="v" sid="LUK 24:15"/>And it came to pass, in their talking and reasoning, that Jesus Himself, having drawn near, was walking along with them. <verse eid="LUK 24:15"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="16" style="v" sid="LUK 24:16"/>But their eyes were held not to know Him. <verse eid="LUK 24:16"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="17" style="v" sid="LUK 24:17"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“What are these words that you⁺ exchange with one another, walking?”</char> And they stood still, looking sad.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.17 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">with one another as you walk along with sadness on your faces</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 24:17"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="18" style="v" sid="LUK 24:18"/>And the one named Cleopas, answering, said to Him, “Are You alone visiting Jerusalem and have not known the things having come to pass in it in these days?” <verse eid="LUK 24:18"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="19" style="v" sid="LUK 24:19"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“What things?”</char> And they said to Him, “The things concerning Jesus of Nazareth, a man who was a prophet, mighty in deed and word before God and all the people, <verse eid="LUK 24:19"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="20" style="v" sid="LUK 24:20"/>and how our chief priests and rulers delivered Him up to the judgment of death, and they crucified Him. <verse eid="LUK 24:20"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="21" style="v" sid="LUK 24:21"/>But we were hoping it is He who is about to redeem Israel. But indeed also with all these things, this brings<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.21 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">today brings</char></note> the third day away from which these things came to pass. <verse eid="LUK 24:21"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="22" style="v" sid="LUK 24:22"/>But also, certain women out from us astonished us. Having been to the tomb early this morning<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.22 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">early</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 24:22"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="23" style="v" sid="LUK 24:23"/>and not having found His body, they came declaring to have also seen a vision of angels, who say He is alive. <verse eid="LUK 24:23"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="24" style="v" sid="LUK 24:24"/>And some of those with us went to the tomb and found <char style="add">it</char> thus, even as the women said. But Him they did not see.” <verse eid="LUK 24:24"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="25" style="v" sid="LUK 24:25"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“O foolish and slow of heart to believe in all that the prophets spoke! </char><verse eid="LUK 24:25"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="26" style="v" sid="LUK 24:26"/><char style="wj">Was it not necessary for the Christ to suffer these things and to enter into His glory?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 24:26"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="27" style="v" sid="LUK 24:27"/>And having begun from Moses and from all the Prophets, He interpreted to them the things concerning Himself in all the Scriptures. <verse eid="LUK 24:27"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="28" style="v" sid="LUK 24:28"/>And they drew near to the village where they were going, and He appeared to be going farther. <verse eid="LUK 24:28"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="29" style="v" sid="LUK 24:29"/>And they constrained Him, saying, “Abide with us, for it is toward evening, and now the day has declined.” And He entered in to abide with them. <verse eid="LUK 24:29"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="30" style="v" sid="LUK 24:30"/>And it came to pass, in His reclining with them, having taken the bread, <char style="add">that</char> He blessed <char style="add">it</char>; and having broken <char style="add">it</char>, He began giving <char style="add">it</char> to them. <verse eid="LUK 24:30"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="31" style="v" sid="LUK 24:31"/>And their eyes were opened, and they knew Him. And He, being seen, vanished from them. <verse eid="LUK 24:31"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="32" style="v" sid="LUK 24:32"/>And they said to one another, “Was not our heart burning within us<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.32 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> WH does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">within us</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> as He was speaking with us on the road, as He was opening the Scriptures to us?” <verse eid="LUK 24:32"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="33" style="v" sid="LUK 24:33"/>And having risen up <char style="add">that</char> same hour, they returned to Jerusalem. And they found the Eleven and those with them gathered together, <verse eid="LUK 24:33"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="34" style="v" sid="LUK 24:34"/>saying, “The Lord is risen indeed, and He has appeared to Simon!” <verse eid="LUK 24:34"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="35" style="v" sid="LUK 24:35"/>And they began relating the things on the road, and how He was known to them in the breaking of the bread. <verse eid="LUK 24:35"/></para>

<para style="s">Jesus Appears to the DisciplesJohn 20:19–23</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="36" style="v" sid="LUK 24:36"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">1 John 1:1–4</char></note>And <char style="add">in</char> their telling these things, He Himself stood in their midst and says to them, <char style="wj">“Peace to you⁺.</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.36 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> SBL does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and says, “Peace be with you.”</char></note><char style="wj">”</char> <verse eid="LUK 24:36"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="37" style="v" sid="LUK 24:37"/>But having been terrified, and having been filled with fear, they were thinking themselves to see a spirit. <verse eid="LUK 24:37"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="38" style="v" sid="LUK 24:38"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Why are you⁺ troubled, and why do doubts come up in your⁺ hearts? </char><verse eid="LUK 24:38"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="39" style="v" sid="LUK 24:39"/><char style="wj">See My hands and My feet, that I am He. Touch Me and see, for a spirit does not have flesh and bones, as you⁺ see Me having.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 24:39"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="40" style="v" sid="LUK 24:40"/>And having said this, He showed to them the hands and the feet. <verse eid="LUK 24:40"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="41" style="v" sid="LUK 24:41"/>And <char style="add">in</char> their still disbelieving from joy and wondering, He said to them, <char style="wj">“Have you⁺ anything here to eat?”</char> <verse eid="LUK 24:41"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="42" style="v" sid="LUK 24:42"/>And they gave to Him part of a broiled fish.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.42 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">and some honeycomb</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 24:42"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="43" style="v" sid="LUK 24:43"/>And having taken, He ate before them. <verse eid="LUK 24:43"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="44" style="v" sid="LUK 24:44"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“These are My words, which I spoke to you⁺ being still with you⁺, that it is necessary to be fulfilled all things having been written concerning Me in the Law of Moses and the Prophets and the Psalms.”</char> <verse eid="LUK 24:44"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="45" style="v" sid="LUK 24:45"/>Then He opened their mind to understand the Scriptures. <verse eid="LUK 24:45"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="46" style="v" sid="LUK 24:46"/>And He said to them, <char style="wj">“Thus it has been written: The Christ</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.46 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And thus it is necessary that the Christ</char></note><char style="wj"> was to suffer and to rise out from the dead on the third day, </char><verse eid="LUK 24:46"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="47" style="v" sid="LUK 24:47"/><char style="wj">and repentance and</char><note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.47 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> NA, NE, and WH </char><char style="fq" closed="false">repentance for</char></note><char style="wj"> forgiveness of sins are to be proclaimed in His name to all nations, having begun from Jerusalem. </char><verse eid="LUK 24:47"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="48" style="v" sid="LUK 24:48"/><char style="wj">You⁺ are witnesses of these things. </char><verse eid="LUK 24:48"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="49" style="v" sid="LUK 24:49"/>And behold,<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.49 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> TH does not include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">And behold</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> I am sending the promise of My Father upon you⁺. But you⁺, remain in the city<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.49 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR include </char><char style="fq" closed="false">of Jerusalem</char><char style="ft" closed="false">.</char></note> until you⁺ should be clothed with power from on high.” <verse eid="LUK 24:49"/></para>

<para style="s">The AscensionMark 16:19–20</para>

<para style="p"><verse number="50" style="v" sid="LUK 24:50"/><note caller="-" style="x"><char style="xt" closed="false">Acts 1:6–11</char></note>And He led them out as far as to Bethany, and having lifted up His hands, He blessed them. <verse eid="LUK 24:50"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="51" style="v" sid="LUK 24:51"/>And it came to pass, in His blessing them, <char style="add">that</char> He was separated from them and was carried up into heaven. <verse eid="LUK 24:51"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="52" style="v" sid="LUK 24:52"/>And they, having worshiped Him, returned to Jerusalem with great joy, <verse eid="LUK 24:52"/></para>

<para style="p"><verse number="53" style="v" sid="LUK 24:53"/>and they were through all <char style="add">time</char> in the temple, blessing God.<note caller="+" style="f"><char style="fr" closed="false">24.53 </char><char style="ft" closed="false"> BYZ and TR </char><char style="fq" closed="false">praising and blessing God</char></note> <verse eid="LUK 24:53"/></para>

<chapter eid="LUK 24"/>

</usx>
